  user_input {
    user_response: "First read the entire prompt and dont hallucinate and then start thinking. You are the principal software architect, senior full-stack engineer, ML engineer, data engineer, and QA engineer responsible for BUILDING the NutriHarvest AI hackathon prototype.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nThis is a real hackathon prototype that will be demonstrated through a recorded demo video and submitted as a repository.\n\nYour job is to BUILD THE ACTUAL WORKING PROJECT in the current workspace.\n\nDO NOT merely describe the implementation.\nDO NOT give me a tutorial.\nDO NOT generate a fake prototype consisting only of static screens.\nDO NOT create a fake database with invented agricultural facts.\nDO NOT hallucinate APIs, datasets, model accuracy, market prices, crop yields, nutrition values, or government data.\n\nYou must inspect the workspace, create the application, run it, test it, fix errors, and leave a working prototype in the workspace.\n\nDO NOT commit to GitHub.\nDO NOT push/upload to GitHub.\nDO NOT create commits.\nDO NOT publish/deploy anything unless explicitly asked later.\n\n==================================================\n1. SOURCE OF TRUTH\n==================================================\n\nThe attached NutriHarvest AI presentation and project specification are the primary product requirements.\n\nProject:\n\nNutriHarvest AI\nPersonalized Crop-to-Plate Planner\n\nCore objective:\n\nOptimize:\n1. Household nutrition\n2. Crop planning\n3. Farm productivity\n4. Climate resilience\n5. Harvest allocation\n6. Market selling decisions\n7. Farmer income\n\nCore philosophy:\n\n\"Grow what your family needs.\nSell what your family doesn\'t need.\nEarn smarter.\"\n\nThe system is designed for smallholder farming families.\n\nThe core product is NOT merely a crop recommendation app.\n\nIt is a CLOSED-LOOP DECISION SUPPORT SYSTEM connecting:\n\nHousehold nutrition\n+\nCrop planning\n+\nYield prediction\n+\nClimate intelligence\n+\nHarvest allocation\n+\nMarket intelligence\n+\nIncome optimization\n\n==================================================\n2. ABSOLUTE ANTI-HALLUCINATION RULES\n==================================================\n\nThese rules have higher priority than convenience.\n\nRULE 1:\nNever invent a dataset.\n\nRULE 2:\nNever fabricate agricultural records and present them as real.\n\nRULE 3:\nNever fabricate mandi prices.\n\nRULE 4:\nNever fabricate weather values.\n\nRULE 5:\nNever fabricate soil measurements.\n\nRULE 6:\nNever fabricate nutrition values.\n\nRULE 7:\nNever claim a model was trained if it was not actually trained.\n\nRULE 8:\nNever claim model accuracy unless the model has actually been evaluated on a held-out test set.\n\nRULE 9:\nNever claim an API is live unless the application successfully calls it.\n\nRULE 10:\nNever claim a recommendation is based on real-time data if the application is using cached data.\n\nRULE 11:\nNever create random CSV files simply to make the application appear data-driven.\n\nRULE 12:\nNever use Gemini-generated numbers as authoritative agricultural calculations.\n\nRULE 13:\nGemini is an explanation/orchestration layer, not the source of truth for numerical optimization.\n\nRULE 14:\nAll numerical recommendations must come from deterministic calculations, trained models, optimization algorithms, or verified external data.\n\nRULE 15:\nIf a real data source is unavailable, clearly expose that limitation instead of silently fabricating data.\n\nRULE 16:\nIf an API key is unavailable, implement a clean provider interface and use a clearly labelled local snapshot only if the snapshot originates from a real source.\n\nRULE 17:\nA local snapshot must contain provenance metadata:\nsource\ndataset name\nretrieval date\nlicense/attribution if applicable\n\nRULE 18:\nNever call a local snapshot \"live\".\n\nRULE 19:\nDo not silently replace a required real dataset with synthetic data.\n\nRULE 20:\nSynthetic data may only be used for UI/unit testing and must be explicitly labelled SYNTHETIC_TEST_DATA and must never appear as real agricultural evidence.\n\n==================================================\n3. REQUIRED TECHNOLOGY STACK\n==================================================\n\nFrontend:\nFlutter\n\nBackend:\nPython FastAPI\n\nDatabase:\nSupabase PostgreSQL\n\nAuthentication:\nSupabase Auth\n\nStorage:\nSupabase Storage where required\n\nML:\nPython\nscikit-learn\nXGBoost\nProphet\n\nOptimization:\nGoogle OR-Tools\n\nLLM:\nGemini API\n\nWeather:\nOpen-Meteo\n\nMarket:\nGovernment of India Data.gov.in / AGMARKNET data\n\nSoil:\nISRIC SoilGrids where available\n\nDeployment architecture should remain compatible with:\nRender/Vercel/Supabase\n\nDo not introduce MongoDB, Firebase, Redis, Pinecone, Weaviate, Qdrant, or another database unless a concrete implementation requirement proves it necessary.\n\nThe architecture should remain as simple as possible.\n\n==================================================\n4. DATABASE ARCHITECTURE\n==================================================\n\nUse Supabase PostgreSQL.\n\nCreate a normalized schema.\n\nMinimum conceptual tables:\n\nprofiles\nfarms\nhousehold_members\nnutrition_requirements\nsoil_observations\ncrops\ncrop_nutrition\ncrop_yield_predictions\ncrop_plans\nplanting_calendar\nweather_observations\nweather_alerts\nmarket_prices\nmarket_forecasts\nharvest_inventory\nharvest_allocations\nmarket_recommendations\noptimization_runs\nagent_runs\ndata_sources\n\nDo not create tables merely because they sound impressive.\n\nEvery table must have a purpose.\n\nAdd:\ncreated_at\nupdated_at\nsource\nsource_date\nwhere appropriate.\n\nFor externally sourced data, preserve provenance.\n\nUse foreign keys.\n\nUse indexes for:\nfarm_id\ncrop_id\ndate\ncommodity\nmarket\ndistrict\nstate\n\nUse Row Level Security for user-owned data.\n\nNever expose service-role credentials to the Flutter application.\n\n==================================================\n5. FIVE-AGENT ARCHITECTURE\n==================================================\n\nImplement five logical agents.\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\"Agent\" does NOT necessarily mean five independent LLMs.\n\nUse deterministic/ML services wherever appropriate.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nA. NUTRITION AGENT\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nResponsibilities:\n\n1. Read household size.\n2. Read household age groups.\n3. Read dietary preferences.\n4. Calculate household nutrient targets.\n5. Identify nutrient gaps.\n6. Evaluate candidate crops/foods.\n7. Produce nutrition coverage metrics.\n\nPrimary nutrients:\n\nProtein\nIron\nCalcium\nVitamin A\nVitamin C\nFiber\nCalories\n\nDo NOT invent nutrient values.\n\nPreferred authoritative source:\n\nIndian Food Composition Tables (IFCT 2017), National Institute of Nutrition / ICMR.\n\nUse only the required structured records and preserve attribution/licensing information.\n\nIf the complete IFCT dataset cannot legally be embedded, design the data ingestion layer so that authorized/locally available records can be imported.\n\nDo not scrape and redistribute the entire book without verifying permission.\n\nNutrition Agent should use deterministic nutrient calculations.\n\nDo not train an ML model merely for calculating nutrient requirements.\n\n==================================================\n6. CROP PLANNING AGENT\n==================================================\n\nResponsibilities:\n\n1. Generate candidate crops.\n2. Consider:\n   soil\n   rainfall\n   temperature\n   season\n   land\n   water\n   crop history\n   budget\n3. Predict expected yield.\n4. Pass candidate crops and constraints to OR-Tools.\n\nMODEL:\n\nXGBoost Regressor for yield prediction.\n\nRecommended training data:\n\nAgricultural Crop Yield in Indian States Dataset.\n\nPotential features:\n\ncrop\nstate\nseason\narea\nannual rainfall\nfertilizer\npesticide\ntemperature\nsoil variables where legitimately available\n\nTarget:\n\nYield.\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\nDo NOT use Production as an input feature if Yield is calculated from Production / Area.\n\nThis would create target leakage.\n\nImplement:\ntrain/validation/test split.\n\nPrefer time-aware validation where appropriate.\n\nStore:\nmodel version\ntraining dataset\ntraining date\nfeatures\nmetrics\n\nEvaluate at minimum:\nMAE\nRMSE\nR2\n\nNever hardcode accuracy.\n\nDisplay actual measured model metrics.\n\nIf the dataset is unavailable, DO NOT claim XGBoost is trained.\n\nInstead show:\n\"Model training dataset not configured.\"\n\n==================================================\n7. CLIMATE AGENT\n==================================================\n\nUse Open-Meteo.\n\nInputs:\n\nlatitude\nlongitude\n\nFetch:\n\ntemperature\nprecipitation\nprecipitation probability\nhumidity where available\nwind\nsoil moisture where available\nET0 where available\n\nResponsibilities:\n\n1. Rain alert.\n2. Irrigation recommendation.\n3. Planting-window advisory.\n4. Heat-safety alert.\n5. Climate risk score.\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\nThese alerts should use transparent rules.\n\nExample:\n\nIF significant rainfall is forecast:\n    reduce/skip irrigation recommendation\n\nIF extreme heat threshold is crossed:\n    issue heat-safety warning\n\nDo not allow Gemini to invent weather conditions.\n\nEvery weather-driven recommendation should store:\nforecast timestamp\nforecast date\nsource = Open-Meteo\n\nIf Open-Meteo is unavailable:\nshow:\n\"Weather data temporarily unavailable.\"\n\nDo not fabricate weather.\n\n==================================================\n8. MARKET AGENT\n==================================================\n\nPrimary data source:\n\nGovernment of India Data.gov.in / AGMARKNET.\n\nHistorical training data may use the Kaggle:\nDaily Wholesale Commodity Prices \342\200\223 India Mandis\n\nExpected fields include:\n\nArrival_Date\nState\nDistrict\nMarket\nCommodity\nVariety\nMin_Price\nMax_Price\nModal_Price\n\nUse Modal_Price as the main forecasting target unless another justified choice is documented.\n\nMarket Agent responsibilities:\n\n1. Current/latest available mandi price.\n2. Historical trend.\n3. Market comparison.\n4. Price forecasting.\n5. Best selling market recommendation.\n6. Selling-time recommendation.\n\nMODEL:\n\nProphet.\n\nTrain separate forecasting models for sufficiently populated:\ncommodity + market time series.\n\nDo not train a forecast model on an insufficiently sized time series.\n\nIf a series has insufficient observations:\nfallback to transparent historical statistics such as recent median/mean/trend.\n\nClearly label this as a fallback.\n\nNever fabricate future prices.\n\nPrediction UI must clearly distinguish:\n\nObserved price\nForecast price\n\nExample:\n\nObserved modal price:\n\342\202\271X/kg\n\nForecast:\n\342\202\271Y/kg\n\nForecast date:\nYYYY-MM-DD\n\nDo not say:\n\"Guaranteed price.\"\n\n==================================================\n9. FINANCE AGENT\n==================================================\n\nDo NOT use an LLM to perform financial arithmetic.\n\nFinance Agent uses:\n\nOR-Tools\n+\npredicted yield\n+\nnutrition constraints\n+\nmarket prices/forecast\n+\ntransport cost\n+\nstorage cost\n+\nfarmer budget\n+\nland\n+\nwater constraints\n\nPrimary objective:\n\nMaximize expected household utility / income\n\nwhile satisfying minimum nutrition requirements and farm constraints.\n\nPossible decision variables:\n\ncrop acreage\nharvest quantity\nfamily consumption quantity\nmarket quantity\nstorage quantity\nemergency reserve\nseed reserve\n\nConstraints:\n\nland <= available land\n\nwater <= available water\n\nbudget <= farmer budget\n\nfamily nutrition >= minimum target\n\nharvest allocation <= expected harvest\n\nmarket quantity + family quantity + storage + reserve <= harvest\n\nDo not create mathematically impossible recommendations.\n\nIf no feasible solution exists:\nreturn a clear explanation of which constraints conflict.\n\n==================================================\n10. HARVEST ALLOCATION\n==================================================\n\nThe PPT contains the concept:\n\nMarket Sale\nStorage\nFamily Consumption\nEmergency Buffer\nSeed Reserve\n\nImplement this as a real optimization problem.\n\nExample demo values from the presentation are illustrative only.\n\nDO NOT hardcode the PPT\'s:\n650 kg\n250 kg\n180 kg\n70 kg\n50 kg\n\nas actual farmer recommendations.\n\nThose values are explicitly illustrative.\n\nThe application must calculate its own result.\n\n==================================================\n11. NUTRITION COVERAGE SCORE\n==================================================\n\nCalculate coverage for:\n\nProtein\nIron\nCalcium\nVitamin A\nVitamin C\nFiber\nCalories\n\nExample formula:\n\ncoverage = achieved / target * 100\n\nCap display at 100% if appropriate for coverage visualization.\n\nKeep the raw value separately if needed.\n\nOverall score should be clearly documented.\n\nDo not fabricate the 78/62/70/etc. values from the presentation.\n\nThose are illustrative demo values.\n\n==================================================\n12. GEMINI ROLE\n==================================================\n\nGemini is the natural-language intelligence layer.\n\nGemini may:\n\n1. Explain recommendations.\n2. Summarize the farm plan.\n3. Answer farmer questions.\n4. Convert technical outputs into simple language.\n5. Provide regional-language explanations.\n6. Explain why a crop was selected.\n7. Explain why produce should be sold/stored/consumed.\n8. Orchestrate calls to specialized backend tools.\n\nGemini must NOT:\n\n1. Invent prices.\n2. Invent weather.\n3. Invent soil values.\n4. Invent nutrient values.\n5. Invent yield.\n6. Override OR-Tools.\n7. Override database values.\n8. Claim live data without live data.\n9. Generate fake model accuracy.\n10. generate fake citations.\n\nGemini should receive structured JSON from backend services and explain it.\n\nUse a system instruction equivalent to:\n\n\"Never invent numerical agricultural facts. Only use values supplied by tools/database. If information is missing, say that it is unavailable.\"\n\n==================================================\n13. API PROVIDER ARCHITECTURE\n==================================================\n\nDo not tightly couple the entire application to external APIs.\n\nCreate provider interfaces such as:\n\nWeatherProvider\nMarketProvider\nSoilProvider\nNutritionProvider\n\nNUTRIHARVEST AI \342\200\224 DATASET / DATABASE LINKS\n\n==============================\n1. CROP YIELD \342\200\224 XGBOOST\n==============================\n\n1. Agricultural Crop Yield in Indian States (1997\342\200\2232020)\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/datasets/akshatgupta7/crop-yield-in-indian-states-dataset/versions/1\n\n2. Crop Yield Data \342\200\224 Enhanced with NASA Temperature Data\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/datasets/apoorvagondane/crop-yield-data\n\nIMPORTANT:\nRemove Production from model features because Yield = Production / Area.\nThe dataset itself explicitly warns about this target leakage.\n\n3. Indian Historical Crop Yield and Weather Data\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/datasets/zoya77/indian-historical-crop-yield-and-weather-data\n\nContains ICRISAT crop-yield data + IMD/Government weather data.\n\n4. Crop Yield Data with Soil and Weather Dataset\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/datasets/anshumish/crop-yield-data-with-soil-and-weather-dataset\n\nContains:\n- Crop yield\n- Soil N/P/K\n- Soil pH\n- Temperature\n- Rainfall\n- Humidity\n- Fertilizer\n- Pesticide\n\n\n==============================\n2. CROP RECOMMENDATION\n==============================\n\n5. Crop Recommendation Dataset \342\200\224 Siddharth Sharma\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/datasets/siddharthss/crop-recommendation-dataset\n\nContains:\n- Nitrogen\n- Phosphorus\n- Potassium\n- Temperature\n- Humidity\n- pH\n- Rainfall\n- Crop\n\n6. Crop Recommendation Dataset \342\200\224 Atharva Ingle\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/datasets/atharvaingle/crop-recommendation-dataset\n\n7. Crop Recommendation Dataset \342\200\224 Madhura Atmaram Bhagat\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/datasets/madhuraatmarambhagat/crop-recommendation-dataset\n\n8. Crop Recommendation \342\200\224 Arka Bhowmik\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/datasets/arkabhowmik/crop-recommendation\n\nWARNING:\nDo NOT use synthetic crop recommendation datasets as real agricultural evidence.\n\n\n==============================\n3. MANDI / MARKET PRICES\n==============================\n\n9. Government of India \342\200\224 Data.gov.in / AGMARKNET\nhttps://www.data.gov.in/catalog/current-daily-price-various-commodities-various-markets-mandi\n\nUse this for:\n- Current/latest mandi prices\n- Minimum price\n- Maximum price\n- Modal price\n- Commodity\n- Market\n\n10. Daily Wholesale Commodity Prices \342\200\224 India Mandis\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/datasets/ishankat/daily-wholesale-commodity-prices-india-mandis\n\nUse for:\n- Historical mandi prices\n- Prophet training\n- Market trend analysis\n\n11. Daily Commodity Prices \342\200\224 India\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/datasets/khandelwalmanas/daily-commodity-prices-india\n\nAlternative historical mandi dataset.\n\n\n==============================\n4. NUTRITION\n==============================\n\n12. Indian Food Composition Tables (IFCT 2017) \342\200\224 ICMR/NIN\nhttps://www.nin.res.in/ebooks/IFCT2017_16122024.pdf\n\nPRIMARY / PREFERRED NUTRITION SOURCE.\n\nUse for:\n- Protein\n- Iron\n- Calcium\n- Vitamin A\n- Vitamin C\n- Fiber\n- Calories\n- Other nutrient composition\n\n13. IFCT 2017 Open-source Structured Repository\nhttps://github.com/ifct2017/ifct2017\n\nStructured implementation/data derived from IFCT 2017.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nCheck licensing/attribution requirements before redistributing IFCT-derived data.\n\n\n==============================\n5. INDIAN FOOD / NUTRITION FALLBACK DATASETS\n==============================\n\n14. Indian Food Nutrition & Calories Dataset\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/datasets/shadab80k/indian-food-nutrition-and-calories-dataset\n\n15. Indian Food Nutritional Values Dataset\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/datasets/batthulavinay/indian-food-nutrition\n\n16. Indian Food Nutrition Dataset\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/datasets/syedkhalid076/indian-food-nutrition\n\nIMPORTANT:\nThese are fallback datasets.\nPrefer IFCT 2017 for authoritative nutrition composition.\n\n\n==============================\n6. INDIAN FOOD / RECIPE DATABASE\n==============================\n\n17. Indian Food Dataset \342\200\224 6000+ Recipes\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/datasets/sukhmandeepsinghbrar/indian-food-dataset\n\nUseful for:\n- Meal suggestions\n- Recipe recommendations\n- Mapping crops/foods to meals\n\nDo NOT use recipe data as the authoritative source for nutrient values.\n\n\n==============================\n7. SOIL DATA\n==============================\n\n18. ISRIC SoilGrids\nhttps://soilgrids.org/\n\n19. SoilGrids REST API Documentation\nhttps://rest.isric.org/soilgrids/v2.0/docs\n\nUse for:\n- Soil properties\n- Soil pH\n- Soil organic carbon\n- Soil texture\n- Soil characteristics\n- Location-based soil information\n\nDo NOT create fake soil.csv files.\n\n\n==============================\n8. WEATHER DATA\n==============================\n\n20. Open-Meteo\nhttps://open-meteo.com/\n\n21. Open-Meteo API Documentation\nhttps://open-meteo.com/en/docs\n\nUse for:\n- Temperature\n- Rainfall\n- Precipitation probability\n- Humidity\n- Wind\n- ET0\n- Soil moisture\n- Forecasts\n\nDo NOT hardcode fake weather values.\n\n\n==============================\n9. OPTIONAL AGRICULTURAL DATA\n==============================\n\n22. Farmer.in \342\200\224 Free Indian Agriculture Datasets\nhttps://www.farmer.in/datasets/\n\nIncludes:\n- India MSP\n- APMC Mandi Directory\n- Crop Modal Price Dataset\n\nUseful as an additional market/reference source.\n\n\n==============================\n10. FUTURE PHASE \342\200\224 DISEASE DETECTION\n==============================\n\n23. PlantVillage Dataset\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/datasets/emmarex/plantdisease\n\n24. PlantDoc Dataset\nhttps://github.com/pratikkayal/PlantDoc-Dataset\n\nIMPORTANT:\nDisease detection is Phase 3.\nDO NOT build/train this CNN for the current MVP unless everything else is already working.\n\n\n==============================\n11. DATABASE / BACKEND\n==============================\n\n25. Supabase\nhttps://supabase.com/\n\n26. Supabase PostgreSQL Documentation\nhttps://supabase.com/docs/guides/database/overview\n\n27. Supabase Auth\nhttps://supabase.com/docs/guides/auth\n\n28. Supabase Storage\nhttps://supabase.com/docs/guides/storage\n\n\n==============================\n12. ML / OPTIMIZATION\n==============================\n\n29. XGBoost\nhttps://xgboost.readthedocs.io/\n\n30. Prophet\nhttps://facebook.github.io/prophet/\n\n31. Google OR-Tools\nhttps://developers.google.com/optimization/\n\n\n==============================\n13. AI\n==============================\n\n32. Google Gemini API\nhttps://ai.google.dev/\n\nGemini should be used for:\n- Natural language\n- Explanation\n- Agent orchestration\n- Farmer queries\n- Regional-language responses\n\nGemini MUST NOT be the authoritative source for:\n- Weather\n- Soil\n- Nutrition\n- Mandi prices\n- Yield\n- Optimization calculations\n\nYou are a senior full-stack engineer, ML engineer, and DevOps engineer. I want you to take my existing project and turn it into a **fully functional, production-ready web application**.\n\n### 1. Complete the Existing Application\n\n* First, inspect the entire existing codebase and understand the current architecture, features, frontend, backend, database, and ML pipeline.\n* Do **not** unnecessarily rewrite working parts of the application.\n* Fix all bugs, broken functionality, incomplete features, API issues, UI issues, and integration problems you find.\n* Make sure every major feature actually works end-to-end rather than being a UI mockup.\n* Remove placeholder/demo functionality where real implementation is possible.\n\n### 2. ML Model & Dataset\n\n* Inspect the existing ML model and dataset carefully.\n* Evaluate whether the current dataset is sufficient for the application\'s intended predictions/classifications.\n* If the dataset is too small, low-quality, biased, outdated, poorly labeled, or unsuitable, **replace or improve it with a better publicly available dataset**.\n* Prefer reliable datasets from sources such as Kaggle, government/open-data portals, research datasets, or other trustworthy sources.\n* Clearly document the dataset source and preprocessing steps.\n* Retrain/rebuild the ML model if necessary.\n* Evaluate the model using appropriate metrics and make sure the final model is actually usable by the application.\n* Integrate the trained model properly with the backend/API.\n* Do not fabricate ML performance metrics or pretend that a model works if it has not been tested.\n\n### 3. Authentication & Supabase\n\nImplement **proper production-ready authentication using Supabase**.\n\nRequirements:\n\n* Supabase must be the authentication/database backend where appropriate.\n* Implement:\n\n  * Sign up\n  * Login\n  * Logout\n  * Session persistence\n  * Protected routes/pages\n  * Authentication state handling\n  * Password reset if appropriate\n  * Proper error handling\n* Store user-specific application data in Supabase where required.\n* Use Supabase Row Level Security (RLS) where applicable so users cannot access another user\'s private data.\n* Never expose Supabase service-role keys or other secrets in frontend code.\n* Use environment variables for all credentials/API keys.\n* Make sure authentication works both locally and after deployment.\n\n### 4. Frontend\n\nMake the frontend polished and production-ready:\n\n* Responsive on desktop, tablet, and mobile.\n* Proper loading states.\n* Proper error states.\n* Form validation.\n* Empty states.\n* Clean navigation.\n* Consistent UI/UX.\n* No broken buttons or dead links.\n* No console errors.\n* Make sure authenticated and unauthenticated users see the correct pages.\n\n### 5. Backend\n\nMake sure the backend is fully functional and production-ready:\n\n* All APIs should work correctly.\n* Validate incoming requests.\n* Handle errors properly.\n* Add appropriate CORS configuration.\n* Ensure the ML inference endpoint works reliably.\n* Do not hardcode localhost URLs.\n* Use environment variables for frontend/backend URLs and secrets.\n\n### 6. Deployment Architecture\n\nDeploy the application using:\n\n**Frontend \342\206\222 Vercel**\n\n**Backend/API/ML service \342\206\222 Railway**\n\n**Authentication + Database \342\206\222 Supabase**\n\nConfigure everything so the deployed application communicates correctly:\n\nUser\n\342\206\223\nVercel Frontend\n\342\206\223\nRailway Backend/API\n\342\206\223\nML Model\n\342\206\223\nSupabase Database/Auth\n\nMake sure:\n\n* Vercel deployment works.\n* Railway deployment works.\n* Frontend correctly calls the Railway backend.\n* Railway correctly communicates with Supabase.\n* Supabase authentication works with the deployed Vercel domain.\n* CORS is configured correctly for the Vercel production URL.\n* Environment variables are configured correctly on both Vercel and Railway.\n* Production URLs are used instead of localhost.\n* No secrets are committed to GitHub.\n\n### 7. Deployment Configuration\n\nCreate/fix all necessary deployment files and configuration, such as:\n\n* `vercel.json` if required\n* `railway.json` / Railway configuration if required\n* `Dockerfile` if useful/necessary\n* `requirements.txt` / `package.json`\n* Proper start commands\n* Build commands\n* Environment variable configuration documentation\n\nMake the project deployable with minimal manual configuration.\n\n### 8. Testing\n\nBefore considering the project complete, test the complete user flow:\n\n1. User opens the deployed Vercel application.\n2. User creates an account.\n3. User logs in.\n4. User accesses the protected dashboard.\n5. User enters real application data.\n6. Frontend sends the request to Railway.\n7. Backend processes the request.\n8. ML model generates the prediction/result.\n9. Result is returned to the frontend.\n10. Relevant user data is stored/retrieved from Supabase.\n11. User logs out.\n12. Protected pages are no longer accessible.\n13. User logs back in and their data/session works correctly.\n\nAlso test invalid inputs, API failures, authentication failures, and missing data.\n\n### 9. Security\n\nFollow reasonable production security practices:\n\n* Never expose secrets.\n* Use `.env` locally and platform environment variables in production.\n* Add `.env` to `.gitignore`.\n* Never commit API keys, passwords, service-role keys, or private credentials.\n* Validate user input.\n* Implement Supabase RLS where necessary.\n* Restrict backend CORS to the correct frontend origin in production.\n* Do not trust client-side authorization alone.\n\n### 10. Final Deliverables\n\nAt the end, provide:\n\n1. What you changed.\n2. Final project architecture.\n3. ML model and dataset used.\n4. Dataset source.\n5. ML evaluation results.\n6. Supabase setup/configuration.\n7. Required environment variables.\n8. Vercel deployment instructions.\n9. Railway deployment instructions.\n10. Any database tables/RLS policies created.\n11. Test results.\n12. Final production URLs for:\n\n* Frontend/Vercel\n* Backend/Railway\n\n13. Any remaining limitations or known issues.\n\n### Important Instructions\n\n* **Do not stop at giving me instructions or code snippets. Actually implement the changes in the project.**\n* **Do not leave features as placeholders.**\n* **Do not use fake authentication.**\n* **Do not use mock ML predictions when a real model can be integrated.**\n* **Do not fabricate datasets, metrics, or test results.**\n* If the existing architecture needs changes to make the application production-ready, make those changes.\n* Preserve existing functionality wherever possible.\n* Prioritize a working end-to-end application over unnecessary complexity.\n* Before finishing, verify that the application can actually be deployed and used by a real user.\n* If you encounter a problem that prevents deployment, diagnose it, fix it, and continue rather than simply reporting the problem.\n\nExample:\n\nWeatherProvider\n    OpenMeteoWeatherProvider\n\nMarketProvider\n    DataGovAgmarknetProvider\n    LocalHistoricalMarketProvider\n\nSoilProvider\n    SoilGridsProvider\n    FarmerEnteredSoilProvider\n\nThis allows the application to work even if one external service temporarily fails.\n\n==================================================\n14. DATA PROVENANCE\n==================================================\n\nCreate a data_sources table.\n\nFor every external dataset store:\n\nname\nsource_url\nprovider\nretrieved_at\nlicense\ndescription\nversion\nlocal_file\nstatus\n\nExamples:\n\nAGMARKNET / Data.gov.in\nOpen-Meteo\nISRIC SoilGrids\nIFCT 2017\nKaggle historical mandi dataset\nKaggle crop yield dataset\n\nThe UI should show a small:\n\"Data sources\"\nsection.\n\nDo not hide provenance.\n\n==================================================\n15. OFFLINE-FIRST REQUIREMENT\n==================================================\n\nThe presentation explicitly requires an offline-first farmer experience.\n\nImplement a practical prototype version.\n\nFlutter should cache:\n\nfarmer profile\nfarm profile\nlatest crop plan\nplanting calendar\nlast known weather result\ninventory\nlast recommendations\n\nWhen internet is unavailable:\n\nshow cached data with:\n\"Last synced: <timestamp>\"\n\nDo not pretend cached information is real-time.\n\nWhen connectivity returns:\nsync with backend.\n\n==================================================\n16. FARMER INPUT FLOW\n==================================================\n\nCreate an easy demo flow.\n\nStep 1:\nFarmer registration\n\nStep 2:\nHousehold:\n\nfamily size\nage groups\ndiet preference\n\nStep 3:\nFarm:\n\nland size\nlocation\nwater availability\nsoil information\nbudget\n\nStep 4:\nCrop history\n\nStep 5:\nGenerate AI crop plan\n\nStep 6:\nShow nutrition coverage\n\nStep 7:\nShow planting calendar\n\nStep 8:\nShow climate alerts\n\nStep 9:\nRecord harvest\n\nStep 10:\nRun harvest allocation\n\nStep 11:\nShow family consumption vs surplus\n\nStep 12:\nShow mandi prices\n\nStep 13:\nShow market forecast\n\nStep 14:\nShow selling recommendation\n\nStep 15:\nShow expected income\n\n==================================================\n17. DASHBOARD\n==================================================\n\nCreate a polished hackathon-quality dashboard.\n\nMain cards:\n\nNutrition Coverage\nExpected Yield\nMarketable Surplus\nExpected Income\nClimate Risk\nSustainability Score\n\nSections:\n\nToday\'s Advisory\nCrop Plan\nNutrition Gaps\nWeather\nMandi Prices\nHarvest Inventory\nSell/Store Recommendation\n\nUse charts where they genuinely communicate data.\n\nDo not create decorative charts containing invented numbers.\n\nEvery chart must be backed by application data.\n\n==================================================\n18. DEMO MODE\n==================================================\n\nCreate a DEMO FARM profile.\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\nThe demo farm must use either:\n\nA. real data from configured sources\n\nOR\n\nB. clearly labelled demo/test values.\n\nNever make test values look like measured field results.\n\nAdd:\n\n\"Demo Scenario\"\nor\n\"Illustrative Scenario\"\n\nwhere appropriate.\n\nDo not claim:\n\"field validated\"\n\"scientifically proven\"\n\"40% reduction\"\n\"35% income increase\"\netc.\n\nThe PPT explicitly labels those impact numbers as target/expected/potential and not experimentally validated.\n\nDo not present them as achieved results.\n\n==================================================\n19. FEATURES FROM PPT\n==================================================\n\nImplement the following core features where feasible:\n\nNutrition:\n- household nutrient scoring\n- micronutrient gap analysis\n- personalized crop-diet match\n- meal suggestions where data supports them\n\nClimate:\n- hyperlocal weather\n- irrigation scheduling\n- climate alerts\n- heat-safety alerts\n\nMarket:\n- mandi price feed\n- historical price trend\n- price forecasting\n- surplus allocation\n- selling-time recommendation\n\nFarmer tools:\n- offline-first app\n- planting calendar\n- inventory\n- voice/regional language architecture\n\nPlatform:\n- multi-agent architecture\n- role-aware backend\n- secure authentication\n- cloud sync\n\n==================================================\n20. FEATURES NOT REQUIRED FOR THIS MVP\n==================================================\n\nDo NOT waste development time implementing:\n\nDisease vision model\nIoT sensors\nhardware integration\nnational-scale infrastructure\ncomplex satellite imagery\nadvanced computer vision\n\nThese are Phase 3/4/5 roadmap items.\n\nThe disease detection vision model is future scope.\n\n==================================================\n21. VOICE\n==================================================\n\nIf implementing voice, make it modular.\n\nDo not build an enormous speech system.\n\nArchitecture:\n\nVoice input\n\342\206\222 speech-to-text\n\342\206\222 structured farmer query\n\342\206\222 backend/Gemini\n\342\206\222 response\n\342\206\222 optional text-to-speech\n\nIf reliable regional-language speech cannot be implemented within the current environment, keep the UI architecture ready but do not fake it.\n\n==================================================\n22. SECURITY\n==================================================\n\nNever place:\n\nGemini API key\nSupabase service role key\nother secrets\n\ninside Flutter source code.\n\nUse environment variables.\n\nProvide:\n\n.env.example\n\nNever commit real secrets.\n\n==================================================\n23. PROJECT STRUCTURE\n==================================================\n\nUse a clean structure similar to:\n\n/\n  frontend/\n    flutter_app/\n\n  backend/\n    app/\n      api/\n      agents/\n      models/\n      services/\n      optimization/\n      ml/\n      providers/\n      schemas/\n      database/\n\n  ml/\n    training/\n    models/\n    evaluation/\n\n  data/\n    raw/\n    processed/\n    README.md\n\n  docs/\n    architecture.md\n    data-sources.md\n    model-card.md\n\n  scripts/\n\n  tests/\n\n  .env.example\n\n  README.md\n\nDo not add unnecessary frameworks.\n\n==================================================\n24. MACHINE LEARNING PIPELINE\n==================================================\n\nCreate reproducible training scripts.\n\nYield:\n\nml/training/train_yield_model.py\n\nMarket:\n\nml/training/train_market_forecast.py\n\nEvaluation:\n\nml/training/evaluate_models.py\n\nSave model metadata.\n\nDo not silently train during application startup.\n\nTraining should be an explicit reproducible process.\n\nIf model artifacts are unavailable, application should fail gracefully and explain that the model needs to be trained/configured.\n\n==================================================\n25. MODEL CARDS\n==================================================\n\nCreate model documentation.\n\nFor XGBoost:\n\npurpose\ndataset\nfeatures\ntarget\nsplit strategy\nmetrics\nlimitations\n\nFor Prophet:\n\npurpose\ndataset\ntime series definition\nforecast horizon\nvalidation method\nmetrics\nlimitations\n\nClearly explain:\n\nThese models are decision-support tools, not guarantees.\n\n==================================================\n26. TESTING\n==================================================\n\nBefore declaring completion:\n\nRun backend tests.\n\nRun frontend tests.\n\nRun ML tests.\n\nTest:\n\nnutrition calculation\ncoverage calculation\nyield prediction interface\nmarket forecast interface\nOR-Tools feasibility\nharvest allocation\nAPI failure\nmissing API key\noffline mode\ndatabase failure\ninvalid farmer input\n\nTest that:\n\nfamily consumption never exceeds available harvest.\n\nmarket + family + storage + reserves never exceeds expected harvest.\n\nnutrition minimum constraints are respected when feasible.\n\nland constraints are respected.\n\nbudget constraints are respected.\n\nwater constraints are respected.\n\n==================================================\n27. ANTI-HALLUCINATION QA\n==================================================\n\nCreate tests specifically for hallucination prevention.\n\nExamples:\n\nIf weather API returns no data:\nsystem must not invent weather.\n\nIf market API returns no price:\nsystem must not invent a price.\n\nIf soil API returns no soil:\nsystem must not invent soil.\n\nIf nutrition database has no food:\nsystem must say data unavailable.\n\nIf XGBoost model isn\'t trained:\nsystem must not claim prediction.\n\nIf Prophet has insufficient history:\nsystem must not claim a reliable forecast.\n\nIf OR-Tools finds no feasible solution:\nsystem must not manufacture one.\n\n==================================================\n28. UI LABELING\n==================================================\n\nDistinguish clearly:\n\nLIVE\nFORECAST\nHISTORICAL\nMODEL PREDICTION\nILLUSTRATIVE\nDEMO\nCACHED\nUNAVAILABLE\n\nExample:\n\n\"\342\202\27134/kg \342\200\224 Latest observed mandi modal price\"\n\nnot:\n\n\"AI knows the price is \342\202\27134 tomorrow.\"\n\nExample:\n\n\"\342\202\27137/kg \342\200\224 Prophet forecast\"\n\nnot:\n\n\"Expected guaranteed selling price.\"\n\n==================================================\n29. SOURCES\n==================================================\n\nPreferred sources:\n\n1. Government of India Data.gov.in / AGMARKNET\n2. Open-Meteo\n3. ISRIC SoilGrids\n4. ICMR-NIN IFCT 2017\n5. Publicly documented Kaggle datasets where necessary\n\nCandidate historical datasets:\n\nDaily Wholesale Commodity Prices \342\200\223 India Mandis\n\nAgricultural Crop Yield in Indian States Dataset\n\nIndian crop yield + weather datasets\n\nCrop recommendation datasets\n\nUse real source provenance.\n\n==================================================\n30. DO NOT OVERBUILD\n==================================================\n\nThis is a hackathon prototype.\n\nPrioritize:\n\nWORKING END-TO-END FLOW\n\nover\n\nhuge number of unfinished features.\n\nThe minimum successful pipeline is:\n\nFarmer Inputs\n\342\206\222 Nutrition Requirements\n\342\206\222 Candidate Crops\n\342\206\222 XGBoost Yield\n\342\206\222 Weather\n\342\206\222 Market Prices\n\342\206\222 Prophet Forecast\n\342\206\222 OR-Tools Allocation\n\342\206\222 Nutrition Coverage\n\342\206\222 Surplus\n\342\206\222 Sell/Store Recommendation\n\342\206\222 Income Estimate\n\nThis pipeline must actually work.\n\n==================================================\n31. DEMO VIDEO REQUIREMENT\n==================================================\n\nBuild the application so a 3\342\200\2235 minute demo can clearly demonstrate:\n\n1. Farmer registers.\n2. Household nutrition profile is entered.\n3. Farm/location/soil/land data is entered.\n4. System generates crop plan.\n5. Nutrition coverage appears.\n6. Planting calendar appears.\n7. Weather alert appears.\n8. Farmer records harvest.\n9. OR-Tools allocates harvest.\n10. Mandi price appears.\n11. Prophet forecast appears.\n12. Finance recommendation appears.\n13. Dashboard shows final impact.\n\nAvoid screens that require manual developer intervention.\n\n==================================================\n32. ERROR HANDLING\n==================================================\n\nEvery external dependency must have graceful failure.\n\nNo blank screen.\n\nNo uncaught exception.\n\nNo fake fallback data.\n\nExample:\n\n\"Live mandi data unavailable.\nShowing the latest locally cached verified dataset from <date>.\"\n\nThat is acceptable.\n\nThis is NOT acceptable:\n\n\"\342\202\27142/kg\"\n\nwhen no source exists.\n\n==================================================\n33. README\n==================================================\n\nCreate a professional README containing:\n\nProject overview\nArchitecture\nFeatures\nTech stack\nDatabase schema\nAgents\nML models\nDatasets\nData provenance\nAPI setup\nEnvironment variables\nLocal setup\nTraining instructions\nTesting\nDemo flow\nLimitations\nFuture roadmap\nCredits\n\nClearly distinguish:\n\nimplemented\nprototype\nfuture scope\n\n==================================================\n34. FINAL VERIFICATION\n==================================================\n\nAfter implementation:\n\n1. Install dependencies.\n2. Run backend.\n3. Run frontend.\n4. Run database migrations.\n5. Train models if datasets are actually available.\n6. Run tests.\n7. Open the application.\n8. Exercise the complete demo flow.\n9. Fix all runtime errors.\n10. Verify API failure handling.\n11. Verify no secrets are exposed.\n12. Verify no fabricated datasets exist.\n13. Verify no hardcoded fake market prices exist.\n14. Verify no hardcoded fake weather exists.\n15. Verify no fabricated ML accuracy exists.\n16. Verify README matches actual implementation.\n\nOnly after all of this should you report completion.\n\n==================================================\n35. FINAL RESPONSE FROM YOU\n==================================================\n\nWhen finished, report ONLY:\n\n1. What was actually implemented.\n2. Which datasets were actually used.\n3. Which APIs are actually connected.\n4. Which ML models were actually trained.\n5. Actual evaluation metrics.\n6. Which parts remain unavailable because of missing credentials/data.\n7. How to run the application.\n8. Demo flow.\n9. Any remaining bugs.\n\nDo not claim anything that cannot be verified from the workspace.\n\nMOST IMPORTANT RULE:\n\nREAL DATA > FABRICATED DATA\n\nREAL CALCULATION > LLM GUESS\n\nREAL MODEL > CLAIMED MODEL\n\nREAL API RESPONSE > INVENTED API RESPONSE\n\nTRANSPARENT LIMITATION > HALLUCINATION\n\nBuild the working NutriHarvest AI prototype now."
    items {
      text: "First read the entire prompt and dont hallucinate and then start thinking. You are the principal software architect, senior full-stack engineer, ML engineer, data engineer, and QA engineer responsible for BUILDING the NutriHarvest AI hackathon prototype.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nThis is a real hackathon prototype that will be demonstrated through a recorded demo video and submitted as a repository.\n\nYour job is to BUILD THE ACTUAL WORKING PROJECT in the current workspace.\n\nDO NOT merely describe the implementation.\nDO NOT give me a tutorial.\nDO NOT generate a fake prototype consisting only of static screens.\nDO NOT create a fake database with invented agricultural facts.\nDO NOT hallucinate APIs, datasets, model accuracy, market prices, crop yields, nutrition values, or government data.\n\nYou must inspect the workspace, create the application, run it, test it, fix errors, and leave a working prototype in the workspace.\n\nDO NOT commit to GitHub.\nDO NOT push/upload to GitHub.\nDO NOT create commits.\nDO NOT publish/deploy anything unless explicitly asked later.\n\n==================================================\n1. SOURCE OF TRUTH\n==================================================\n\nThe attached NutriHarvest AI presentation and project specification are the primary product requirements.\n\nProject:\n\nNutriHarvest AI\nPersonalized Crop-to-Plate Planner\n\nCore objective:\n\nOptimize:\n1. Household nutrition\n2. Crop planning\n3. Farm productivity\n4. Climate resilience\n5. Harvest allocation\n6. Market selling decisions\n7. Farmer income\n\nCore philosophy:\n\n\"Grow what your family needs.\nSell what your family doesn\'t need.\nEarn smarter.\"\n\nThe system is designed for smallholder farming families.\n\nThe core product is NOT merely a crop recommendation app.\n\nIt is a CLOSED-LOOP DECISION SUPPORT SYSTEM connecting:\n\nHousehold nutrition\n+\nCrop planning\n+\nYield prediction\n+\nClimate intelligence\n+\nHarvest allocation\n+\nMarket intelligence\n+\nIncome optimization\n\n==================================================\n2. ABSOLUTE ANTI-HALLUCINATION RULES\n==================================================\n\nThese rules have higher priority than convenience.\n\nRULE 1:\nNever invent a dataset.\n\nRULE 2:\nNever fabricate agricultural records and present them as real.\n\nRULE 3:\nNever fabricate mandi prices.\n\nRULE 4:\nNever fabricate weather values.\n\nRULE 5:\nNever fabricate soil measurements.\n\nRULE 6:\nNever fabricate nutrition values.\n\nRULE 7:\nNever claim a model was trained if it was not actually trained.\n\nRULE 8:\nNever claim model accuracy unless the model has actually been evaluated on a held-out test set.\n\nRULE 9:\nNever claim an API is live unless the application successfully calls it.\n\nRULE 10:\nNever claim a recommendation is based on real-time data if the application is using cached data.\n\nRULE 11:\nNever create random CSV files simply to make the application appear data-driven.\n\nRULE 12:\nNever use Gemini-generated numbers as authoritative agricultural calculations.\n\nRULE 13:\nGemini is an explanation/orchestration layer, not the source of truth for numerical optimization.\n\nRULE 14:\nAll numerical recommendations must come from deterministic calculations, trained models, optimization algorithms, or verified external data.\n\nRULE 15:\nIf a real data source is unavailable, clearly expose that limitation instead of silently fabricating data.\n\nRULE 16:\nIf an API key is unavailable, implement a clean provider interface and use a clearly labelled local snapshot only if the snapshot originates from a real source.\n\nRULE 17:\nA local snapshot must contain provenance metadata:\nsource\ndataset name\nretrieval date\nlicense/attribution if applicable\n\nRULE 18:\nNever call a local snapshot \"live\".\n\nRULE 19:\nDo not silently replace a required real dataset with synthetic data.\n\nRULE 20:\nSynthetic data may only be used for UI/unit testing and must be explicitly labelled SYNTHETIC_TEST_DATA and must never appear as real agricultural evidence.\n\n==================================================\n3. REQUIRED TECHNOLOGY STACK\n==================================================\n\nFrontend:\nFlutter\n\nBackend:\nPython FastAPI\n\nDatabase:\nSupabase PostgreSQL\n\nAuthentication:\nSupabase Auth\n\nStorage:\nSupabase Storage where required\n\nML:\nPython\nscikit-learn\nXGBoost\nProphet\n\nOptimization:\nGoogle OR-Tools\n\nLLM:\nGemini API\n\nWeather:\nOpen-Meteo\n\nMarket:\nGovernment of India Data.gov.in / AGMARKNET data\n\nSoil:\nISRIC SoilGrids where available\n\nDeployment architecture should remain compatible with:\nRender/Vercel/Supabase\n\nDo not introduce MongoDB, Firebase, Redis, Pinecone, Weaviate, Qdrant, or another database unless a concrete implementation requirement proves it necessary.\n\nThe architecture should remain as simple as possible.\n\n==================================================\n4. DATABASE ARCHITECTURE\n==================================================\n\nUse Supabase PostgreSQL.\n\nCreate a normalized schema.\n\nMinimum conceptual tables:\n\nprofiles\nfarms\nhousehold_members\nnutrition_requirements\nsoil_observations\ncrops\ncrop_nutrition\ncrop_yield_predictions\ncrop_plans\nplanting_calendar\nweather_observations\nweather_alerts\nmarket_prices\nmarket_forecasts\nharvest_inventory\nharvest_allocations\nmarket_recommendations\noptimization_runs\nagent_runs\ndata_sources\n\nDo not create tables merely because they sound impressive.\n\nEvery table must have a purpose.\n\nAdd:\ncreated_at\nupdated_at\nsource\nsource_date\nwhere appropriate.\n\nFor externally sourced data, preserve provenance.\n\nUse foreign keys.\n\nUse indexes for:\nfarm_id\ncrop_id\ndate\ncommodity\nmarket\ndistrict\nstate\n\nUse Row Level Security for user-owned data.\n\nNever expose service-role credentials to the Flutter application.\n\n==================================================\n5. FIVE-AGENT ARCHITECTURE\n==================================================\n\nImplement five logical agents.\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\"Agent\" does NOT necessarily mean five independent LLMs.\n\nUse deterministic/ML services wherever appropriate.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nA. NUTRITION AGENT\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nResponsibilities:\n\n1. Read household size.\n2. Read household age groups.\n3. Read dietary preferences.\n4. Calculate household nutrient targets.\n5. Identify nutrient gaps.\n6. Evaluate candidate crops/foods.\n7. Produce nutrition coverage metrics.\n\nPrimary nutrients:\n\nProtein\nIron\nCalcium\nVitamin A\nVitamin C\nFiber\nCalories\n\nDo NOT invent nutrient values.\n\nPreferred authoritative source:\n\nIndian Food Composition Tables (IFCT 2017), National Institute of Nutrition / ICMR.\n\nUse only the required structured records and preserve attribution/licensing information.\n\nIf the complete IFCT dataset cannot legally be embedded, design the data ingestion layer so that authorized/locally available records can be imported.\n\nDo not scrape and redistribute the entire book without verifying permission.\n\nNutrition Agent should use deterministic nutrient calculations.\n\nDo not train an ML model merely for calculating nutrient requirements.\n\n==================================================\n6. CROP PLANNING AGENT\n==================================================\n\nResponsibilities:\n\n1. Generate candidate crops.\n2. Consider:\n   soil\n   rainfall\n   temperature\n   season\n   land\n   water\n   crop history\n   budget\n3. Predict expected yield.\n4. Pass candidate crops and constraints to OR-Tools.\n\nMODEL:\n\nXGBoost Regressor for yield prediction.\n\nRecommended training data:\n\nAgricultural Crop Yield in Indian States Dataset.\n\nPotential features:\n\ncrop\nstate\nseason\narea\nannual rainfall\nfertilizer\npesticide\ntemperature\nsoil variables where legitimately available\n\nTarget:\n\nYield.\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\nDo NOT use Production as an input feature if Yield is calculated from Production / Area.\n\nThis would create target leakage.\n\nImplement:\ntrain/validation/test split.\n\nPrefer time-aware validation where appropriate.\n\nStore:\nmodel version\ntraining dataset\ntraining date\nfeatures\nmetrics\n\nEvaluate at minimum:\nMAE\nRMSE\nR2\n\nNever hardcode accuracy.\n\nDisplay actual measured model metrics.\n\nIf the dataset is unavailable, DO NOT claim XGBoost is trained.\n\nInstead show:\n\"Model training dataset not configured.\"\n\n==================================================\n7. CLIMATE AGENT\n==================================================\n\nUse Open-Meteo.\n\nInputs:\n\nlatitude\nlongitude\n\nFetch:\n\ntemperature\nprecipitation\nprecipitation probability\nhumidity where available\nwind\nsoil moisture where available\nET0 where available\n\nResponsibilities:\n\n1. Rain alert.\n2. Irrigation recommendation.\n3. Planting-window advisory.\n4. Heat-safety alert.\n5. Climate risk score.\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\nThese alerts should use transparent rules.\n\nExample:\n\nIF significant rainfall is forecast:\n    reduce/skip irrigation recommendation\n\nIF extreme heat threshold is crossed:\n    issue heat-safety warning\n\nDo not allow Gemini to invent weather conditions.\n\nEvery weather-driven recommendation should store:\nforecast timestamp\nforecast date\nsource = Open-Meteo\n\nIf Open-Meteo is unavailable:\nshow:\n\"Weather data temporarily unavailable.\"\n\nDo not fabricate weather.\n\n==================================================\n8. MARKET AGENT\n==================================================\n\nPrimary data source:\n\nGovernment of India Data.gov.in / AGMARKNET.\n\nHistorical training data may use the Kaggle:\nDaily Wholesale Commodity Prices \342\200\223 India Mandis\n\nExpected fields include:\n\nArrival_Date\nState\nDistrict\nMarket\nCommodity\nVariety\nMin_Price\nMax_Price\nModal_Price\n\nUse Modal_Price as the main forecasting target unless another justified choice is documented.\n\nMarket Agent responsibilities:\n\n1. Current/latest available mandi price.\n2. Historical trend.\n3. Market comparison.\n4. Price forecasting.\n5. Best selling market recommendation.\n6. Selling-time recommendation.\n\nMODEL:\n\nProphet.\n\nTrain separate forecasting models for sufficiently populated:\ncommodity + market time series.\n\nDo not train a forecast model on an insufficiently sized time series.\n\nIf a series has insufficient observations:\nfallback to transparent historical statistics such as recent median/mean/trend.\n\nClearly label this as a fallback.\n\nNever fabricate future prices.\n\nPrediction UI must clearly distinguish:\n\nObserved price\nForecast price\n\nExample:\n\nObserved modal price:\n\342\202\271X/kg\n\nForecast:\n\342\202\271Y/kg\n\nForecast date:\nYYYY-MM-DD\n\nDo not say:\n\"Guaranteed price.\"\n\n==================================================\n9. FINANCE AGENT\n==================================================\n\nDo NOT use an LLM to perform financial arithmetic.\n\nFinance Agent uses:\n\nOR-Tools\n+\npredicted yield\n+\nnutrition constraints\n+\nmarket prices/forecast\n+\ntransport cost\n+\nstorage cost\n+\nfarmer budget\n+\nland\n+\nwater constraints\n\nPrimary objective:\n\nMaximize expected household utility / income\n\nwhile satisfying minimum nutrition requirements and farm constraints.\n\nPossible decision variables:\n\ncrop acreage\nharvest quantity\nfamily consumption quantity\nmarket quantity\nstorage quantity\nemergency reserve\nseed reserve\n\nConstraints:\n\nland <= available land\n\nwater <= available water\n\nbudget <= farmer budget\n\nfamily nutrition >= minimum target\n\nharvest allocation <= expected harvest\n\nmarket quantity + family quantity + storage + reserve <= harvest\n\nDo not create mathematically impossible recommendations.\n\nIf no feasible solution exists:\nreturn a clear explanation of which constraints conflict.\n\n==================================================\n10. HARVEST ALLOCATION\n==================================================\n\nThe PPT contains the concept:\n\nMarket Sale\nStorage\nFamily Consumption\nEmergency Buffer\nSeed Reserve\n\nImplement this as a real optimization problem.\n\nExample demo values from the presentation are illustrative only.\n\nDO NOT hardcode the PPT\'s:\n650 kg\n250 kg\n180 kg\n70 kg\n50 kg\n\nas actual farmer recommendations.\n\nThose values are explicitly illustrative.\n\nThe application must calculate its own result.\n\n==================================================\n11. NUTRITION COVERAGE SCORE\n==================================================\n\nCalculate coverage for:\n\nProtein\nIron\nCalcium\nVitamin A\nVitamin C\nFiber\nCalories\n\nExample formula:\n\ncoverage = achieved / target * 100\n\nCap display at 100% if appropriate for coverage visualization.\n\nKeep the raw value separately if needed.\n\nOverall score should be clearly documented.\n\nDo not fabricate the 78/62/70/etc. values from the presentation.\n\nThose are illustrative demo values.\n\n==================================================\n12. GEMINI ROLE\n==================================================\n\nGemini is the natural-language intelligence layer.\n\nGemini may:\n\n1. Explain recommendations.\n2. Summarize the farm plan.\n3. Answer farmer questions.\n4. Convert technical outputs into simple language.\n5. Provide regional-language explanations.\n6. Explain why a crop was selected.\n7. Explain why produce should be sold/stored/consumed.\n8. Orchestrate calls to specialized backend tools.\n\nGemini must NOT:\n\n1. Invent prices.\n2. Invent weather.\n3. Invent soil values.\n4. Invent nutrient values.\n5. Invent yield.\n6. Override OR-Tools.\n7. Override database values.\n8. Claim live data without live data.\n9. Generate fake model accuracy.\n10. generate fake citations.\n\nGemini should receive structured JSON from backend services and explain it.\n\nUse a system instruction equivalent to:\n\n\"Never invent numerical agricultural facts. Only use values supplied by tools/database. If information is missing, say that it is unavailable.\"\n\n==================================================\n13. API PROVIDER ARCHITECTURE\n==================================================\n\nDo not tightly couple the entire application to external APIs.\n\nCreate provider interfaces such as:\n\nWeatherProvider\nMarketProvider\nSoilProvider\nNutritionProvider\n\nNUTRIHARVEST AI \342\200\224 DATASET / DATABASE LINKS\n\n==============================\n1. CROP YIELD \342\200\224 XGBOOST\n==============================\n\n1. Agricultural Crop Yield in Indian States (1997\342\200\2232020)\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/datasets/akshatgupta7/crop-yield-in-indian-states-dataset/versions/1\n\n2. Crop Yield Data \342\200\224 Enhanced with NASA Temperature Data\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/datasets/apoorvagondane/crop-yield-data\n\nIMPORTANT:\nRemove Production from model features because Yield = Production / Area.\nThe dataset itself explicitly warns about this target leakage.\n\n3. Indian Historical Crop Yield and Weather Data\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/datasets/zoya77/indian-historical-crop-yield-and-weather-data\n\nContains ICRISAT crop-yield data + IMD/Government weather data.\n\n4. Crop Yield Data with Soil and Weather Dataset\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/datasets/anshumish/crop-yield-data-with-soil-and-weather-dataset\n\nContains:\n- Crop yield\n- Soil N/P/K\n- Soil pH\n- Temperature\n- Rainfall\n- Humidity\n- Fertilizer\n- Pesticide\n\n\n==============================\n2. CROP RECOMMENDATION\n==============================\n\n5. Crop Recommendation Dataset \342\200\224 Siddharth Sharma\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/datasets/siddharthss/crop-recommendation-dataset\n\nContains:\n- Nitrogen\n- Phosphorus\n- Potassium\n- Temperature\n- Humidity\n- pH\n- Rainfall\n- Crop\n\n6. Crop Recommendation Dataset \342\200\224 Atharva Ingle\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/datasets/atharvaingle/crop-recommendation-dataset\n\n7. Crop Recommendation Dataset \342\200\224 Madhura Atmaram Bhagat\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/datasets/madhuraatmarambhagat/crop-recommendation-dataset\n\n8. Crop Recommendation \342\200\224 Arka Bhowmik\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/datasets/arkabhowmik/crop-recommendation\n\nWARNING:\nDo NOT use synthetic crop recommendation datasets as real agricultural evidence.\n\n\n==============================\n3. MANDI / MARKET PRICES\n==============================\n\n9. Government of India \342\200\224 Data.gov.in / AGMARKNET\nhttps://www.data.gov.in/catalog/current-daily-price-various-commodities-various-markets-mandi\n\nUse this for:\n- Current/latest mandi prices\n- Minimum price\n- Maximum price\n- Modal price\n- Commodity\n- Market\n\n10. Daily Wholesale Commodity Prices \342\200\224 India Mandis\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/datasets/ishankat/daily-wholesale-commodity-prices-india-mandis\n\nUse for:\n- Historical mandi prices\n- Prophet training\n- Market trend analysis\n\n11. Daily Commodity Prices \342\200\224 India\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/datasets/khandelwalmanas/daily-commodity-prices-india\n\nAlternative historical mandi dataset.\n\n\n==============================\n4. NUTRITION\n==============================\n\n12. Indian Food Composition Tables (IFCT 2017) \342\200\224 ICMR/NIN\nhttps://www.nin.res.in/ebooks/IFCT2017_16122024.pdf\n\nPRIMARY / PREFERRED NUTRITION SOURCE.\n\nUse for:\n- Protein\n- Iron\n- Calcium\n- Vitamin A\n- Vitamin C\n- Fiber\n- Calories\n- Other nutrient composition\n\n13. IFCT 2017 Open-source Structured Repository\nhttps://github.com/ifct2017/ifct2017\n\nStructured implementation/data derived from IFCT 2017.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nCheck licensing/attribution requirements before redistributing IFCT-derived data.\n\n\n==============================\n5. INDIAN FOOD / NUTRITION FALLBACK DATASETS\n==============================\n\n14. Indian Food Nutrition & Calories Dataset\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/datasets/shadab80k/indian-food-nutrition-and-calories-dataset\n\n15. Indian Food Nutritional Values Dataset\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/datasets/batthulavinay/indian-food-nutrition\n\n16. Indian Food Nutrition Dataset\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/datasets/syedkhalid076/indian-food-nutrition\n\nIMPORTANT:\nThese are fallback datasets.\nPrefer IFCT 2017 for authoritative nutrition composition.\n\n\n==============================\n6. INDIAN FOOD / RECIPE DATABASE\n==============================\n\n17. Indian Food Dataset \342\200\224 6000+ Recipes\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/datasets/sukhmandeepsinghbrar/indian-food-dataset\n\nUseful for:\n- Meal suggestions\n- Recipe recommendations\n- Mapping crops/foods to meals\n\nDo NOT use recipe data as the authoritative source for nutrient values.\n\n\n==============================\n7. SOIL DATA\n==============================\n\n18. ISRIC SoilGrids\nhttps://soilgrids.org/\n\n19. SoilGrids REST API Documentation\nhttps://rest.isric.org/soilgrids/v2.0/docs\n\nUse for:\n- Soil properties\n- Soil pH\n- Soil organic carbon\n- Soil texture\n- Soil characteristics\n- Location-based soil information\n\nDo NOT create fake soil.csv files.\n\n\n==============================\n8. WEATHER DATA\n==============================\n\n20. Open-Meteo\nhttps://open-meteo.com/\n\n21. Open-Meteo API Documentation\nhttps://open-meteo.com/en/docs\n\nUse for:\n- Temperature\n- Rainfall\n- Precipitation probability\n- Humidity\n- Wind\n- ET0\n- Soil moisture\n- Forecasts\n\nDo NOT hardcode fake weather values.\n\n\n==============================\n9. OPTIONAL AGRICULTURAL DATA\n==============================\n\n22. Farmer.in \342\200\224 Free Indian Agriculture Datasets\nhttps://www.farmer.in/datasets/\n\nIncludes:\n- India MSP\n- APMC Mandi Directory\n- Crop Modal Price Dataset\n\nUseful as an additional market/reference source.\n\n\n==============================\n10. FUTURE PHASE \342\200\224 DISEASE DETECTION\n==============================\n\n23. PlantVillage Dataset\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/datasets/emmarex/plantdisease\n\n24. PlantDoc Dataset\nhttps://github.com/pratikkayal/PlantDoc-Dataset\n\nIMPORTANT:\nDisease detection is Phase 3.\nDO NOT build/train this CNN for the current MVP unless everything else is already working.\n\n\n==============================\n11. DATABASE / BACKEND\n==============================\n\n25. Supabase\nhttps://supabase.com/\n\n26. Supabase PostgreSQL Documentation\nhttps://supabase.com/docs/guides/database/overview\n\n27. Supabase Auth\nhttps://supabase.com/docs/guides/auth\n\n28. Supabase Storage\nhttps://supabase.com/docs/guides/storage\n\n\n==============================\n12. ML / OPTIMIZATION\n==============================\n\n29. XGBoost\nhttps://xgboost.readthedocs.io/\n\n30. Prophet\nhttps://facebook.github.io/prophet/\n\n31. Google OR-Tools\nhttps://developers.google.com/optimization/\n\n\n==============================\n13. AI\n==============================\n\n32. Google Gemini API\nhttps://ai.google.dev/\n\nGemini should be used for:\n- Natural language\n- Explanation\n- Agent orchestration\n- Farmer queries\n- Regional-language responses\n\nGemini MUST NOT be the authoritative source for:\n- Weather\n- Soil\n- Nutrition\n- Mandi prices\n- Yield\n- Optimization calculations\n\nYou are a senior full-stack engineer, ML engineer, and DevOps engineer. I want you to take my existing project and turn it into a **fully functional, production-ready web application**.\n\n### 1. Complete the Existing Application\n\n* First, inspect the entire existing codebase and understand the current architecture, features, frontend, backend, database, and ML pipeline.\n* Do **not** unnecessarily rewrite working parts of the application.\n* Fix all bugs, broken functionality, incomplete features, API issues, UI issues, and integration problems you find.\n* Make sure every major feature actually works end-to-end rather than being a UI mockup.\n* Remove placeholder/demo functionality where real implementation is possible.\n\n### 2. ML Model & Dataset\n\n* Inspect the existing ML model and dataset carefully.\n* Evaluate whether the current dataset is sufficient for the application\'s intended predictions/classifications.\n* If the dataset is too small, low-quality, biased, outdated, poorly labeled, or unsuitable, **replace or improve it with a better publicly available dataset**.\n* Prefer reliable datasets from sources such as Kaggle, government/open-data portals, research datasets, or other trustworthy sources.\n* Clearly document the dataset source and preprocessing steps.\n* Retrain/rebuild the ML model if necessary.\n* Evaluate the model using appropriate metrics and make sure the final model is actually usable by the application.\n* Integrate the trained model properly with the backend/API.\n* Do not fabricate ML performance metrics or pretend that a model works if it has not been tested.\n\n### 3. Authentication & Supabase\n\nImplement **proper production-ready authentication using Supabase**.\n\nRequirements:\n\n* Supabase must be the authentication/database backend where appropriate.\n* Implement:\n\n  * Sign up\n  * Login\n  * Logout\n  * Session persistence\n  * Protected routes/pages\n  * Authentication state handling\n  * Password reset if appropriate\n  * Proper error handling\n* Store user-specific application data in Supabase where required.\n* Use Supabase Row Level Security (RLS) where applicable so users cannot access another user\'s private data.\n* Never expose Supabase service-role keys or other secrets in frontend code.\n* Use environment variables for all credentials/API keys.\n* Make sure authentication works both locally and after deployment.\n\n### 4. Frontend\n\nMake the frontend polished and production-ready:\n\n* Responsive on desktop, tablet, and mobile.\n* Proper loading states.\n* Proper error states.\n* Form validation.\n* Empty states.\n* Clean navigation.\n* Consistent UI/UX.\n* No broken buttons or dead links.\n* No console errors.\n* Make sure authenticated and unauthenticated users see the correct pages.\n\n### 5. Backend\n\nMake sure the backend is fully functional and production-ready:\n\n* All APIs should work correctly.\n* Validate incoming requests.\n* Handle errors properly.\n* Add appropriate CORS configuration.\n* Ensure the ML inference endpoint works reliably.\n* Do not hardcode localhost URLs.\n* Use environment variables for frontend/backend URLs and secrets.\n\n### 6. Deployment Architecture\n\nDeploy the application using:\n\n**Frontend \342\206\222 Vercel**\n\n**Backend/API/ML service \342\206\222 Railway**\n\n**Authentication + Database \342\206\222 Supabase**\n\nConfigure everything so the deployed application communicates correctly:\n\nUser\n\342\206\223\nVercel Frontend\n\342\206\223\nRailway Backend/API\n\342\206\223\nML Model\n\342\206\223\nSupabase Database/Auth\n\nMake sure:\n\n* Vercel deployment works.\n* Railway deployment works.\n* Frontend correctly calls the Railway backend.\n* Railway correctly communicates with Supabase.\n* Supabase authentication works with the deployed Vercel domain.\n* CORS is configured correctly for the Vercel production URL.\n* Environment variables are configured correctly on both Vercel and Railway.\n* Production URLs are used instead of localhost.\n* No secrets are committed to GitHub.\n\n### 7. Deployment Configuration\n\nCreate/fix all necessary deployment files and configuration, such as:\n\n* `vercel.json` if required\n* `railway.json` / Railway configuration if required\n* `Dockerfile` if useful/necessary\n* `requirements.txt` / `package.json`\n* Proper start commands\n* Build commands\n* Environment variable configuration documentation\n\nMake the project deployable with minimal manual configuration.\n\n### 8. Testing\n\nBefore considering the project complete, test the complete user flow:\n\n1. User opens the deployed Vercel application.\n2. User creates an account.\n3. User logs in.\n4. User accesses the protected dashboard.\n5. User enters real application data.\n6. Frontend sends the request to Railway.\n7. Backend processes the request.\n8. ML model generates the prediction/result.\n9. Result is returned to the frontend.\n10. Relevant user data is stored/retrieved from Supabase.\n11. User logs out.\n12. Protected pages are no longer accessible.\n13. User logs back in and their data/session works correctly.\n\nAlso test invalid inputs, API failures, authentication failures, and missing data.\n\n### 9. Security\n\nFollow reasonable production security practices:\n\n* Never expose secrets.\n* Use `.env` locally and platform environment variables in production.\n* Add `.env` to `.gitignore`.\n* Never commit API keys, passwords, service-role keys, or private credentials.\n* Validate user input.\n* Implement Supabase RLS where necessary.\n* Restrict backend CORS to the correct frontend origin in production.\n* Do not trust client-side authorization alone.\n\n### 10. Final Deliverables\n\nAt the end, provide:\n\n1. What you changed.\n2. Final project architecture.\n3. ML model and dataset used.\n4. Dataset source.\n5. ML evaluation results.\n6. Supabase setup/configuration.\n7. Required environment variables.\n8. Vercel deployment instructions.\n9. Railway deployment instructions.\n10. Any database tables/RLS policies created.\n11. Test results.\n12. Final production URLs for:\n\n* Frontend/Vercel\n* Backend/Railway\n\n13. Any remaining limitations or known issues.\n\n### Important Instructions\n\n* **Do not stop at giving me instructions or code snippets. Actually implement the changes in the project.**\n* **Do not leave features as placeholders.**\n* **Do not use fake authentication.**\n* **Do not use mock ML predictions when a real model can be integrated.**\n* **Do not fabricate datasets, metrics, or test results.**\n* If the existing architecture needs changes to make the application production-ready, make those changes.\n* Preserve existing functionality wherever possible.\n* Prioritize a working end-to-end application over unnecessary complexity.\n* Before finishing, verify that the application can actually be deployed and used by a real user.\n* If you encounter a problem that prevents deployment, diagnose it, fix it, and continue rather than simply reporting the problem.\n\nExample:\n\nWeatherProvider\n    OpenMeteoWeatherProvider\n\nMarketProvider\n    DataGovAgmarknetProvider\n    LocalHistoricalMarketProvider\n\nSoilProvider\n    SoilGridsProvider\n    FarmerEnteredSoilProvider\n\nThis allows the application to work even if one external service temporarily fails.\n\n==================================================\n14. DATA PROVENANCE\n==================================================\n\nCreate a data_sources table.\n\nFor every external dataset store:\n\nname\nsource_url\nprovider\nretrieved_at\nlicense\ndescription\nversion\nlocal_file\nstatus\n\nExamples:\n\nAGMARKNET / Data.gov.in\nOpen-Meteo\nISRIC SoilGrids\nIFCT 2017\nKaggle historical mandi dataset\nKaggle crop yield dataset\n\nThe UI should show a small:\n\"Data sources\"\nsection.\n\nDo not hide provenance.\n\n==================================================\n15. OFFLINE-FIRST REQUIREMENT\n==================================================\n\nThe presentation explicitly requires an offline-first farmer experience.\n\nImplement a practical prototype version.\n\nFlutter should cache:\n\nfarmer profile\nfarm profile\nlatest crop plan\nplanting calendar\nlast known weather result\ninventory\nlast recommendations\n\nWhen internet is unavailable:\n\nshow cached data with:\n\"Last synced: <timestamp>\"\n\nDo not pretend cached information is real-time.\n\nWhen connectivity returns:\nsync with backend.\n\n==================================================\n16. FARMER INPUT FLOW\n==================================================\n\nCreate an easy demo flow.\n\nStep 1:\nFarmer registration\n\nStep 2:\nHousehold:\n\nfamily size\nage groups\ndiet preference\n\nStep 3:\nFarm:\n\nland size\nlocation\nwater availability\nsoil information\nbudget\n\nStep 4:\nCrop history\n\nStep 5:\nGenerate AI crop plan\n\nStep 6:\nShow nutrition coverage\n\nStep 7:\nShow planting calendar\n\nStep 8:\nShow climate alerts\n\nStep 9:\nRecord harvest\n\nStep 10:\nRun harvest allocation\n\nStep 11:\nShow family consumption vs surplus\n\nStep 12:\nShow mandi prices\n\nStep 13:\nShow market forecast\n\nStep 14:\nShow selling recommendation\n\nStep 15:\nShow expected income\n\n==================================================\n17. DASHBOARD\n==================================================\n\nCreate a polished hackathon-quality dashboard.\n\nMain cards:\n\nNutrition Coverage\nExpected Yield\nMarketable Surplus\nExpected Income\nClimate Risk\nSustainability Score\n\nSections:\n\nToday\'s Advisory\nCrop Plan\nNutrition Gaps\nWeather\nMandi Prices\nHarvest Inventory\nSell/Store Recommendation\n\nUse charts where they genuinely communicate data.\n\nDo not create decorative charts containing invented numbers.\n\nEvery chart must be backed by application data.\n\n==================================================\n18. DEMO MODE\n==================================================\n\nCreate a DEMO FARM profile.\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\nThe demo farm must use either:\n\nA. real data from configured sources\n\nOR\n\nB. clearly labelled demo/test values.\n\nNever make test values look like measured field results.\n\nAdd:\n\n\"Demo Scenario\"\nor\n\"Illustrative Scenario\"\n\nwhere appropriate.\n\nDo not claim:\n\"field validated\"\n\"scientifically proven\"\n\"40% reduction\"\n\"35% income increase\"\netc.\n\nThe PPT explicitly labels those impact numbers as target/expected/potential and not experimentally validated.\n\nDo not present them as achieved results.\n\n==================================================\n19. FEATURES FROM PPT\n==================================================\n\nImplement the following core features where feasible:\n\nNutrition:\n- household nutrient scoring\n- micronutrient gap analysis\n- personalized crop-diet match\n- meal suggestions where data supports them\n\nClimate:\n- hyperlocal weather\n- irrigation scheduling\n- climate alerts\n- heat-safety alerts\n\nMarket:\n- mandi price feed\n- historical price trend\n- price forecasting\n- surplus allocation\n- selling-time recommendation\n\nFarmer tools:\n- offline-first app\n- planting calendar\n- inventory\n- voice/regional language architecture\n\nPlatform:\n- multi-agent architecture\n- role-aware backend\n- secure authentication\n- cloud sync\n\n==================================================\n20. FEATURES NOT REQUIRED FOR THIS MVP\n==================================================\n\nDo NOT waste development time implementing:\n\nDisease vision model\nIoT sensors\nhardware integration\nnational-scale infrastructure\ncomplex satellite imagery\nadvanced computer vision\n\nThese are Phase 3/4/5 roadmap items.\n\nThe disease detection vision model is future scope.\n\n==================================================\n21. VOICE\n==================================================\n\nIf implementing voice, make it modular.\n\nDo not build an enormous speech system.\n\nArchitecture:\n\nVoice input\n\342\206\222 speech-to-text\n\342\206\222 structured farmer query\n\342\206\222 backend/Gemini\n\342\206\222 response\n\342\206\222 optional text-to-speech\n\nIf reliable regional-language speech cannot be implemented within the current environment, keep the UI architecture ready but do not fake it.\n\n==================================================\n22. SECURITY\n==================================================\n\nNever place:\n\nGemini API key\nSupabase service role key\nother secrets\n\ninside Flutter source code.\n\nUse environment variables.\n\nProvide:\n\n.env.example\n\nNever commit real secrets.\n\n==================================================\n23. PROJECT STRUCTURE\n==================================================\n\nUse a clean structure similar to:\n\n/\n  frontend/\n    flutter_app/\n\n  backend/\n    app/\n      api/\n      agents/\n      models/\n      services/\n      optimization/\n      ml/\n      providers/\n      schemas/\n      database/\n\n  ml/\n    training/\n    models/\n    evaluation/\n\n  data/\n    raw/\n    processed/\n    README.md\n\n  docs/\n    architecture.md\n    data-sources.md\n    model-card.md\n\n  scripts/\n\n  tests/\n\n  .env.example\n\n  README.md\n\nDo not add unnecessary frameworks.\n\n==================================================\n24. MACHINE LEARNING PIPELINE\n==================================================\n\nCreate reproducible training scripts.\n\nYield:\n\nml/training/train_yield_model.py\n\nMarket:\n\nml/training/train_market_forecast.py\n\nEvaluation:\n\nml/training/evaluate_models.py\n\nSave model metadata.\n\nDo not silently train during application startup.\n\nTraining should be an explicit reproducible process.\n\nIf model artifacts are unavailable, application should fail gracefully and explain that the model needs to be trained/configured.\n\n==================================================\n25. MODEL CARDS\n==================================================\n\nCreate model documentation.\n\nFor XGBoost:\n\npurpose\ndataset\nfeatures\ntarget\nsplit strategy\nmetrics\nlimitations\n\nFor Prophet:\n\npurpose\ndataset\ntime series definition\nforecast horizon\nvalidation method\nmetrics\nlimitations\n\nClearly explain:\n\nThese models are decision-support tools, not guarantees.\n\n==================================================\n26. TESTING\n==================================================\n\nBefore declaring completion:\n\nRun backend tests.\n\nRun frontend tests.\n\nRun ML tests.\n\nTest:\n\nnutrition calculation\ncoverage calculation\nyield prediction interface\nmarket forecast interface\nOR-Tools feasibility\nharvest allocation\nAPI failure\nmissing API key\noffline mode\ndatabase failure\ninvalid farmer input\n\nTest that:\n\nfamily consumption never exceeds available harvest.\n\nmarket + family + storage + reserves never exceeds expected harvest.\n\nnutrition minimum constraints are respected when feasible.\n\nland constraints are respected.\n\nbudget constraints are respected.\n\nwater constraints are respected.\n\n==================================================\n27. ANTI-HALLUCINATION QA\n==================================================\n\nCreate tests specifically for hallucination prevention.\n\nExamples:\n\nIf weather API returns no data:\nsystem must not invent weather.\n\nIf market API returns no price:\nsystem must not invent a price.\n\nIf soil API returns no soil:\nsystem must not invent soil.\n\nIf nutrition database has no food:\nsystem must say data unavailable.\n\nIf XGBoost model isn\'t trained:\nsystem must not claim prediction.\n\nIf Prophet has insufficient history:\nsystem must not claim a reliable forecast.\n\nIf OR-Tools finds no feasible solution:\nsystem must not manufacture one.\n\n==================================================\n28. UI LABELING\n==================================================\n\nDistinguish clearly:\n\nLIVE\nFORECAST\nHISTORICAL\nMODEL PREDICTION\nILLUSTRATIVE\nDEMO\nCACHED\nUNAVAILABLE\n\nExample:\n\n\"\342\202\27134/kg \342\200\224 Latest observed mandi modal price\"\n\nnot:\n\n\"AI knows the price is \342\202\27134 tomorrow.\"\n\nExample:\n\n\"\342\202\27137/kg \342\200\224 Prophet forecast\"\n\nnot:\n\n\"Expected guaranteed selling price.\"\n\n==================================================\n29. SOURCES\n==================================================\n\nPreferred sources:\n\n1. Government of India Data.gov.in / AGMARKNET\n2. Open-Meteo\n3. ISRIC SoilGrids\n4. ICMR-NIN IFCT 2017\n5. Publicly documented Kaggle datasets where necessary\n\nCandidate historical datasets:\n\nDaily Wholesale Commodity Prices \342\200\223 India Mandis\n\nAgricultural Crop Yield in Indian States Dataset\n\nIndian crop yield + weather datasets\n\nCrop recommendation datasets\n\nUse real source provenance.\n\n==================================================\n30. DO NOT OVERBUILD\n==================================================\n\nThis is a hackathon prototype.\n\nPrioritize:\n\nWORKING END-TO-END FLOW\n\nover\n\nhuge number of unfinished features.\n\nThe minimum successful pipeline is:\n\nFarmer Inputs\n\342\206\222 Nutrition Requirements\n\342\206\222 Candidate Crops\n\342\206\222 XGBoost Yield\n\342\206\222 Weather\n\342\206\222 Market Prices\n\342\206\222 Prophet Forecast\n\342\206\222 OR-Tools Allocation\n\342\206\222 Nutrition Coverage\n\342\206\222 Surplus\n\342\206\222 Sell/Store Recommendation\n\342\206\222 Income Estimate\n\nThis pipeline must actually work.\n\n==================================================\n31. DEMO VIDEO REQUIREMENT\n==================================================\n\nBuild the application so a 3\342\200\2235 minute demo can clearly demonstrate:\n\n1. Farmer registers.\n2. Household nutrition profile is entered.\n3. Farm/location/soil/land data is entered.\n4. System generates crop plan.\n5. Nutrition coverage appears.\n6. Planting calendar appears.\n7. Weather alert appears.\n8. Farmer records harvest.\n9. OR-Tools allocates harvest.\n10. Mandi price appears.\n11. Prophet forecast appears.\n12. Finance recommendation appears.\n13. Dashboard shows final impact.\n\nAvoid screens that require manual developer intervention.\n\n==================================================\n32. ERROR HANDLING\n==================================================\n\nEvery external dependency must have graceful failure.\n\nNo blank screen.\n\nNo uncaught exception.\n\nNo fake fallback data.\n\nExample:\n\n\"Live mandi data unavailable.\nShowing the latest locally cached verified dataset from <date>.\"\n\nThat is acceptable.\n\nThis is NOT acceptable:\n\n\"\342\202\27142/kg\"\n\nwhen no source exists.\n\n==================================================\n33. README\n==================================================\n\nCreate a professional README containing:\n\nProject overview\nArchitecture\nFeatures\nTech stack\nDatabase schema\nAgents\nML models\nDatasets\nData provenance\nAPI setup\nEnvironment variables\nLocal setup\nTraining instructions\nTesting\nDemo flow\nLimitations\nFuture roadmap\nCredits\n\nClearly distinguish:\n\nimplemented\nprototype\nfuture scope\n\n==================================================\n34. FINAL VERIFICATION\n==================================================\n\nAfter implementation:\n\n1. Install dependencies.\n2. Run backend.\n3. Run frontend.\n4. Run database migrations.\n5. Train models if datasets are actually available.\n6. Run tests.\n7. Open the application.\n8. Exercise the complete demo flow.\n9. Fix all runtime errors.\n10. Verify API failure handling.\n11. Verify no secrets are exposed.\n12. Verify no fabricated datasets exist.\n13. Verify no hardcoded fake market prices exist.\n14. Verify no hardcoded fake weather exists.\n15. Verify no fabricated ML accuracy exists.\n16. Verify README matches actual implementation.\n\nOnly after all of this should you report completion.\n\n==================================================\n35. FINAL RESPONSE FROM YOU\n==================================================\n\nWhen finished, report ONLY:\n\n1. What was actually implemented.\n2. Which datasets were actually used.\n3. Which APIs are actually connected.\n4. Which ML models were actually trained.\n5. Actual evaluation metrics.\n6. Which parts remain unavailable because of missing credentials/data.\n7. How to run the application.\n8. Demo flow.\n9. Any remaining bugs.\n\nDo not claim anything that cannot be verified from the workspace.\n\nMOST IMPORTANT RULE:\n\nREAL DATA > FABRICATED DATA\n\nREAL CALCULATION > LLM GUESS\n\nREAL MODEL > CLAIMED MODEL\n\nREAL API RESPONSE > INVENTED API RESPONSE\n\nTRANSPARENT LIMITATION > HALLUCINATION\n\nBuild the working NutriHarvest AI prototype now."
    }
  }
}
