  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Environment Configuration**\n\nI\'m currently investigating the application\'s environment configuration to determine how Supabase is set up. I\'m checking for the existence and values of the `.env` file, and `VITE_SUPABASE_URL`, to see if Supabase is being mocked or used in production. I\'ve also pivoted to confirm whether the configuration is defined. I\'m also checking `process.env` and `import.meta`.\n\n\n**Tracing Configuration Paths**\n\nI\'m now tracing the branches of execution based on `isConfigured()`. I\'ve examined how `signIn` calls Supabase auth. I\'ve realized that even when Supabase returns an error for an invalid email/password combination, I need to consider both `true` and `false` states. If the `email` has never been signed up, I wonder how this impacts things. I\'m focusing on the user\'s login flow and error handling.\n\n\n**Uncovering Auth Bypass**\n\nI\'ve just uncovered a significant flaw: the `signIn` function returns a mocked user with a hardcoded ID when Supabase isn\'t configured, or even in mock mode. Critically, this bypasses authentication, even if the email wasn\'t signed up! This behavior occurs whether or not the environment is configured, and allows anyone to bypass the intended authentication mechanism. It\'s a fundamental security concern, as even invalid emails were resulting in \"successful\" logins.\n\n\n**Addressing Login Flaws**\n\nI\'m now focusing on restoring saved user data on re-login, which is not functioning correctly. I\'m especially concerned that *any* email allows access, even when the user never signed up. This violates the strict authentication flow. My focus now is enforcing Supabase Auth as the source of truth, specifically verifying the authentication response after Supabase\'s login/sign-in method, to ensure only registered users can proceed. I also have to verify that the profile is created.\n\n\n**Enforcing Strict Authentication**\n\nI\'m now implementing strict authentication to safeguard protected routes. I\'m ensuring navigation to the dashboard only occurs with a valid, authenticated session. Failure redirects the user, with clear messaging. I\'m also addressing profile restoration after successful authentication, using the Supabase `user.id` to fetch the user profile. I\'m designing the logic for complete/incomplete profiles to manage onboarding.\n\n\n**Verifying Initialization & Fallback**\n\nI\'m now investigating how the Supabase client initializes, especially within `SupabaseService.ts`. I\'m comparing the behavior of a placeholder URL versus a real one. I\'m also looking into how a local storage-based user registry behaves in fallback/demo mode or with a real Supabase client. I need to verify whether Supabase is provisioned and if the client uses a placeholder/local mock or real Supabase, or if Firebase is used instead.\n\n\n**Confirming Config Sources**\n\nI\'m now confirming the source of truth for configuration. I see `import.meta.env` is used to define `supabaseUrl` and `supabaseAnonKey` in `supabase_service.ts`. Also, I\'m verifying the `isConfigured()` function logic, specifically the condition checking for placeholder URLs. I need to understand how local mock auth is handled when `isConfigured()` returns false, especially for sign-up, sign-in, and profile fetching in this mode. I see that sign up uses localStorage.\n\n\n**Identifying User Persistence Issues**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a critical failure: The mocked `signIn` function generates a hardcoded ID, regardless of existing user data or even sign-up status. This means a logged-in user can be assigned a different ID (`usr_demo_101`) than their real one. This leads to broken profile restoration and authentication. I\'m focusing on ensuring consistent user identification. This is especially true for registered users. Any sign-in creates a new `usr_demo_101` profile, regardless.\n\n\n**Validating Auth Scenarios**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a critical aspect: robustly validating authentication across different scenarios. I\'m carefully analyzing the behavior in cases where Supabase IS configured, where `signUp` uses `supabase.auth.signUp()`, and `signIn` utilizes `supabase.auth.signInWithPassword()`. I\'m closely scrutinizing error handling within Supabase, especially when dealing with incorrect email/password combinations. I\'m also ensuring all auth-related methods function correctly with and without Supabase client initialization.\n\n\n"
