steps {
  type: CORTEX_STEP_TYPE_USER_INPUT
  status: CORTEX_STEP_STATUS_DONE
  metadata {
    created_at {
      seconds: 1786464927
      nanos: 158139429
    }
    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "I want you to significantly improve the application\'s data and recommendation system.\n\nThe most important requirement is:\n\n> **NEVER fabricate, assume, randomly generate, or hardcode real-world values and present them as actual data.**\n\nEvery recommendation, calculation, prediction, nutrition value, climate value, irrigation recommendation, and mandi price must be based on either:\n\n1. Data explicitly provided by the user,\n2. A verified dataset that I provide,\n3. A properly trained ML model using an appropriate dataset,\n4. Or a live API/source.\n\nIf the required data is unavailable, clearly tell the user that the data is unavailable instead of making up a number.\n\n---\n\n# 1. USER-SPECIFIC DATA MUST DRIVE THE APP\n\nThe application should work on an individual farmer/user basis.\n\nUse the information collected during onboarding, such as:\n\n* Location\n* Farm size\n* Soil type\n* Soil properties if available\n* Water availability\n* Irrigation method\n* Existing crops\n* Household size\n* Household age groups\n* Dietary preferences\n* Current food production\n* Historical yields\n* Previous crops\n* Sowing date\n* Harvest date\n* Available resources\n* Any other information already collected by the application\n\nEvery recommendation should be calculated using the user\'s actual information.\n\nDo NOT provide generic recommendations when enough user-specific information is available.\n\n---\n\n# 2. NUTRITION MUST BE USER-SPECIFIC\n\nThe nutrition system must NOT display made-up or generic nutrition numbers.\n\nCalculate nutritional requirements based on the actual household information provided by the user.\n\nFor example, consider:\n\n* Number of people\n* Age groups\n* Relevant dietary preferences\n* Food requirements\n* Current food production\n* Crops being grown\n* Expected/actual yield\n* Nutritional composition from a trusted dataset\n\nThen calculate:\n\n* Estimated household nutritional requirements\n* Nutrients currently being produced\n* Nutritional gaps\n* Which crops could help fill those gaps\n* Approximate quantity of each crop needed\n* Whether the farmer\'s current production is sufficient\n\nClearly distinguish between:\n\n**Required nutrition \342\206\222 Current available production \342\206\222 Nutritional gap \342\206\222 Recommended crops**\n\nDo not invent nutritional values.\n\nIf nutritional composition data is required, use a reliable dataset/database.\n\nIf I provide a Kaggle dataset, integrate that dataset into the application/model instead of inventing replacement data.\n\n---\n\n# 3. CROP RECOMMENDATION SYSTEM\n\nBuild a recommendation system that answers:\n\n> \"What should this farmer grow in this particular area to improve household nutrition while considering the farm\'s actual conditions?\"\n\nThe recommendation should consider as many verified inputs as are available, including:\n\n* Geographic location\n* Soil characteristics\n* Climate\n* Temperature\n* Rainfall\n* Water availability\n* Irrigation availability\n* Farm size\n* Current crops\n* Crop rotation considerations\n* Growing season\n* Historical yield\n* Household nutritional deficiencies\n* Crop nutritional composition\n* Expected yield\n* Farmer\'s resources\n\nThe system should rank suitable crops and explain WHY each crop was recommended.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nRecommended crop: Crop X\n\nReasons:\n\342\234\223 Suitable for your soil\n\342\234\223 Suitable for your current climate\n\342\234\223 Fits your available water\n\342\234\223 Can be grown during the current season\n\342\234\223 Helps address your household\'s nutritional gap\n\342\234\223 Expected yield is suitable for your farm size\n```\n\nDo NOT recommend a crop simply because it is generally popular.\n\nThe recommendation must be based on the user\'s actual location and available data.\n\n---\n\n# 4. ML MODEL / DATASET\n\nIf machine learning is required for:\n\n* Crop recommendation\n* Yield prediction\n* Irrigation prediction\n* Nutrition optimization\n* Risk prediction\n\nuse an actual trained model.\n\nI may provide a Kaggle dataset or another dataset link.\n\nWhen I provide a dataset:\n\n1. Inspect the dataset.\n2. Understand its columns and meaning.\n3. Clean and preprocess it.\n4. Identify appropriate features and target variables.\n5. Train an appropriate model.\n6. Validate/test the model.\n7. Report appropriate evaluation metrics.\n8. Integrate the trained model into the application.\n9. Do not pretend the model is accurate if validation shows otherwise.\n\nDo not claim predictions are guaranteed.\n\nFor example, display:\n\n```text\nPredicted yield: 3.2 tonnes\nModel confidence / expected error: ...\nBased on: soil + location + crop + weather + farm size + historical information\n```\n\nUse appropriate uncertainty/error information whenever possible.\n\nIf a suitable dataset is not available, tell me what dataset is required rather than generating fake training data.\n\n---\n\n# 5. YIELD PREDICTION + ACTUAL YIELD TRACKING\n\nThe application should not only predict yield.\n\nIt should also TRACK the farmer\'s actual yield over time.\n\nBefore harvest:\n\n```text\nPredicted yield\n\342\206\223\nExpected harvest\n```\n\nAfter harvest, the farmer enters:\n\n```text\nActual yield\n```\n\nThe application should store:\n\n* Predicted yield\n* Actual yield\n* Crop\n* Farm/plot\n* Sowing date\n* Harvest date\n* Location\n* Relevant environmental conditions\n* Irrigation information\n* Other available inputs\n\nThen calculate:\n\n```text\nPrediction error\n= Actual yield - Predicted yield\n```\n\nand show:\n\n* Predicted vs actual yield\n* Historical yield\n* Yield trends\n* Prediction accuracy over time\n\nIf enough historical data becomes available, use the farmer\'s actual historical results to improve future predictions/recommendations.\n\nThis should become a **continuous learning/feedback system**, while avoiding unsafe automatic retraining without validation.\n\n---\n\n# 6. DAY-TO-DAY FARM PROGRESS TRACKING\n\nTurn the application into a farm progress tracker, not just a one-time recommendation tool.\n\nThe farmer should be able to record daily/periodic information such as:\n\n* Crop growth stage\n* Irrigation performed\n* Amount of water used\n* Rainfall if known\n* Fertilizer application\n* Pest/disease observations\n* Crop condition\n* Planting progress\n* Harvest progress\n* Actual yield\n* Other farm activities\n\nCreate a timeline/dashboard showing:\n\n```text\nDay 1 \342\206\222 Day 2 \342\206\222 Day 3 \342\206\222 ... \342\206\222 Harvest\n```\n\nThe farmer should be able to see the current status of each crop/plot.\n\n---\n\n# 7. LIVE IRRIGATION RECOMMENDATIONS\n\nIrrigation recommendations must NOT be based on made-up weather values.\n\nUse live/real data through an appropriate weather/climate API.\n\nThe system should consider, where data is available:\n\n* Current temperature\n* Humidity\n* Rainfall\n* Forecast rainfall\n* Wind\n* Evapotranspiration or appropriate water-demand indicators\n* Crop type\n* Crop growth stage\n* Soil type\n* Soil moisture if available\n* Farm area\n* Irrigation method\n* Previous irrigation\n* Water availability\n\nThen provide a practical recommendation such as:\n\n```text\nIrrigation recommendation:\nWater today: X litres / mm\n\nReason:\n- Current soil moisture: ...\n- Recent rainfall: ...\n- Forecast rainfall: ...\n- Crop growth stage: ...\n- Estimated water requirement: ...\n```\n\nIf soil moisture sensors are not available, clearly state that the recommendation is an estimate based on available weather/soil/crop data.\n\nDo NOT pretend to know soil moisture if there is no sensor/data source.\n\n---\n\n# 8. LIVE CLIMATE / WEATHER DATA\n\nClimate/weather information must be LIVE.\n\nDo NOT use:\n\n* Hardcoded temperatures\n* Fake rainfall\n* Static sample weather\n* Randomly generated climate values\n\nIntegrate a reliable weather API.\n\nThe app should dynamically retrieve data based on the farmer\'s actual location.\n\nWeather should update automatically and show:\n\n* Current temperature\n* Humidity\n* Rainfall\n* Wind\n* Forecast\n* Relevant agricultural weather indicators\n\nShow the timestamp/source of the latest update.\n\nIf the API is unavailable:\n\n```text\nLive weather data currently unavailable.\n```\n\nDo NOT replace it with fake data.\n\n---\n\n# 9. LIVE MANDI PRICES\n\nMandi prices must also be based on LIVE/official/current data.\n\nDo NOT hardcode prices such as:\n\n```text\nWheat: \342\202\271X\nRice: \342\202\271Y\n```\n\nunless those values actually come from the selected data source.\n\nIntegrate an appropriate official/current mandi/agricultural market data API or verified data source.\n\nThe system should consider:\n\n* Crop\n* Nearby mandi\n* Location\n* Current price\n* Minimum/maximum/modal price where available\n* Date/time of data\n* Market/mandi name\n\nClearly display:\n\n```text\nPrice: \342\202\271____\nMandi: ______\nLast updated: ______\nSource: ______\n```\n\nIf live mandi data is unavailable, explicitly say so.\n\n---\n\n# 10. RECOMMENDATIONS SHOULD CONNECT EVERYTHING\n\nThe main intelligence of the application should connect:\n\n```text\nUSER PROFILE\n      \342\206\223\nFARM + SOIL + LOCATION\n      \342\206\223\nLIVE WEATHER / CLIMATE\n      \342\206\223\nNUTRITION REQUIREMENTS\n      \342\206\223\nCROP DATABASE / ML MODEL\n      \342\206\223\nCROP RECOMMENDATIONS\n      \342\206\223\nYIELD PREDICTION\n      \342\206\223\nIRRIGATION PLAN\n      \342\206\223\nFARMER DAILY TRACKING\n      \342\206\223\nACTUAL YIELD\n      \342\206\223\nPREDICTION VS ACTUAL\n      \342\206\223\nFUTURE RECOMMENDATIONS\n```\n\nThe goal is to create a **continuous farm decision-support and progress-tracking system**, rather than a static information website.\n\n---\n\n# 11. DATA SOURCE TRANSPARENCY\n\nFor every important piece of externally sourced information, maintain metadata such as:\n\n* Source\n* Timestamp\n* Dataset/API name\n* Last updated time\n* Whether the value is user-provided, model-predicted, or live\n\nClearly distinguish:\n\n\360\237\237\242 User-provided data\n\360\237\224\265 Live API data\n\360\237\237\243 Dataset-derived information\n\360\237\237\240 ML prediction\n\342\232\252 Estimated/calculated value\n\nNever present an ML prediction as an actual measurement.\n\nNever present an estimate as live data.\n\nNever present a generic value as a user\'s actual value.\n\n---\n\n# 12. NO FAKE DATA POLICY\n\nThis is extremely important.\n\nIf the application does not have enough information to make a recommendation, DO NOT make up an answer.\n\nInstead say:\n\n> \"Insufficient data to provide a reliable recommendation. Please provide ______.\"\n\nIf an API fails:\n\n> \"Live data is currently unavailable.\"\n\nIf the model cannot make a reliable prediction:\n\n> \"A reliable prediction cannot currently be generated because required input data is missing.\"\n\nIf a dataset does not contain the required variable:\n\n> \"This dataset does not contain sufficient information for this calculation.\"\n\nNever fill missing real-world values with arbitrary numbers.\n\n---\n\n# 13. DASHBOARD\n\nCreate a dashboard showing the farmer\'s actual progress.\n\nFor example:\n\n### Farm Overview\n\n* Farm size\n* Current crops\n* Current growth stage\n* Water availability\n* Soil information\n\n### Nutrition\n\n* Household requirement\n* Current production\n* Nutritional gap\n* Recommended crops\n\n### Crop Progress\n\n* Sowing date\n* Current growth stage\n* Expected harvest\n* Predicted yield\n* Actual yield\n\n### Irrigation\n\n* Today\'s recommendation\n* Previous irrigation\n* Upcoming rainfall\n* Water usage\n\n### Weather\n\n* Live conditions\n* Forecast\n* Last updated time\n\n### Market\n\n* Current mandi prices\n* Nearby mandis\n* Price trends where reliable historical data exists\n\n### Prediction Accuracy\n\n* Predicted yield\n* Actual yield\n* Prediction error\n* Historical performance\n\n---\n\n# 14. IMPORTANT IMPLEMENTATION REQUIREMENT\n\nBefore implementing any ML model or recommendation algorithm, inspect the existing project architecture and determine:\n\n1. What data is already available?\n2. What data is missing?\n3. Which calculations can be deterministic?\n4. Which require ML?\n5. Which require external APIs?\n6. Which require user input?\n7. Which require a dataset?\n\nDo not create fake implementations just to make the UI appear functional.\n\nIf I need to provide a Kaggle dataset, tell me exactly:\n\n* Which dataset is required\n* Which columns are needed\n* What prediction task it will be used for\n* What preprocessing is required\n* What model you recommend\n* How it will integrate with the application\n\nI will provide the dataset/link when required.\n\nSimilarly, identify which live APIs are required for:\n\n* Weather/climate\n* Mandi prices\n* Any other live agricultural information\n\nBuild the application so these data sources can be replaced/configured through environment variables rather than hardcoded into the frontend.\n\n---\n\n# FINAL GOAL\n\nThe finished application should behave like a **real agricultural decision-support and farm-progress tracking platform**.\n\nIt should answer:\n\n> \"Given THIS farmer, THIS household, THIS farm, THIS location, THIS soil, THIS water availability, THIS crop, CURRENT live weather, CURRENT market conditions, and VERIFIED historical/training data \342\200\224 what is the best action right now?\"\n\nEvery recommendation should be traceable to actual data.\n\n**No fabricated numbers. No fake weather. No fake mandi prices. No generic nutrition values presented as personalized values. No guaranteed ML predictions. No fake confidence scores.**\n\nIf data is unavailable, say that it is unavailable and tell me what is needed to produce a reliable result."
    items {
      text: "I want you to significantly improve the application\'s data and recommendation system.\n\nThe most important requirement is:\n\n> **NEVER fabricate, assume, randomly generate, or hardcode real-world values and present them as actual data.**\n\nEvery recommendation, calculation, prediction, nutrition value, climate value, irrigation recommendation, and mandi price must be based on either:\n\n1. Data explicitly provided by the user,\n2. A verified dataset that I provide,\n3. A properly trained ML model using an appropriate dataset,\n4. Or a live API/source.\n\nIf the required data is unavailable, clearly tell the user that the data is unavailable instead of making up a number.\n\n---\n\n# 1. USER-SPECIFIC DATA MUST DRIVE THE APP\n\nThe application should work on an individual farmer/user basis.\n\nUse the information collected during onboarding, such as:\n\n* Location\n* Farm size\n* Soil type\n* Soil properties if available\n* Water availability\n* Irrigation method\n* Existing crops\n* Household size\n* Household age groups\n* Dietary preferences\n* Current food production\n* Historical yields\n* Previous crops\n* Sowing date\n* Harvest date\n* Available resources\n* Any other information already collected by the application\n\nEvery recommendation should be calculated using the user\'s actual information.\n\nDo NOT provide generic recommendations when enough user-specific information is available.\n\n---\n\n# 2. NUTRITION MUST BE USER-SPECIFIC\n\nThe nutrition system must NOT display made-up or generic nutrition numbers.\n\nCalculate nutritional requirements based on the actual household information provided by the user.\n\nFor example, consider:\n\n* Number of people\n* Age groups\n* Relevant dietary preferences\n* Food requirements\n* Current food production\n* Crops being grown\n* Expected/actual yield\n* Nutritional composition from a trusted dataset\n\nThen calculate:\n\n* Estimated household nutritional requirements\n* Nutrients currently being produced\n* Nutritional gaps\n* Which crops could help fill those gaps\n* Approximate quantity of each crop needed\n* Whether the farmer\'s current production is sufficient\n\nClearly distinguish between:\n\n**Required nutrition \342\206\222 Current available production \342\206\222 Nutritional gap \342\206\222 Recommended crops**\n\nDo not invent nutritional values.\n\nIf nutritional composition data is required, use a reliable dataset/database.\n\nIf I provide a Kaggle dataset, integrate that dataset into the application/model instead of inventing replacement data.\n\n---\n\n# 3. CROP RECOMMENDATION SYSTEM\n\nBuild a recommendation system that answers:\n\n> \"What should this farmer grow in this particular area to improve household nutrition while considering the farm\'s actual conditions?\"\n\nThe recommendation should consider as many verified inputs as are available, including:\n\n* Geographic location\n* Soil characteristics\n* Climate\n* Temperature\n* Rainfall\n* Water availability\n* Irrigation availability\n* Farm size\n* Current crops\n* Crop rotation considerations\n* Growing season\n* Historical yield\n* Household nutritional deficiencies\n* Crop nutritional composition\n* Expected yield\n* Farmer\'s resources\n\nThe system should rank suitable crops and explain WHY each crop was recommended.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nRecommended crop: Crop X\n\nReasons:\n\342\234\223 Suitable for your soil\n\342\234\223 Suitable for your current climate\n\342\234\223 Fits your available water\n\342\234\223 Can be grown during the current season\n\342\234\223 Helps address your household\'s nutritional gap\n\342\234\223 Expected yield is suitable for your farm size\n```\n\nDo NOT recommend a crop simply because it is generally popular.\n\nThe recommendation must be based on the user\'s actual location and available data.\n\n---\n\n# 4. ML MODEL / DATASET\n\nIf machine learning is required for:\n\n* Crop recommendation\n* Yield prediction\n* Irrigation prediction\n* Nutrition optimization\n* Risk prediction\n\nuse an actual trained model.\n\nI may provide a Kaggle dataset or another dataset link.\n\nWhen I provide a dataset:\n\n1. Inspect the dataset.\n2. Understand its columns and meaning.\n3. Clean and preprocess it.\n4. Identify appropriate features and target variables.\n5. Train an appropriate model.\n6. Validate/test the model.\n7. Report appropriate evaluation metrics.\n8. Integrate the trained model into the application.\n9. Do not pretend the model is accurate if validation shows otherwise.\n\nDo not claim predictions are guaranteed.\n\nFor example, display:\n\n```text\nPredicted yield: 3.2 tonnes\nModel confidence / expected error: ...\nBased on: soil + location + crop + weather + farm size + historical information\n```\n\nUse appropriate uncertainty/error information whenever possible.\n\nIf a suitable dataset is not available, tell me what dataset is required rather than generating fake training data.\n\n---\n\n# 5. YIELD PREDICTION + ACTUAL YIELD TRACKING\n\nThe application should not only predict yield.\n\nIt should also TRACK the farmer\'s actual yield over time.\n\nBefore harvest:\n\n```text\nPredicted yield\n\342\206\223\nExpected harvest\n```\n\nAfter harvest, the farmer enters:\n\n```text\nActual yield\n```\n\nThe application should store:\n\n* Predicted yield\n* Actual yield\n* Crop\n* Farm/plot\n* Sowing date\n* Harvest date\n* Location\n* Relevant environmental conditions\n* Irrigation information\n* Other available inputs\n\nThen calculate:\n\n```text\nPrediction error\n= Actual yield - Predicted yield\n```\n\nand show:\n\n* Predicted vs actual yield\n* Historical yield\n* Yield trends\n* Prediction accuracy over time\n\nIf enough historical data becomes available, use the farmer\'s actual historical results to improve future predictions/recommendations.\n\nThis should become a **continuous learning/feedback system**, while avoiding unsafe automatic retraining without validation.\n\n---\n\n# 6. DAY-TO-DAY FARM PROGRESS TRACKING\n\nTurn the application into a farm progress tracker, not just a one-time recommendation tool.\n\nThe farmer should be able to record daily/periodic information such as:\n\n* Crop growth stage\n* Irrigation performed\n* Amount of water used\n* Rainfall if known\n* Fertilizer application\n* Pest/disease observations\n* Crop condition\n* Planting progress\n* Harvest progress\n* Actual yield\n* Other farm activities\n\nCreate a timeline/dashboard showing:\n\n```text\nDay 1 \342\206\222 Day 2 \342\206\222 Day 3 \342\206\222 ... \342\206\222 Harvest\n```\n\nThe farmer should be able to see the current status of each crop/plot.\n\n---\n\n# 7. LIVE IRRIGATION RECOMMENDATIONS\n\nIrrigation recommendations must NOT be based on made-up weather values.\n\nUse live/real data through an appropriate weather/climate API.\n\nThe system should consider, where data is available:\n\n* Current temperature\n* Humidity\n* Rainfall\n* Forecast rainfall\n* Wind\n* Evapotranspiration or appropriate water-demand indicators\n* Crop type\n* Crop growth stage\n* Soil type\n* Soil moisture if available\n* Farm area\n* Irrigation method\n* Previous irrigation\n* Water availability\n\nThen provide a practical recommendation such as:\n\n```text\nIrrigation recommendation:\nWater today: X litres / mm\n\nReason:\n- Current soil moisture: ...\n- Recent rainfall: ...\n- Forecast rainfall: ...\n- Crop growth stage: ...\n- Estimated water requirement: ...\n```\n\nIf soil moisture sensors are not available, clearly state that the recommendation is an estimate based on available weather/soil/crop data.\n\nDo NOT pretend to know soil moisture if there is no sensor/data source.\n\n---\n\n# 8. LIVE CLIMATE / WEATHER DATA\n\nClimate/weather information must be LIVE.\n\nDo NOT use:\n\n* Hardcoded temperatures\n* Fake rainfall\n* Static sample weather\n* Randomly generated climate values\n\nIntegrate a reliable weather API.\n\nThe app should dynamically retrieve data based on the farmer\'s actual location.\n\nWeather should update automatically and show:\n\n* Current temperature\n* Humidity\n* Rainfall\n* Wind\n* Forecast\n* Relevant agricultural weather indicators\n\nShow the timestamp/source of the latest update.\n\nIf the API is unavailable:\n\n```text\nLive weather data currently unavailable.\n```\n\nDo NOT replace it with fake data.\n\n---\n\n# 9. LIVE MANDI PRICES\n\nMandi prices must also be based on LIVE/official/current data.\n\nDo NOT hardcode prices such as:\n\n```text\nWheat: \342\202\271X\nRice: \342\202\271Y\n```\n\nunless those values actually come from the selected data source.\n\nIntegrate an appropriate official/current mandi/agricultural market data API or verified data source.\n\nThe system should consider:\n\n* Crop\n* Nearby mandi\n* Location\n* Current price\n* Minimum/maximum/modal price where available\n* Date/time of data\n* Market/mandi name\n\nClearly display:\n\n```text\nPrice: \342\202\271____\nMandi: ______\nLast updated: ______\nSource: ______\n```\n\nIf live mandi data is unavailable, explicitly say so.\n\n---\n\n# 10. RECOMMENDATIONS SHOULD CONNECT EVERYTHING\n\nThe main intelligence of the application should connect:\n\n```text\nUSER PROFILE\n      \342\206\223\nFARM + SOIL + LOCATION\n      \342\206\223\nLIVE WEATHER / CLIMATE\n      \342\206\223\nNUTRITION REQUIREMENTS\n      \342\206\223\nCROP DATABASE / ML MODEL\n      \342\206\223\nCROP RECOMMENDATIONS\n      \342\206\223\nYIELD PREDICTION\n      \342\206\223\nIRRIGATION PLAN\n      \342\206\223\nFARMER DAILY TRACKING\n      \342\206\223\nACTUAL YIELD\n      \342\206\223\nPREDICTION VS ACTUAL\n      \342\206\223\nFUTURE RECOMMENDATIONS\n```\n\nThe goal is to create a **continuous farm decision-support and progress-tracking system**, rather than a static information website.\n\n---\n\n# 11. DATA SOURCE TRANSPARENCY\n\nFor every important piece of externally sourced information, maintain metadata such as:\n\n* Source\n* Timestamp\n* Dataset/API name\n* Last updated time\n* Whether the value is user-provided, model-predicted, or live\n\nClearly distinguish:\n\n\360\237\237\242 User-provided data\n\360\237\224\265 Live API data\n\360\237\237\243 Dataset-derived information\n\360\237\237\240 ML prediction\n\342\232\252 Estimated/calculated value\n\nNever present an ML prediction as an actual measurement.\n\nNever present an estimate as live data.\n\nNever present a generic value as a user\'s actual value.\n\n---\n\n# 12. NO FAKE DATA POLICY\n\nThis is extremely important.\n\nIf the application does not have enough information to make a recommendation, DO NOT make up an answer.\n\nInstead say:\n\n> \"Insufficient data to provide a reliable recommendation. Please provide ______.\"\n\nIf an API fails:\n\n> \"Live data is currently unavailable.\"\n\nIf the model cannot make a reliable prediction:\n\n> \"A reliable prediction cannot currently be generated because required input data is missing.\"\n\nIf a dataset does not contain the required variable:\n\n> \"This dataset does not contain sufficient information for this calculation.\"\n\nNever fill missing real-world values with arbitrary numbers.\n\n---\n\n# 13. DASHBOARD\n\nCreate a dashboard showing the farmer\'s actual progress.\n\nFor example:\n\n### Farm Overview\n\n* Farm size\n* Current crops\n* Current growth stage\n* Water availability\n* Soil information\n\n### Nutrition\n\n* Household requirement\n* Current production\n* Nutritional gap\n* Recommended crops\n\n### Crop Progress\n\n* Sowing date\n* Current growth stage\n* Expected harvest\n* Predicted yield\n* Actual yield\n\n### Irrigation\n\n* Today\'s recommendation\n* Previous irrigation\n* Upcoming rainfall\n* Water usage\n\n### Weather\n\n* Live conditions\n* Forecast\n* Last updated time\n\n### Market\n\n* Current mandi prices\n* Nearby mandis\n* Price trends where reliable historical data exists\n\n### Prediction Accuracy\n\n* Predicted yield\n* Actual yield\n* Prediction error\n* Historical performance\n\n---\n\n# 14. IMPORTANT IMPLEMENTATION REQUIREMENT\n\nBefore implementing any ML model or recommendation algorithm, inspect the existing project architecture and determine:\n\n1. What data is already available?\n2. What data is missing?\n3. Which calculations can be deterministic?\n4. Which require ML?\n5. Which require external APIs?\n6. Which require user input?\n7. Which require a dataset?\n\nDo not create fake implementations just to make the UI appear functional.\n\nIf I need to provide a Kaggle dataset, tell me exactly:\n\n* Which dataset is required\n* Which columns are needed\n* What prediction task it will be used for\n* What preprocessing is required\n* What model you recommend\n* How it will integrate with the application\n\nI will provide the dataset/link when required.\n\nSimilarly, identify which live APIs are required for:\n\n* Weather/climate\n* Mandi prices\n* Any other live agricultural information\n\nBuild the application so these data sources can be replaced/configured through environment variables rather than hardcoded into the frontend.\n\n---\n\n# FINAL GOAL\n\nThe finished application should behave like a **real agricultural decision-support and farm-progress tracking platform**.\n\nIt should answer:\n\n> \"Given THIS farmer, THIS household, THIS farm, THIS location, THIS soil, THIS water availability, THIS crop, CURRENT live weather, CURRENT market conditions, and VERIFIED historical/training data \342\200\224 what is the best action right now?\"\n\nEvery recommendation should be traceable to actual data.\n\n**No fabricated numbers. No fake weather. No fake mandi prices. No generic nutrition values presented as personalized values. No guaranteed ML predictions. No fake confidence scores.**\n\nIf data is unavailable, say that it is unavailable and tell me what is needed to produce a reliable result."
