steps {
  type: CORTEX_STEP_TYPE_USER_INPUT
  status: CORTEX_STEP_STATUS_DONE
  metadata {
    created_at {
      seconds: 1786467508
      nanos: 649948644
    }
    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "Make the following changes to the existing application. Do not fabricate any agricultural data or modify unrelated features.\n\n## 1. Add District Dropdown\n\nIn the farmer\'s farm information/onboarding section, add a proper **District dropdown**.\n\nThe farmer should select:\n\n**State \342\206\222 District**\n\nThe district list should use a real/static verified list of Indian states and districts, not randomly generated values.\n\nUse the selected district as an input for location-specific recommendations and agricultural data wherever a verified data source supports it.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Add Soil Type Dropdown\n\nAdd a **Soil Type dropdown** so farmers who are not familiar with technical soil parameters can still use the application.\n\nExample options can include commonly recognized soil categories such as:\n\n* Alluvial\n* Black\n* Red\n* Laterite\n* Desert/Arid\n* Mountain/Forest\n* Loamy\n* Sandy\n* Clayey\n* Other\n\nDo not force the farmer to manually enter:\n\n* pH\n* Nitrogen\n* Phosphorus\n* Potassium\n\nunless they actually know these values.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Handle Farmers Who Don\'t Know N/P/K/pH\n\nThis is very important.\n\nMany farmers will not know exact:\n\n* Nitrogen\n* Phosphorus\n* Potassium\n* pH\n\nvalues.\n\nTherefore provide two options:\n\n### Option A \342\200\224 Farmer knows soil test values\n\nAllow the farmer to enter:\n\n```text\npH\nNitrogen\nPhosphorus\nPotassium\n```\n\nMark these as:\n\n**Farmer/Soil-Test Provided**\n\n### Option B \342\200\224 Farmer does not know them\n\nAllow the farmer to select:\n\n```text\nI don\'t know my soil values\n```\n\nThen use:\n\n**District + Soil Type + verified agricultural/soil dataset**\n\nto obtain an appropriate estimate ONLY if a reliable dataset contains that information.\n\nClearly label these values as:\n\n> \"Estimated from location and soil type\"\n\nNOT:\n\n> \"Your actual soil value\"\n\nIf there is no reliable data available for that district/soil combination, do NOT invent values.\n\nInstead display:\n\n> \"Soil nutrient data is unavailable for this location. For more accurate recommendations, enter values from a Soil Health Card or soil test.\"\n\n---\n\n## 4. NEVER INVENT SOIL VALUES\n\nDo NOT do something like:\n\n```text\nDistrict = Noida\nSoil = Alluvial\n\342\206\222 pH = 6.8\n\342\206\222 N = 120\n\342\206\222 P = 45\n```\n\nunless those values actually come from a verified source.\n\nDo not use averages, random values, AI guesses, or hardcoded numbers and present them as actual measurements.\n\nEvery soil value must have a source and status:\n\n```text\nActual soil test\nOR\nVerified dataset estimate\nOR\nUnavailable\n```\n\n---\n\n# 5. Crop Recommendation Model \342\200\224 FIX THE CURRENT R\302\262 = 0 PROBLEM\n\nThe current crop/yield ML section shows:\n\n```text\nR\302\262 = 0\nValidated on = 5 samples\n```\n\nThis is not sufficient to claim that the model is reliable.\n\nDo NOT artificially increase the R\302\262 score.\n\nDo NOT manipulate the test data.\n\nDo NOT fabricate additional training samples.\n\nInstead, improve the model properly.\n\nI can provide a Kaggle dataset for training if required.\n\nYou may also search for suitable publicly available agricultural datasets online, but ONLY use datasets that actually exist and whose contents can be verified.\n\nPotential datasets can include information such as:\n\n* Crop\n* Yield\n* Location/state/district\n* Soil type\n* N\n* P\n* K\n* pH\n* Temperature\n* Rainfall\n* Humidity\n* Area\n* Production\n* Fertilizer\n* Irrigation\n* Other relevant agricultural variables\n\nFor example, publicly available Kaggle datasets exist containing crop recommendation features such as N, P, K, pH, temperature, humidity and rainfall, and datasets combining Indian crop yield, soil and weather information also exist. These should be inspected for suitability rather than blindly used.\n\n---\n\n# 6. PROPER ML TRAINING\n\nBefore training the final model:\n\n1. Inspect the dataset.\n2. Check its source and license.\n3. Check the number of samples.\n4. Check missing values.\n5. Check duplicate records.\n6. Check feature distributions.\n7. Check whether the target variable is appropriate.\n8. Remove data leakage.\n9. Preprocess the data correctly.\n10. Split the data properly into training and testing sets.\n11. Use cross-validation where appropriate.\n12. Train suitable models.\n13. Compare models.\n14. Evaluate using:\n\n* R\302\262\n* MAE\n* RMSE\n\n15. Use a sufficiently large and meaningful test set.\n16. Display the actual number of training and test samples.\n\nDo NOT use only 5 test samples unless the dataset genuinely contains almost no data, in which case clearly state that the model cannot be considered reliable.\n\n---\n\n# 7. IF I PROVIDE A DATASET\n\nIf I provide a Kaggle dataset/link:\n\nDo NOT immediately assume it is suitable.\n\nFirst inspect it and tell me:\n\n```text\nDataset:\nNumber of rows:\nNumber of columns:\nFeatures:\nTarget:\nMissing values:\nPossible leakage:\nSuitable for crop recommendation? YES/NO\nSuitable for yield prediction? YES/NO\n```\n\nThen train the model using the appropriate dataset.\n\nIf one dataset is suitable for crop recommendation but not yield prediction, do NOT use the same dataset for both tasks just for convenience.\n\nUse separate models/datasets if necessary.\n\n---\n\n# 8. DO NOT FABRICATE MODEL METRICS\n\nThe UI must display the actual metrics produced during evaluation.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nR\302\262: 0.73\nMAE: 412 kg/ha\nRMSE: 615 kg/ha\n\nTraining samples: 8,420\nTest samples: 1,054\n```\n\nThese numbers must come directly from the actual evaluation.\n\nNever create fake metrics to make the application look better.\n\nIf R\302\262 remains poor:\n\n```text\nModel performance is currently insufficient for reliable prediction.\n```\n\nThat is preferable to displaying fabricated accuracy.\n\n---\n\n# 9. MODEL SHOULD USE AVAILABLE REAL DATA\n\nThe model should use the strongest available verified inputs.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nDistrict\nSoil Type\nN\nP\nK\npH\nTemperature\nRainfall\nHumidity\nFarm Area\nCrop\nSeason\nIrrigation\nHistorical Yield\n```\n\nHowever, do NOT force unavailable values into the model.\n\nIf the farmer doesn\'t know N/P/K/pH, use only a model that can legitimately work with the available information, or use a verified location/soil-derived estimate.\n\nDo NOT fill missing values with arbitrary numbers.\n\n---\n\n# 10. MAKE THE DATA SOURCE VISIBLE\n\nFor important agricultural inputs, show where the information came from.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nSoil Type:\nFarmer provided\n\npH:\nEstimated from verified dataset\n\nTemperature:\nLive Weather API\n\nRainfall:\nLive Weather API\n\nYield:\nML Prediction\n\nActual Yield:\nFarmer recorded\n```\n\nThis makes the system transparent.\n\n---\n\n# 11. IMPORTANT: NO VITAMIN C FABRICATION\n\nIf the nutrition recommendation system says there is a vitamin C deficiency/gap, do NOT simply generate a random crop recommendation.\n\nUse the actual nutritional composition of candidate crops from a verified nutrition dataset.\n\nThe logic should be:\n\n```text\nFarmer\'s current crops\n        \342\206\223\nActual/Predicted production\n        \342\206\223\nVerified nutrient composition\n        \342\206\223\nNutrients produced\n        \342\206\223\nHousehold requirement\n        \342\206\223\nNutritional gap\n        \342\206\223\nCandidate crops\n        \342\206\223\nCheck whether candidate crop grows suitably\n        \342\206\223\nRecommend suitable crop\n```\n\nFor example, if vitamin C is insufficient, the system should search among crops that:\n\n1. Actually contain meaningful vitamin C according to the nutrition dataset.\n2. Are suitable for the farmer\'s location.\n3. Are suitable for the farmer\'s soil type.\n4. Fit the climate/growing season.\n5. Fit available water.\n6. Fit the farmer\'s farm size.\n\nDo NOT recommend a vitamin-C-rich crop if agricultural conditions indicate it is unsuitable.\n\nIf there is no suitable crop based on available verified data, say:\n\n> \"No suitable crop was identified from the available data.\"\n\n---\n\n# 12. DELETE BUTTONS ARE CURRENTLY NOT WORKING \342\200\224 FIX THEM PROPERLY\n\nThe Journal and Yield History delete buttons currently do not work.\n\nDo a complete investigation of the delete functionality.\n\nFor Journal:\n\n```text\nDelete button\n\342\206\223\nConfirmation\n\342\206\223\nSupabase DELETE operation\n\342\206\223\nDatabase record actually deleted\n\342\206\223\nRefresh/re-fetch journal data\n\342\206\223\nEntry disappears\n```\n\nFor Yield History:\n\n```text\nDelete button\n\342\206\223\nConfirmation\n\342\206\223\nSupabase DELETE operation\n\342\206\223\nDatabase record actually deleted\n\342\206\223\nRefresh/re-fetch yield history\n\342\206\223\nCharts/statistics recalculated\n```\n\nDo NOT simply remove the item from React/frontend state.\n\nThe database record itself must be deleted.\n\n---\n\n# 13. CHECK SUPABASE RLS POLICIES\n\nIf deletion fails because of Supabase Row Level Security:\n\nInspect the existing RLS policies.\n\nThe authenticated user should be able to delete ONLY their own:\n\n* Journal entries\n* Yield history records\n\nUse the authenticated user\'s Supabase `user.id` to enforce ownership.\n\nDo not disable RLS just to make deletion work.\n\n---\n\n# 14. ADD REFRESH BUTTONS\n\nAdd working refresh buttons for dynamically generated or externally retrieved data.\n\nExamples:\n\n```text\nRefresh Recommendations\nRefresh Weather\nRefresh Mandi Prices\nRefresh Nutrition\nRefresh Yield Prediction\nRefresh Dashboard\n```\n\nRefresh must actually:\n\n* Re-fetch API data\n* Re-fetch database data\n* Recalculate derived values\n* Re-run model inference where appropriate\n\nDo NOT just reload the webpage.\n\nShow:\n\n```text\nRefreshing...\n```\n\nthen:\n\n```text\nUpdated just now\n```\n\nwhere appropriate.\n\n---\n\n# 15. REMOVE LOAD DATA / DEMO DATA\n\nCompletely remove:\n\n* Load Data\n* Load Demo Data\n* Load Sample Data\n* Generate Sample Data\n* Any similar demo-data functionality\n\nDo not use fake data when real data is unavailable.\n\nIf there is no data:\n\n> \"No data available yet.\"\n\nIf an API fails:\n\n> \"Live data is currently unavailable. Please try again.\"\n\nDo NOT silently replace unavailable data with random/sample values.\n\n---\n\n# FINAL REQUIREMENT\n\nThe application must follow this principle:\n\n**REAL USER DATA + VERIFIED DATASETS + LIVE APIs + ACTUAL ML PREDICTIONS + TRANSPARENT CALCULATIONS**\n\nNever:\n\n**Random numbers + fabricated datasets + fake ML accuracy + hardcoded agricultural values**\n\nIf the system does not have enough real data to make a reliable recommendation, it must clearly say so instead of guessing.\n\nBefore making these changes, inspect the existing codebase and fix the underlying implementation rather than creating UI-only workarounds."
    items {
      text: "Make the following changes to the existing application. Do not fabricate any agricultural data or modify unrelated features.\n\n## 1. Add District Dropdown\n\nIn the farmer\'s farm information/onboarding section, add a proper **District dropdown**.\n\nThe farmer should select:\n\n**State \342\206\222 District**\n\nThe district list should use a real/static verified list of Indian states and districts, not randomly generated values.\n\nUse the selected district as an input for location-specific recommendations and agricultural data wherever a verified data source supports it.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Add Soil Type Dropdown\n\nAdd a **Soil Type dropdown** so farmers who are not familiar with technical soil parameters can still use the application.\n\nExample options can include commonly recognized soil categories such as:\n\n* Alluvial\n* Black\n* Red\n* Laterite\n* Desert/Arid\n* Mountain/Forest\n* Loamy\n* Sandy\n* Clayey\n* Other\n\nDo not force the farmer to manually enter:\n\n* pH\n* Nitrogen\n* Phosphorus\n* Potassium\n\nunless they actually know these values.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Handle Farmers Who Don\'t Know N/P/K/pH\n\nThis is very important.\n\nMany farmers will not know exact:\n\n* Nitrogen\n* Phosphorus\n* Potassium\n* pH\n\nvalues.\n\nTherefore provide two options:\n\n### Option A \342\200\224 Farmer knows soil test values\n\nAllow the farmer to enter:\n\n```text\npH\nNitrogen\nPhosphorus\nPotassium\n```\n\nMark these as:\n\n**Farmer/Soil-Test Provided**\n\n### Option B \342\200\224 Farmer does not know them\n\nAllow the farmer to select:\n\n```text\nI don\'t know my soil values\n```\n\nThen use:\n\n**District + Soil Type + verified agricultural/soil dataset**\n\nto obtain an appropriate estimate ONLY if a reliable dataset contains that information.\n\nClearly label these values as:\n\n> \"Estimated from location and soil type\"\n\nNOT:\n\n> \"Your actual soil value\"\n\nIf there is no reliable data available for that district/soil combination, do NOT invent values.\n\nInstead display:\n\n> \"Soil nutrient data is unavailable for this location. For more accurate recommendations, enter values from a Soil Health Card or soil test.\"\n\n---\n\n## 4. NEVER INVENT SOIL VALUES\n\nDo NOT do something like:\n\n```text\nDistrict = Noida\nSoil = Alluvial\n\342\206\222 pH = 6.8\n\342\206\222 N = 120\n\342\206\222 P = 45\n```\n\nunless those values actually come from a verified source.\n\nDo not use averages, random values, AI guesses, or hardcoded numbers and present them as actual measurements.\n\nEvery soil value must have a source and status:\n\n```text\nActual soil test\nOR\nVerified dataset estimate\nOR\nUnavailable\n```\n\n---\n\n# 5. Crop Recommendation Model \342\200\224 FIX THE CURRENT R\302\262 = 0 PROBLEM\n\nThe current crop/yield ML section shows:\n\n```text\nR\302\262 = 0\nValidated on = 5 samples\n```\n\nThis is not sufficient to claim that the model is reliable.\n\nDo NOT artificially increase the R\302\262 score.\n\nDo NOT manipulate the test data.\n\nDo NOT fabricate additional training samples.\n\nInstead, improve the model properly.\n\nI can provide a Kaggle dataset for training if required.\n\nYou may also search for suitable publicly available agricultural datasets online, but ONLY use datasets that actually exist and whose contents can be verified.\n\nPotential datasets can include information such as:\n\n* Crop\n* Yield\n* Location/state/district\n* Soil type\n* N\n* P\n* K\n* pH\n* Temperature\n* Rainfall\n* Humidity\n* Area\n* Production\n* Fertilizer\n* Irrigation\n* Other relevant agricultural variables\n\nFor example, publicly available Kaggle datasets exist containing crop recommendation features such as N, P, K, pH, temperature, humidity and rainfall, and datasets combining Indian crop yield, soil and weather information also exist. These should be inspected for suitability rather than blindly used.\n\n---\n\n# 6. PROPER ML TRAINING\n\nBefore training the final model:\n\n1. Inspect the dataset.\n2. Check its source and license.\n3. Check the number of samples.\n4. Check missing values.\n5. Check duplicate records.\n6. Check feature distributions.\n7. Check whether the target variable is appropriate.\n8. Remove data leakage.\n9. Preprocess the data correctly.\n10. Split the data properly into training and testing sets.\n11. Use cross-validation where appropriate.\n12. Train suitable models.\n13. Compare models.\n14. Evaluate using:\n\n* R\302\262\n* MAE\n* RMSE\n\n15. Use a sufficiently large and meaningful test set.\n16. Display the actual number of training and test samples.\n\nDo NOT use only 5 test samples unless the dataset genuinely contains almost no data, in which case clearly state that the model cannot be considered reliable.\n\n---\n\n# 7. IF I PROVIDE A DATASET\n\nIf I provide a Kaggle dataset/link:\n\nDo NOT immediately assume it is suitable.\n\nFirst inspect it and tell me:\n\n```text\nDataset:\nNumber of rows:\nNumber of columns:\nFeatures:\nTarget:\nMissing values:\nPossible leakage:\nSuitable for crop recommendation? YES/NO\nSuitable for yield prediction? YES/NO\n```\n\nThen train the model using the appropriate dataset.\n\nIf one dataset is suitable for crop recommendation but not yield prediction, do NOT use the same dataset for both tasks just for convenience.\n\nUse separate models/datasets if necessary.\n\n---\n\n# 8. DO NOT FABRICATE MODEL METRICS\n\nThe UI must display the actual metrics produced during evaluation.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nR\302\262: 0.73\nMAE: 412 kg/ha\nRMSE: 615 kg/ha\n\nTraining samples: 8,420\nTest samples: 1,054\n```\n\nThese numbers must come directly from the actual evaluation.\n\nNever create fake metrics to make the application look better.\n\nIf R\302\262 remains poor:\n\n```text\nModel performance is currently insufficient for reliable prediction.\n```\n\nThat is preferable to displaying fabricated accuracy.\n\n---\n\n# 9. MODEL SHOULD USE AVAILABLE REAL DATA\n\nThe model should use the strongest available verified inputs.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nDistrict\nSoil Type\nN\nP\nK\npH\nTemperature\nRainfall\nHumidity\nFarm Area\nCrop\nSeason\nIrrigation\nHistorical Yield\n```\n\nHowever, do NOT force unavailable values into the model.\n\nIf the farmer doesn\'t know N/P/K/pH, use only a model that can legitimately work with the available information, or use a verified location/soil-derived estimate.\n\nDo NOT fill missing values with arbitrary numbers.\n\n---\n\n# 10. MAKE THE DATA SOURCE VISIBLE\n\nFor important agricultural inputs, show where the information came from.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nSoil Type:\nFarmer provided\n\npH:\nEstimated from verified dataset\n\nTemperature:\nLive Weather API\n\nRainfall:\nLive Weather API\n\nYield:\nML Prediction\n\nActual Yield:\nFarmer recorded\n```\n\nThis makes the system transparent.\n\n---\n\n# 11. IMPORTANT: NO VITAMIN C FABRICATION\n\nIf the nutrition recommendation system says there is a vitamin C deficiency/gap, do NOT simply generate a random crop recommendation.\n\nUse the actual nutritional composition of candidate crops from a verified nutrition dataset.\n\nThe logic should be:\n\n```text\nFarmer\'s current crops\n        \342\206\223\nActual/Predicted production\n        \342\206\223\nVerified nutrient composition\n        \342\206\223\nNutrients produced\n        \342\206\223\nHousehold requirement\n        \342\206\223\nNutritional gap\n        \342\206\223\nCandidate crops\n        \342\206\223\nCheck whether candidate crop grows suitably\n        \342\206\223\nRecommend suitable crop\n```\n\nFor example, if vitamin C is insufficient, the system should search among crops that:\n\n1. Actually contain meaningful vitamin C according to the nutrition dataset.\n2. Are suitable for the farmer\'s location.\n3. Are suitable for the farmer\'s soil type.\n4. Fit the climate/growing season.\n5. Fit available water.\n6. Fit the farmer\'s farm size.\n\nDo NOT recommend a vitamin-C-rich crop if agricultural conditions indicate it is unsuitable.\n\nIf there is no suitable crop based on available verified data, say:\n\n> \"No suitable crop was identified from the available data.\"\n\n---\n\n# 12. DELETE BUTTONS ARE CURRENTLY NOT WORKING \342\200\224 FIX THEM PROPERLY\n\nThe Journal and Yield History delete buttons currently do not work.\n\nDo a complete investigation of the delete functionality.\n\nFor Journal:\n\n```text\nDelete button\n\342\206\223\nConfirmation\n\342\206\223\nSupabase DELETE operation\n\342\206\223\nDatabase record actually deleted\n\342\206\223\nRefresh/re-fetch journal data\n\342\206\223\nEntry disappears\n```\n\nFor Yield History:\n\n```text\nDelete button\n\342\206\223\nConfirmation\n\342\206\223\nSupabase DELETE operation\n\342\206\223\nDatabase record actually deleted\n\342\206\223\nRefresh/re-fetch yield history\n\342\206\223\nCharts/statistics recalculated\n```\n\nDo NOT simply remove the item from React/frontend state.\n\nThe database record itself must be deleted.\n\n---\n\n# 13. CHECK SUPABASE RLS POLICIES\n\nIf deletion fails because of Supabase Row Level Security:\n\nInspect the existing RLS policies.\n\nThe authenticated user should be able to delete ONLY their own:\n\n* Journal entries\n* Yield history records\n\nUse the authenticated user\'s Supabase `user.id` to enforce ownership.\n\nDo not disable RLS just to make deletion work.\n\n---\n\n# 14. ADD REFRESH BUTTONS\n\nAdd working refresh buttons for dynamically generated or externally retrieved data.\n\nExamples:\n\n```text\nRefresh Recommendations\nRefresh Weather\nRefresh Mandi Prices\nRefresh Nutrition\nRefresh Yield Prediction\nRefresh Dashboard\n```\n\nRefresh must actually:\n\n* Re-fetch API data\n* Re-fetch database data\n* Recalculate derived values\n* Re-run model inference where appropriate\n\nDo NOT just reload the webpage.\n\nShow:\n\n```text\nRefreshing...\n```\n\nthen:\n\n```text\nUpdated just now\n```\n\nwhere appropriate.\n\n---\n\n# 15. REMOVE LOAD DATA / DEMO DATA\n\nCompletely remove:\n\n* Load Data\n* Load Demo Data\n* Load Sample Data\n* Generate Sample Data\n* Any similar demo-data functionality\n\nDo not use fake data when real data is unavailable.\n\nIf there is no data:\n\n> \"No data available yet.\"\n\nIf an API fails:\n\n> \"Live data is currently unavailable. Please try again.\"\n\nDo NOT silently replace unavailable data with random/sample values.\n\n---\n\n# FINAL REQUIREMENT\n\nThe application must follow this principle:\n\n**REAL USER DATA + VERIFIED DATASETS + LIVE APIs + ACTUAL ML PREDICTIONS + TRANSPARENT CALCULATIONS**\n\nNever:\n\n**Random numbers + fabricated datasets + fake ML accuracy + hardcoded agricultural values**\n\nIf the system does not have enough real data to make a reliable recommendation, it must clearly say so instead of guessing.\n\nBefore making these changes, inspect the existing codebase and fix the underlying implementation rather than creating UI-only workarounds."
    }
  }
}
