  user_input {
    user_response: "The technical audit has confirmed that the current crop-yield model is NOT trustworthy.\n\nThe current implementation uses:\n- Synthetic/generated training data\n- pseudoRandom()\n- Hardcoded cropBaseYields\n- A mathematical heuristic instead of a real XGBoost model\n- A formula that matches the synthetic data generator\n- Only 48 test samples\n- No cross-validation\n\nREMOVE THIS ENTIRE SYNTHETIC YIELD MODEL PIPELINE.\n\nDo NOT try to improve the existing R\302\262 = 0.886.\nDo NOT preserve the current metric.\nDo NOT generate synthetic replacement data.\nDo NOT use Math.random(), pseudoRandom(), hardcoded crop yields, or mathematical formulas to create training samples.\n\nI want a REAL crop-yield ML pipeline.\n\n## 1. USE A REAL DATASET\n\nUse a verified real-world crop-yield dataset.\n\nStart by investigating this dataset:\n\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/datasets/akshatgupta7/crop-yield-in-indian-states-dataset\n\nDo NOT assume it is suitable.\n\nFirst inspect:\n- Number of rows\n- Columns\n- Target variable\n- Geographic coverage\n- Crop coverage\n- Years available\n- Missing values\n- Duplicate records\n- Whether yield is an actual observed value\n- Whether production/area can cause target leakage\n\nIf this dataset is unsuitable, find another REAL crop-yield dataset from Kaggle, Government of India, ICAR, data.gov.in, or another verifiable source.\n\nDo not fabricate missing data.\n\n## 2. IMPORTANT DISTINCTION\n\nThe following datasets are primarily CROP RECOMMENDATION datasets:\n\n- Siddharth Sharma Crop Recommendation\n- Atharva Ingle Crop Recommendation\n- Madhura Atmaram Bhagat Crop Recommendation\n- Arka Bhowmik Crop Recommendation\n\nDo NOT use these datasets as crop-yield training data unless the actual dataset contains a legitimate observed yield target.\n\nThey can potentially be used separately for crop recommendation.\n\n## 3. TRAIN A REAL XGBOOST MODEL\n\nUse an actual XGBoost regression model trained on the real dataset.\n\nThe model should have:\n\n- Real training data\n- Real target values\n- Proper preprocessing\n- Appropriate categorical encoding\n- Missing-value handling\n- Train/test split\n- Cross-validation\n- Hyperparameter tuning where appropriate\n- Final held-out test set\n\nThe target should be the actual observed crop yield, preferably kg/ha.\n\n## 4. PREVENT DATA LEAKAGE\n\nBefore training, explicitly inspect every feature.\n\nIf predicting:\n\nYield (kg/ha)\n\nDO NOT use features that directly reveal the answer, such as:\n\n- Production when yield = Production / Area\n- Harvest quantity\n- Actual yield\n- Any post-harvest measurement\n- Any feature mathematically derived from yield\n\nArea may be used only if appropriate and if it does not create leakage for the specific target definition.\n\nDocument which features were removed and why.\n\n## 5. PROPER VALIDATION\n\nDo NOT use only 48 test samples if the dataset allows a larger meaningful test set.\n\nUse an appropriate split based on the dataset.\n\nIf the dataset contains multiple years:\n\nConsider using a temporal holdout where appropriate.\n\nIf there are repeated observations from the same state/district/crop:\n\nCheck whether random splitting causes information leakage.\n\nUse grouped or temporal validation where appropriate.\n\nAlso perform cross-validation on the training data.\n\nReport:\n\n- Number of total samples\n- Training samples\n- Validation samples\n- Test samples\n- Number of CV folds\n\n## 6. REPORT REAL METRICS\n\nCalculate the actual:\n\n- R\302\262\n- MAE\n- RMSE\n\nfrom the real held-out test data.\n\nAlso compare against a simple baseline:\n\nMean predictor\n\nDisplay:\n\nModel R\302\262\nBaseline R\302\262\nModel MAE\nBaseline MAE\nModel RMSE\nBaseline RMSE\n\nDo NOT modify the metrics manually.\n\nDo NOT fabricate metrics.\n\nDo NOT claim accuracy based only on training performance.\n\n## 7. SAVE THE ACTUAL MODEL\n\nThe application must use the actual trained model for prediction.\n\nDo not replace the model with a TypeScript formula.\n\nDo not create:\n\ncropBaseYields\npredictYieldInternal()\nfake prediction multipliers\nsynthetic yield generators\n\nThe prediction displayed to the farmer must come from the trained ML model.\n\n## 8. MAKE DATASET SOURCE TRANSPARENT\n\nIn the application show:\n\nDataset:\n[actual dataset name]\n\nSource:\n[actual source]\n\nTraining samples:\nX\n\nTest samples:\nY\n\nModel:\nXGBoost Regressor\n\nR\302\262:\nX\n\nMAE:\nX kg/ha\n\nRMSE:\nX kg/ha\n\nLast trained:\n[date]\n\nDo not show the previous R\302\262 = 0.886.\n\nOnly display the new metrics after the real model has been trained and evaluated.\n\n## 9. FARMER PREDICTION\n\nAfter training the model, connect it to the farmer\'s real inputs.\n\nUse available real information such as:\n\n- District\n- State\n- Crop\n- Season\n- Farm area\n- Soil information\n- Soil type\n- pH if available\n- Nitrogen if available\n- Phosphorus if available\n- Potassium if available\n- Rainfall\n- Temperature\n- Other features actually supported by the training dataset\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\nDo not send features to the model that were not represented during training.\n\nIf the farmer does not know soil N/P/K/pH, do not invent them.\n\nUse verified SoilGrids/location data if appropriate and available.\n\nOtherwise tell the farmer that the parameter is unavailable.\n\n## 10. NO FAKE FALLBACK\n\nIf the model cannot make a reliable prediction because required information is missing:\n\nDO NOT generate a fake yield.\n\nShow:\n\n\"Insufficient verified data to generate a reliable yield prediction.\"\n\nTell the farmer which information is missing.\n\n## 11. REAL DATA ONLY\n\nThe entire yield pipeline must contain ZERO:\n\n- Synthetic rows\n- Random rows\n- pseudoRandom()\n- Hardcoded crop yield values\n- Fake test samples\n- Fake model metrics\n- Formula-generated targets\n- Fake confidence scores\n\nThe only acceptable sources are:\n\nREAL DATASET\n+\nREAL FARMER INPUT\n+\nREAL LIVE API DATA\n+\nREAL ML MODEL OUTPUT\n+\nLEGITIMATE CALCULATIONS\n\n## 12. BEFORE IMPLEMENTATION\n\nDo not immediately write code.\n\nFirst provide me with an audit of the real dataset you intend to use:\n\nDataset:\nRows:\nColumns:\nTarget:\nFeatures:\nYears:\nStates:\nDistricts:\nMissing values:\nPotential leakage:\nSuitable for yield prediction: YES/NO\nRecommended train/test strategy:\nRecommended model features:\n\nThen wait for confirmation before replacing the current model.\n\nThe current synthetic model and its R\302\262 = 0.886 must be considered INVALID."
    items {
      text: "The technical audit has confirmed that the current crop-yield model is NOT trustworthy.\n\nThe current implementation uses:\n- Synthetic/generated training data\n- pseudoRandom()\n- Hardcoded cropBaseYields\n- A mathematical heuristic instead of a real XGBoost model\n- A formula that matches the synthetic data generator\n- Only 48 test samples\n- No cross-validation\n\nREMOVE THIS ENTIRE SYNTHETIC YIELD MODEL PIPELINE.\n\nDo NOT try to improve the existing R\302\262 = 0.886.\nDo NOT preserve the current metric.\nDo NOT generate synthetic replacement data.\nDo NOT use Math.random(), pseudoRandom(), hardcoded crop yields, or mathematical formulas to create training samples.\n\nI want a REAL crop-yield ML pipeline.\n\n## 1. USE A REAL DATASET\n\nUse a verified real-world crop-yield dataset.\n\nStart by investigating this dataset:\n\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/datasets/akshatgupta7/crop-yield-in-indian-states-dataset\n\nDo NOT assume it is suitable.\n\nFirst inspect:\n- Number of rows\n- Columns\n- Target variable\n- Geographic coverage\n- Crop coverage\n- Years available\n- Missing values\n- Duplicate records\n- Whether yield is an actual observed value\n- Whether production/area can cause target leakage\n\nIf this dataset is unsuitable, find another REAL crop-yield dataset from Kaggle, Government of India, ICAR, data.gov.in, or another verifiable source.\n\nDo not fabricate missing data.\n\n## 2. IMPORTANT DISTINCTION\n\nThe following datasets are primarily CROP RECOMMENDATION datasets:\n\n- Siddharth Sharma Crop Recommendation\n- Atharva Ingle Crop Recommendation\n- Madhura Atmaram Bhagat Crop Recommendation\n- Arka Bhowmik Crop Recommendation\n\nDo NOT use these datasets as crop-yield training data unless the actual dataset contains a legitimate observed yield target.\n\nThey can potentially be used separately for crop recommendation.\n\n## 3. TRAIN A REAL XGBOOST MODEL\n\nUse an actual XGBoost regression model trained on the real dataset.\n\nThe model should have:\n\n- Real training data\n- Real target values\n- Proper preprocessing\n- Appropriate categorical encoding\n- Missing-value handling\n- Train/test split\n- Cross-validation\n- Hyperparameter tuning where appropriate\n- Final held-out test set\n\nThe target should be the actual observed crop yield, preferably kg/ha.\n\n## 4. PREVENT DATA LEAKAGE\n\nBefore training, explicitly inspect every feature.\n\nIf predicting:\n\nYield (kg/ha)\n\nDO NOT use features that directly reveal the answer, such as:\n\n- Production when yield = Production / Area\n- Harvest quantity\n- Actual yield\n- Any post-harvest measurement\n- Any feature mathematically derived from yield\n\nArea may be used only if appropriate and if it does not create leakage for the specific target definition.\n\nDocument which features were removed and why.\n\n## 5. PROPER VALIDATION\n\nDo NOT use only 48 test samples if the dataset allows a larger meaningful test set.\n\nUse an appropriate split based on the dataset.\n\nIf the dataset contains multiple years:\n\nConsider using a temporal holdout where appropriate.\n\nIf there are repeated observations from the same state/district/crop:\n\nCheck whether random splitting causes information leakage.\n\nUse grouped or temporal validation where appropriate.\n\nAlso perform cross-validation on the training data.\n\nReport:\n\n- Number of total samples\n- Training samples\n- Validation samples\n- Test samples\n- Number of CV folds\n\n## 6. REPORT REAL METRICS\n\nCalculate the actual:\n\n- R\302\262\n- MAE\n- RMSE\n\nfrom the real held-out test data.\n\nAlso compare against a simple baseline:\n\nMean predictor\n\nDisplay:\n\nModel R\302\262\nBaseline R\302\262\nModel MAE\nBaseline MAE\nModel RMSE\nBaseline RMSE\n\nDo NOT modify the metrics manually.\n\nDo NOT fabricate metrics.\n\nDo NOT claim accuracy based only on training performance.\n\n## 7. SAVE THE ACTUAL MODEL\n\nThe application must use the actual trained model for prediction.\n\nDo not replace the model with a TypeScript formula.\n\nDo not create:\n\ncropBaseYields\npredictYieldInternal()\nfake prediction multipliers\nsynthetic yield generators\n\nThe prediction displayed to the farmer must come from the trained ML model.\n\n## 8. MAKE DATASET SOURCE TRANSPARENT\n\nIn the application show:\n\nDataset:\n[actual dataset name]\n\nSource:\n[actual source]\n\nTraining samples:\nX\n\nTest samples:\nY\n\nModel:\nXGBoost Regressor\n\nR\302\262:\nX\n\nMAE:\nX kg/ha\n\nRMSE:\nX kg/ha\n\nLast trained:\n[date]\n\nDo not show the previous R\302\262 = 0.886.\n\nOnly display the new metrics after the real model has been trained and evaluated.\n\n## 9. FARMER PREDICTION\n\nAfter training the model, connect it to the farmer\'s real inputs.\n\nUse available real information such as:\n\n- District\n- State\n- Crop\n- Season\n- Farm area\n- Soil information\n- Soil type\n- pH if available\n- Nitrogen if available\n- Phosphorus if available\n- Potassium if available\n- Rainfall\n- Temperature\n- Other features actually supported by the training dataset\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\nDo not send features to the model that were not represented during training.\n\nIf the farmer does not know soil N/P/K/pH, do not invent them.\n\nUse verified SoilGrids/location data if appropriate and available.\n\nOtherwise tell the farmer that the parameter is unavailable.\n\n## 10. NO FAKE FALLBACK\n\nIf the model cannot make a reliable prediction because required information is missing:\n\nDO NOT generate a fake yield.\n\nShow:\n\n\"Insufficient verified data to generate a reliable yield prediction.\"\n\nTell the farmer which information is missing.\n\n## 11. REAL DATA ONLY\n\nThe entire yield pipeline must contain ZERO:\n\n- Synthetic rows\n- Random rows\n- pseudoRandom()\n- Hardcoded crop yield values\n- Fake test samples\n- Fake model metrics\n- Formula-generated targets\n- Fake confidence scores\n\nThe only acceptable sources are:\n\nREAL DATASET\n+\nREAL FARMER INPUT\n+\nREAL LIVE API DATA\n+\nREAL ML MODEL