  user_input {
    user_response: "The dataset audit is acceptable as a starting point, but before training the final model I need you to correct several issues.\n\nDO NOT TRAIN THE MODEL YET.\n\n### 1. Correct the soil data assumptions\n\nDo NOT claim that SoilGrids provides phosphorus and potassium.\n\nAccording to the current SoilGrids documentation, it provides properties including:\n\n- pH\n- Total Nitrogen\n- Soil Organic Carbon\n- Sand\n- Silt\n- Clay\n- CEC\n- Bulk density\n- etc.\n\nIt does NOT provide P and K as standard SoilGrids mapped properties.\n\nTherefore:\n\nSoilGrids \342\206\222 pH + Nitrogen + other available soil properties\n\nFor Phosphorus and Potassium:\n- Use another verified source if one is available.\n- Otherwise ask the farmer for a soil-test value.\n- If unavailable, leave the feature missing or use a model that does not require it.\n- NEVER fabricate P/K values.\n\nClearly label SoilGrids values as:\n\"Location-based soil estimate\"\n\nDo not call them the farmer\'s actual soil-test values.\n\n### 2. Separate PRE-SEASON and IN-SEASON yield prediction\n\nThis is extremely important.\n\nDo not train a model that requires future information when making a pre-season prediction.\n\nCreate two conceptual prediction modes.\n\n#### PRE-SEASON\n\nUse only information available before planting / at the start of the crop:\n\n- State\n- District\n- Crop\n- Season\n- Farm area\n- Soil properties\n- Historical climate information\n- Planned fertilizer if provided\n- Other genuinely available pre-season information\n\nDo NOT use future annual rainfall.\n\nDo NOT use future actual pesticide/fertilizer usage.\n\nDo NOT use actual harvested production.\n\n#### IN-SEASON\n\nAfter planting, allow the prediction to update using information that has actually become available:\n\n- Actual rainfall received so far\n- Live/current weather\n- Irrigation\n- Actual fertilizer application\n- Crop growth stage\n- Other verified observations\n\nClearly label the result:\n\n\"Pre-season estimate\"\n\nor\n\n\"In-season updated estimate\"\n\n### 3. Do not use future annual rainfall for pre-season prediction\n\nThe historical dataset contains Annual_Rainfall.\n\nThat can be useful for historical model training, but when making a future prediction, the system must not magically know the full future year\'s rainfall.\n\nInstead:\n\nFor pre-season:\nUse historical climate statistics / historical rainfall features.\n\nFor in-season:\nUse actual rainfall-to-date and live weather/forecast information.\n\nMake sure the model\'s training features correspond to the information that will actually be available at prediction time.\n\n### 4. Reconsider fertilizer and pesticide features\n\nCheck exactly what the historical dataset\'s Fertilizer and Pesticide columns represent.\n\nIf they represent quantities applied during the completed crop season, they cannot be treated as known inputs for a pre-season prediction unless the farmer provides a planned amount.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- Planned fertilizer \342\206\222 can be used for pre-season prediction.\n- Actual fertilizer applied \342\206\222 can be used for in-season updates.\n- Future/unknown fertilizer \342\206\222 must not be used.\n\nDo the same analysis for pesticide.\n\n### 5. Area feature\n\nKeep Area only if there is a defensible reason for it to improve yield prediction and it is available at prediction time.\n\nVerify that it does not introduce leakage or simply act as a proxy for another target-related variable.\n\nReport its importance and test model performance with and without Area.\n\n### 6. Temporal validation\n\nUse a genuine temporal evaluation.\n\nPreferred structure:\n\nTraining:\n1997\342\200\2232015\n\nValidation:\n2016\n\nFinal held-out test:\n2017\342\200\2232020\n\nDo not use 2017\342\200\2232020 for model selection or hyperparameter tuning.\n\nOptionally perform rolling/time-series cross-validation inside 1997\342\200\2232015.\n\n### 7. Target encoding\n\nIf using target encoding for:\n\n- State\n- District\n- Crop\n\nthe encoder MUST be fitted only on the training data for each split/fold.\n\nNever calculate target encodings using the validation or test target values.\n\nOtherwise this creates target leakage.\n\n### 8. Test generalization properly\n\nReport:\n\nOverall:\n- R\302\262\n- MAE\n- RMSE\n\nAlso report performance by:\n\n- Crop\n- State/region\n- Year\n\nwhere enough samples exist.\n\nThis is important because a high overall R\302\262 can hide poor performance on individual crops.\n\n### 9. Baseline\n\nCompare XGBoost against:\n\n- Mean predictor\n- Crop-specific historical mean where appropriate\n\nReport both.\n\n### 10. Model reliability\n\nDo NOT display a prediction as if it were guaranteed.\n\nFor example:\n\nPredicted yield:\n2,850 kg/ha\n\nHistorical model error:\nMAE = X kg/ha\n\nThe UI should communicate that this is an ML estimate, not a guaranteed yield.\n\n### 11. FINAL REQUIREMENT\n\nBefore training, provide a final table:\n\n| Feature | Source | Available pre-season? | Available in-season? | Leakage risk |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| District | Farmer | Yes | Yes | No |\n| Crop | Farmer | Yes | Yes | No |\n| Soil pH | SoilGrids / soil test | Yes | Yes | No |\n| Soil N | SoilGrids / soil test | Yes | Yes | No |\n| Soil P | Verified source / farmer | Depends | Depends | No |\n| Soil K | Verified source / farmer | Depends | Depends | No |\n| Rainfall | Historical/live API | Yes (historical) | Yes | Check |\n| Fertilizer | Farmer planned/actual | Planned | Actual | Check |\n| Pesticide | Farmer planned/actual | Planned | Actual | Check |\n| Area | Farmer | Yes | Yes | Check |\n\nOnly after this audit is correct should you train the real XGBoost model.\n\nNO synthetic data.\nNO random data.\nNO hardcoded crop yields.\nNO fabricated metrics.\nNO fabricated soil values.\nNO future information leakage."
    items {
      text: "The dataset audit is acceptable as a starting point, but before training the final model I need you to correct several issues.\n\nDO NOT TRAIN THE MODEL YET.\n\n### 1. Correct the soil data assumptions\n\nDo NOT claim that SoilGrids provides phosphorus and potassium.\n\nAccording to the current SoilGrids documentation, it provides properties including:\n\n- pH\n- Total Nitrogen\n- Soil Organic Carbon\n- Sand\n- Silt\n- Clay\n- CEC\n- Bulk density\n- etc.\n\nIt does NOT provide P and K as standard SoilGrids mapped properties.\n\nTherefore:\n\nSoilGrids \342\206\222 pH + Nitrogen + other available soil properties\n\nFor Phosphorus and Potassium:\n- Use another verified source if one is available.\n- Otherwise ask the farmer for a soil-test value.\n- If unavailable, leave the feature missing or use a model that does not require it.\n- NEVER fabricate P/K values.\n\nClearly label SoilGrids values as:\n\"Location-based soil estimate\"\n\nDo not call them the farmer\'s actual soil-test values.\n\n### 2. Separate PRE-SEASON and IN-SEASON yield prediction\n\nThis is extremely important.\n\nDo not train a model that requires future information when making a pre-season prediction.\n\nCreate two conceptual prediction modes.\n\n#### PRE-SEASON\n\nUse only information available before planting / at the start of the crop:\n\n- State\n- District\n- Crop\n- Season\n- Farm area\n- Soil properties\n- Historical climate information\n- Planned fertilizer if provided\n- Other genuinely available pre-season information\n\nDo NOT use future annual rainfall.\n\nDo NOT use future actual pesticide/fertilizer usage.\n\nDo NOT use actual harvested production.\n\n#### IN-SEASON\n\nAfter planting, allow the prediction to update using information that has actually become available:\n\n- Actual rainfall received so far\n- Live/current weather\n- Irrigation\n- Actual fertilizer application\n- Crop growth stage\n- Other verified observations\n\nClearly label the result:\n\n\"Pre-season estimate\"\n\nor\n\n\"In-season updated estimate\"\n\n### 3. Do not use future annual rainfall for pre-season prediction\n\nThe historical dataset contains Annual_Rainfall.\n\nThat can be useful for historical model training, but when making a future prediction, the system must not magically know the full future year\'s rainfall.\n\nInstead:\n\nFor pre-season:\nUse historical climate statistics / historical rainfall features.\n\nFor in-season:\nUse actual rainfall-to-date and live weather/forecast information.\n\nMake sure the model\'s training features correspond to the information that will actually be available at prediction time.\n\n### 4. Reconsider fertilizer and pesticide features\n\nCheck exactly what the historical dataset\'s Fertilizer and Pesticide columns represent.\n\nIf they represent quantities applied during the completed crop season, they cannot be treated as known inputs for a pre-season prediction unless the farmer provides a planned amount.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- Planned fertilizer \342\206\222 can be used for pre-season prediction.\n- Actual fertilizer applied \342\206\222 can be used for in-season updates.\n- Future/unknown fertilizer \342\206\222 must not be used.\n\nDo the same analysis for pesticide.\n\n### 5. Area feature\n\nKeep Area only if there is a defensible reason for it to improve yield prediction and it is available at prediction time.\n\nVerify that it does not introduce leakage or simply act as a proxy for another target-related variable.\n\nReport its importance and test model performance with and without Area.\n\n### 6. Temporal validation\n\nUse a genuine temporal evaluation.\n\nPreferred structure:\n\nTraining:\n1997\342\200\2232015\n\nValidation:\n2016\n\nFinal held-out test:\n2017\342\200\2232020\n\nDo not use 2017\342\200\2232020 for model selection or hyperparameter tuning.\n\nOptionally perform rolling/time-series cross-validation inside 1997\342\200\2232015.\n\n### 7. Target encoding\n\nIf using target encoding for:\n\n- State\n- District\n- Crop\n\nthe encoder MUST be fitted only on the training data for each split/fold.\n\nNever calculate target encodings using the validation or test target values.\n\nOtherwise this creates target leakage.\n\n### 8. Test generalization properly\n\nReport:\n\nOverall:\n- R\302\262\n- MAE\n- RMSE\n\nAlso report performance by:\n\n- Crop\n- State/region\n- Year\n\nwhere enough samples exist.\n\nThis is important because a high overall R\302\262 can hide poor performance on individual crops.\n\n### 9. Baseline\n\nCompare XGBoost against:\n\n- Mean predictor\n- Crop-specific historical mean where appropriate\n\nReport both.\n\n### 10. Model reliability\n\nDo NOT display a prediction as if it were guaranteed.\n\nFor example:\n\nPredicted yield:\n2,850 kg/ha\n\nHistorical model error:\nMAE = X kg/ha\n\nThe UI should communicate that this is an ML estimate, not a guaranteed yield.\n\n### 11. FINAL REQUIREMENT\n\nBefore training, provide a final table:\n\n| Feature | Source | Available pre-season? | Available in-season? | Leakage risk |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| District | Farmer | Yes | Yes | No |\n| Crop | Farmer | Yes | Yes | No |\n| Soil pH | SoilGrids / soil test | Yes | Yes | No |\n| Soil N | SoilGrids / soil test | Yes | Yes | No |\n| Soil P | Verified source / farmer | Depends | Depends | No |\n| Soil K | Verified source / farmer | Depends | Depends | No |\n| Rainfall | Historical/live API | Yes (historical) | Yes | Check |\n| Fertilizer | Farmer planned/actual | Planned | Actual | Check |\n| Pesticide | Farmer planned/actual | Planned | Actual | Check |\n| Area | Farmer | Yes | Yes | Check |\n\nOnly after this audit is correct should you train the real XGBoost model.\n\nNO synthetic data.\nNO random data.\nNO hardcoded crop yields.\nNO fabricated metrics.\nNO fabricated soil values.\nNO future information leakage."
