  user_input {
    user_response: "I am attaching the actual datasets/files for my NutriHarvest AI application.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nDo NOT immediately modify the application or train any model.\n\nFirst inspect EVERY attached file and produce a complete data audit. Only after the audit is correct should you implement the data/ML pipeline.\n\nThe core principle of this application is:\n\nREAL FARMER DATA\n+\nREAL VERIFIED DATASETS\n+\nLIVE VERIFIED APIs\n+\nREAL ML MODELS\n+\nTRANSPARENT CALCULATIONS\n\nNEVER:\n- fabricate data\n- generate synthetic training rows\n- use random values\n- create fake model metrics\n- hardcode agricultural values\n- use Gemini to invent agricultural facts\n- silently substitute missing data with fake values\n\n==================================================\nPART 1 \342\200\224 IDENTIFY EVERY ATTACHED FILE\n==================================================\n\nFor every attached file, report:\n\n- Filename\n- File type\n- Number of rows\n- Number of columns\n- Column names\n- Data types\n- Missing values\n- Duplicate records\n- Date/year range\n- Geographic coverage\n- What the dataset appears to represent\n- What feature of NutriHarvest AI it should be used for\n\nCreate a table:\n\n| File | Rows | Columns | Purpose | Recommended Use |\n|------|------|---------|---------|-----------------|\n\nDo NOT assume that a dataset is suitable just because its filename sounds appropriate.\n\nInspect the actual contents.\n\n==================================================\nPART 2 \342\200\224 CROP YIELD DATASET\n==================================================\n\nIdentify the real Agricultural Crop Yield in Indian States dataset.\n\nThis dataset should be used for the REAL XGBoost crop-yield model.\n\nExpected information may include:\n\n- State\n- District\n- Crop Year\n- Season\n- Crop\n- Area\n- Production\n- Rainfall\n- Fertilizer\n- Pesticide\n- Yield\n\nVerify the actual columns in the uploaded file.\n\nTARGET:\n\nYield in kg/hectare.\n\nCRITICAL:\n\nProduction MUST NOT be used as a model feature because:\n\nYield = Production / Area\n\nUsing Production would create target leakage.\n\nRemove/exclude Production from all yield-model feature vectors.\n\nDo NOT delete Production from the original dataset. Keep it available for historical analysis if needed, but never pass it to the yield prediction model.\n\n==================================================\nPART 3 \342\200\224 VERIFY THE YIELD DATASET SIZE\n==================================================\n\nThis is extremely important because the previous implementation incorrectly showed:\n\nTraining = 93 rows\nTest = 52 rows\nR\302\262 = 0.931\n\nDo NOT trust those previous metrics.\n\nUsing the uploaded CSV, calculate the REAL number of rows.\n\nShow:\n\nTotal records:\nTraining:\nValidation:\nFinal Test:\n\nAlso show the number of records for EVERY year.\n\nExpected time range:\n\n1997\342\200\2232020\n\nUse:\n\nTRAINING:\n1997\342\200\2232015\n\nVALIDATION:\n2016\n\nFINAL HELD-OUT TEST:\n2017\342\200\2232020\n\nDo NOT randomly sample the dataset.\n\nDo NOT use only a tiny subset.\n\nDo NOT use .slice() or similar arbitrary row limits.\n\nUse all valid real observations unless there is a documented reason to exclude them.\n\nIf the uploaded dataset contains fewer rows than expected, tell me exactly why.\n\n==================================================\nPART 4 \342\200\224 REAL XGBOOST YIELD MODEL\n==================================================\n\nAfter the dataset audit is complete, train a REAL XGBoost regression model.\n\nThe model must be genuinely trained using the uploaded real dataset.\n\nDO NOT use:\n\n- pseudoRandom()\n- Math.random()\n- synthetic records\n- cropBaseYields\n- hardcoded yield tables\n- mathematical yield generators\n- heuristic prediction functions\n- fake XGBoost metrics\n- manually created training data\n\nThe prediction must come from the actual trained XGBoost model.\n\nEvaluate using:\n\n- R\302\262\n- MAE\n- RMSE\n\nUse the untouched 2017\342\200\2232020 data as the FINAL test set.\n\nDo NOT use the final test set during:\n\n- Training\n- Feature selection\n- Hyperparameter tuning\n- Target encoding\n- Model selection\n\n==================================================\nPART 5 \342\200\224 PREVENT DATA LEAKAGE\n==================================================\n\nAudit every feature for leakage.\n\nFor each feature report:\n\n| Feature | Source | Keep/Remove | Leakage Risk | Reason |\n\nProduction MUST be removed.\n\nAlso check for indirect leakage.\n\nFor example, remove any variable that is mathematically derived from Yield or Production.\n\nTarget encoding must be performed safely:\n\n- Fit only on training data.\n- During cross-validation, fit encoding only on the training fold.\n- Never use validation/test target values when calculating encodings.\n\n==================================================\nPART 6 \342\200\224 PRE-SEASON VS IN-SEASON MODEL\n==================================================\n\nThe application must distinguish between what is known before planting and what becomes known during crop growth.\n\n### PRE-SEASON\n\nOnly use information actually available before planting:\n\n- State\n- District\n- Crop\n- Season\n- Farm area\n- Soil pH\n- Soil nitrogen\n- Historical climate information\n- Planned fertilizer\n- Planned pesticide where appropriate\n\nDO NOT use:\n\n- Future rainfall\n- Actual future fertilizer\n- Actual future pesticide\n- Harvest information\n- Production\n- Actual yield\n\n### IN-SEASON\n\nThe prediction can be updated using information that has actually become available:\n\n- Actual cumulative rainfall\n- Live weather\n- Actual fertilizer applied\n- Actual pesticide applied\n- Irrigation\n- Crop growth stage\n- Farmer observations\n\nClearly label:\n\n\"Pre-season ML Yield Estimate\"\n\nor\n\n\"In-season Updated ML Yield Estimate\"\n\nDo not pretend that future information is already known.\n\n==================================================\nPART 7 \342\200\224 HISTORICAL CLIMATE\n==================================================\n\nIf historical rainfall averages are used:\n\nFor a prediction in year Y, historical statistics must only use information available before year Y.\n\nExample:\n\nA 2017 prediction must NOT use:\n\n2018 rainfall\n2019 rainfall\n2020 rainfall\n\nto calculate its historical rainfall feature.\n\nPrevent this type of temporal leakage.\n\n==================================================\nPART 8 \342\200\224 SOIL DATA\n==================================================\n\nUse the uploaded soil-related data only if it is verified and appropriate.\n\nAlso integrate SoilGrids through its official API where appropriate:\n\nhttps://soilgrids.org/\n\nhttps://rest.isric.org/soilgrids/v2.0/docs\n\nUse SoilGrids only for properties it actually provides, such as:\n\n- pH\n- Total nitrogen\n- Organic carbon\n- Sand\n- Silt\n- Clay\n- etc.\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\nSoilGrids does NOT provide mapped phosphorus and potassium in the standard product.\n\nTherefore:\n\nP + K should ONLY come from:\n\n- Farmer\'s Soil Health Card\n- Laboratory soil test\n- Another verified source\n\nIf unavailable:\n\nLeave P/K missing.\n\nDO NOT invent them.\n\nLabel SoilGrids values:\n\n\"Location-based soil estimate\"\n\nLabel farmer/lab values:\n\n\"Farmer/Soil-Test Provided\"\n\n==================================================\nPART 9 \342\200\224 FARMER SOIL INPUT\n==================================================\n\nThe UI should provide two options.\n\nOPTION A:\n\nFarmer knows their soil-test values.\n\nAllow:\n\n- pH\n- Nitrogen\n- Phosphorus\n- Potassium\n\nOPTION B:\n\nFarmer does not know them.\n\nAllow:\n\n\"I don\'t know my soil values.\"\n\nThen:\n\n- Use SoilGrids for supported properties.\n- Leave unsupported properties unavailable.\n- Do NOT fabricate values.\n\nThe farmer should not be forced to understand technical soil measurements.\n\nAlso add:\n\nState dropdown\n\342\206\222 District dropdown\n\nUse a real verified list of Indian states/districts.\n\n==================================================\nPART 10 \342\200\224 CROP RECOMMENDATION DATASET\n==================================================\n\nIdentify the attached Crop Recommendation Dataset.\n\nUse it ONLY for crop suitability/recommendation.\n\nDo NOT use it as the yield-training dataset.\n\nThe crop recommendation system should consider:\n\n- Soil conditions\n- Climate\n- Rainfall\n- Location\n- Season\n- Water availability\n- Farm size\n- Current crops\n- Household nutrition gaps\n- Crop suitability\n\nThe recommendation should answer:\n\n\"What crops are suitable for THIS farmer?\"\n\nNot simply:\n\n\"What crop is generally popular?\"\n\n==================================================\nPART 11 \342\200\224 NUTRITION DATA\n==================================================\n\nIdentify the attached IFCT 2017 nutrition data/reference.\n\nUse IFCT 2017 as the PRIMARY nutrition composition source.\n\nNutrition must be calculated from what the farmer is actually growing.\n\nThe logic must be:\n\nFarmer\'s crop\n\342\206\223\nExpected or actual yield\n\342\206\223\nUsable/edible quantity where applicable\n\342\206\223\nVerified IFCT nutrient composition\n\342\206\223\nTotal nutrients produced\n\342\206\223\nHousehold nutritional requirement\n\342\206\223\nNutrition coverage %\n\342\206\223\nNutrition gap\n\342\206\223\nPotential crop recommendations\n\nDO NOT generate random nutrition numbers.\n\nFor example, if the farmer produces 500 kg wheat:\n\nThe application must calculate nutrient quantities from the verified nutrient composition of wheat.\n\nIt must NOT simply display a generic percentage.\n\nClearly distinguish:\n\nESTIMATED:\nBased on predicted yield\n\nACTUAL:\nBased on farmer-recorded harvested yield\n\n==================================================\nPART 12 \342\200\224 NUTRITIONAL REQUIREMENTS\n==================================================\n\nDo NOT use Gemini to invent household nutritional requirements.\n\nUse an authoritative nutritional requirement reference.\n\nThe system should calculate requirements based on the household information already collected, such as:\n\n- Household size\n- Age groups\n- Relevant dietary information\n- Other available household parameters\n\nThen compare:\n\nRequired nutrition\nvs\nNutrition produced by farmer\'s crops\n\nShow:\n\n- Required\n- Produced\n- Coverage %\n- Gap\n\nDo not fabricate requirements.\n\n==================================================\nPART 13 \342\200\224 VITAMIN / NUTRIENT GAP CROP RECOMMENDATIONS\n==================================================\n\nIf the household has a nutritional gap:\n\nExample:\n\nVitamin C gap\n\nThe system should:\n\n1. Identify crops containing meaningful Vitamin C from the verified nutrition data.\n2. Check whether those crops are suitable for the farmer\'s location.\n3. Check soil compatibility.\n4. Check climate.\n5. Check water availability.\n6. Check growing season.\n7. Check expected yield.\n8. Recommend only realistic candidates.\n\nDo NOT recommend a crop simply because it contains Vitamin C.\n\n==================================================\nPART 14 \342\200\224 LIVE WEATHER\n==================================================\n\nDo NOT use the uploaded dataset as the source of current weather.\n\nUse Open-Meteo:\n\nhttps://open-meteo.com/\n\nhttps://open-meteo.com/en/docs\n\nUse live/current/forecast information for:\n\n- Temperature\n- Rainfall\n- Humidity\n- Wind\n- ET0\n- Soil moisture where available\n- Forecast\n\nShow:\n\nLast updated:\nTimestamp\n\nSource:\nOpen-Meteo\n\nIf live weather is unavailable:\n\n\"Live weather data currently unavailable.\"\n\nDO NOT replace it with fake weather.\n\n==================================================\nPART 15 \342\200\224 IRRIGATION\n==================================================\n\nBuild irrigation recommendations using actual available information.\n\nPotential inputs:\n\n- Crop\n- Growth stage\n- Farm area\n- Soil characteristics\n- Weather\n- Rainfall\n- Forecast rainfall\n- ET0\n- Soil moisture if available\n- Previous irrigation\n- Water availability\n- Irrigation method\n\nIf soil moisture is unavailable, clearly say that the recommendation is an estimate.\n\nDO NOT pretend that the system knows soil moisture when it does not.\n\n==================================================\nPART 16 \342\200\224 LIVE MANDI PRICES\n==================================================\n\nUse Government of India AGMARKNET/Data.gov.in for current mandi prices:\n\nhttps://www.data.gov.in/catalog/current-daily-price-various-commodities-various-markets-mandi\n\nUse it for:\n\n- Current price\n- Minimum price\n- Maximum price\n- Modal price\n- Market\n- Commodity\n- Date\n\nShow:\n\nPrice\nMandi\nDate\nLast updated\nSource\n\nDo NOT use a historical Kaggle dataset as today\'s price.\n\nHistorical mandi datasets may be used separately for:\n\n- Price trends\n- Historical analysis\n- Forecasting\n\n==================================================\nPART 17 \342\200\224 YIELD TRACKING\n==================================================\n\nThe application must track predicted vs actual yield.\n\nBefore harvest:\n\nPredicted yield\n\nAfter harvest:\n\nFarmer enters actual yield\n\nThen store:\n\n- Crop\n- Farm/plot\n- Predicted yield\n- Actual yield\n- Date\n- Season\n- Relevant inputs\n\nCalculate:\n\nPrediction error =\nActual yield - Predicted yield\n\nDo NOT automatically retrain the production model from one farmer\'s data.\n\nUse farmer history for tracking and future analysis.\n\n==================================================\nPART 18 \342\200\224 JOURNAL AND YIELD HISTORY\n==================================================\n\nMake sure Journal and Yield History support:\n\n- Create\n- View\n- Delete\n\nDelete must actually remove the database record.\n\nDo not merely hide it from the frontend.\n\nUse Supabase RLS so users can only delete their own records.\n\nAfter deletion:\n\n- Re-fetch data\n- Update UI\n- Recalculate charts/statistics\n- Update dependent calculations\n\n==================================================\nPART 19 \342\200\224 REFRESH BUTTONS\n==================================================\n\nAdd working Refresh buttons for dynamically generated/live/calculated data:\n\n- Weather\n- Mandi prices\n- Crop recommendations\n- Nutrition analysis\n- Yield prediction\n- Dashboard\n- Farm progress\n\nRefresh must actually:\n\n- Re-fetch API data\n- Re-fetch database data\n- Recalculate derived data\n- Re-run ML inference where appropriate\n\nDo not simply reload the page.\n\nShow:\n\n\"Refreshing...\"\n\nand then:\n\n\"Updated just now\"\n\nwhere appropriate.\n\n==================================================\nPART 20 \342\200\224 REMOVE DEMO DATA\n==================================================\n\nCompletely remove:\n\n- Load Data\n- Load Demo Data\n- Load Sample Data\n- Generate Sample Data\n- Any fake-data fallback\n\nIf there is no data:\n\n\"No data available yet.\"\n\nIf an API fails:\n\n\"Live data currently unavailable.\"\n\nNEVER substitute fake values.\n\n==================================================\nPART 21 \342\200\224 GEMINI\n==================================================\n\nGemini is NOT an authoritative agricultural data source.\n\nUse Gemini ONLY for:\n\n- Farmer chat\n- Natural-language explanations\n- Explaining recommendations\n- Regional-language responses\n- Summarizing farm progress\n\nGemini must NOT generate:\n\n- Weather\n- Soil values\n- Nutrition values\n- Mandi prices\n- Yield values\n- ML metrics\n- Irrigation calculations\n\nIf Gemini quota is exceeded, core application features must continue working.\n\n==================================================\nPART 22 \342\200\224 DATA PROVENANCE\n==================================================\n\nEvery important value should have a source/type.\n\nUse labels such as:\n\nACTUAL\n\342\206\222 Farmer recorded\n\nLIVE\n\342\206\222 External API\n\nDATASET\n\342\206\222 Verified dataset\n\nPREDICTED\n\342\206\222 ML model\n\nCALCULATED\n\342\206\222 Mathematical calculation\n\nESTIMATED\n\342\206\222 Location/model-derived estimate\n\nNever mix these categories.\n\n==================================================\nPART 23 \342\200\224 FINAL MODEL METRICS\n==================================================\n\nThe previous model displayed:\n\nR\302\262 = 0.931\nTraining = 93 rows\nTest = 52 rows\n\nTreat those metrics as INVALID.\n\nAfter using the complete uploaded dataset, report:\n\nTotal records\nTraining records\nValidation records\nTest records\n\nR\302\262\nMAE\nRMSE\n\nAlso compare:\n\nGlobal mean baseline\nCrop-specific historical mean baseline\n\nAnd report per-crop performance where enough test samples exist.\n\nDO NOT optimize for a high R\302\262.\n\nAn honest R\302\262 of 0.60 is preferable to a fabricated 0.95.\n\n==================================================\nPART 24 \342\200\224 FINAL IMPLEMENTATION ORDER\n==================================================\n\nFollow this order:\n\nSTEP 1:\nAudit every uploaded file.\n\nSTEP 2:\nShow me the dataset audit.\n\nSTEP 3:\nVerify the yield dataset size and year distribution.\n\nSTEP 4:\nVerify there is no target leakage.\n\nSTEP 5:\nBuild the real XGBoost yield pipeline.\n\nSTEP 6:\nEvaluate using temporal validation.\n\nSTEP 7:\nIntegrate soil data.\n\nSTEP 8:\nIntegrate crop recommendation.\n\nSTEP 9:\nIntegrate nutrition calculations.\n\nSTEP 10:\nIntegrate live weather.\n\nSTEP 11:\nIntegrate live mandi prices.\n\nSTEP 12:\nIntegrate irrigation recommendations.\n\nSTEP 13:\nIntegrate actual-yield feedback.\n\nSTEP 14:\nFix Journal/Yield deletion.\n\nSTEP 15:\nAdd Refresh buttons.\n\nSTEP 16:\nRemove demo/sample data.\n\nSTEP 17:\nRun a complete final data-integrity audit.\n\n==================================================\nMOST IMPORTANT RULE\n==================================================\n\nDO NOT MAKE THE APPLICATION LOOK FUNCTIONAL BY GENERATING FAKE DATA.\n\nIf data is unavailable, say:\n\n\"Data unavailable.\"\n\nIf the model cannot make a reliable prediction:\n\n\"Insufficient verified data for a reliable prediction.\"\n\nIf an API fails:\n\n\"Live data currently unavailable.\"\n\nIf a dataset does not contain the required information:\n\n\"Required information is not present in the dataset.\"\n\nNEVER fill the gap with invented values.\n\nThe goal is to build a technically honest SIH prototype using real data, real APIs, real ML, and transparent calculations.\n\nBefore making major changes, inspect the existing application architecture and preserve all working features, authentication, Supabase integration, onboarding, dashboard, and UI design.\n\nSoilGrids does not require an API key.\n\nHowever, the SoilGrids REST API is currently unavailable/paused.\n\nDo NOT fabricate soil values and do NOT make the entire application fail because the REST API is unavailable.\n\nImplement the soil-data layer with a provider abstraction:\n\n1. Try SoilGrids REST API when it becomes available.\n2. If unavailable, use an appropriate official SoilGrids alternative such as WCS/WebDAV.\n3. If no SoilGrids data can be obtained for the farmer\'s location, clearly display:\n   \"Location-based soil data unavailable.\"\n\nNever substitute random or hardcoded soil values.\n\nOnly use SoilGrids properties that are actually available:\n- pH\n- Total Nitrogen\n- Organic Carbon\n- Sand\n- Silt\n- Clay\n- etc.\n\nDo NOT obtain or fabricate Phosphorus or Potassium from SoilGrids.\n\nP and K should only come from:\n- Farmer\'s Soil Health Card\n- Laboratory soil test\n- Another verified source\n\nClearly label SoilGrids values as:\n\"Location-based soil estimate\"\n\nand farmer/lab values as:\n\"Farmer/Soil-Test Provided.\"\nIMPLEMENT THE NUTRITION SYSTEM FOR NUTRIHARVEST AI.\n\nI am attaching the official/reference IFCT 2017 nutrition material.\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\nNutrition must be based on VERIFIED DATA and DETERMINISTIC CALCULATIONS.\n\nGemini must NOT invent nutrition values.\n\nDo NOT use random nutrition values.\n\nDo NOT hardcode arbitrary nutrition numbers.\n\nDo NOT use a generic percentage such as \"this crop provides 70% nutrition\" without calculating it from actual quantities.\n\n==================================================\n1. INSPECT THE ATTACHED NUTRITION DATA\n==================================================\n\nFirst inspect the attached IFCT 2017 material.\n\nIdentify:\n\n- Food/crop names\n- Nutrient fields\n- Units\n- Per-100g/per-unit basis\n- Edible portion information\n- Any preparation/processing distinctions\n- Missing values\n\nCreate a mapping between the farmer\'s crop names and appropriate IFCT food entries.\n\nDo NOT automatically assume that a crop name and an IFCT food entry are equivalent.\n\nIf a mapping is uncertain:\n\nFlag it for review.\n\nIf no appropriate verified entry exists:\n\nDisplay:\n\n\"Nutrition data unavailable for this crop.\"\n\nDo NOT fabricate a value.\n\n==================================================\n2. CROP \342\206\222 FOOD \342\206\222 NUTRIENT PIPELINE\n==================================================\n\nThe calculation must work like:\n\nFarmer crop\n\342\206\223\nCanonical crop/food mapping\n\342\206\223\nVerified IFCT entry\n\342\206\223\nNutrient composition\n\342\206\223\nActual or estimated usable production\n\342\206\223\nTotal nutrient quantity\n\n==================================================\n3. PREDICTED NUTRITION\n==================================================\n\nBefore harvest:\n\nUse:\n\nPredicted yield from the REAL XGBoost model\n\303\227\nFarm area\n=\nEstimated production\n\nThen calculate estimated nutrition using the verified nutrient composition.\n\nClearly label:\n\n\"Estimated Nutrition\"\n\nDo NOT present estimated nutrition as actual nutrition.\n\n==================================================\n4. ACTUAL NUTRITION\n==================================================\n\nAfter harvest:\n\nUse the farmer\'s actual recorded harvest quantity/yield.\n\nCalculate:\n\nActual production\n\342\206\223\nActual nutrition contribution\n\nClearly label:\n\n\"Actual Nutrition\"\n\nActual farmer data must override predicted yield for the actual nutrition calculation.\n\n==================================================\n5. EDIBLE PORTION\n==================================================\n\nDo not blindly multiply harvested agricultural biomass by an IFCT food value.\n\nCheck whether the IFCT entry is based on:\n\n- edible portion\n- raw food\n- cooked food\n- processed food\n- other preparation state\n\nApply the appropriate conversion only when the source provides enough information to justify it.\n\nIf a scientifically valid conversion cannot be established:\n\nDo not invent one.\n\nClearly state what quantity basis is being used.\n\n==================================================\n6. HOUSEHOLD REQUIREMENTS\n==================================================\n\nUse an authoritative NIN/ICMR nutritional requirement reference.\n\nDo NOT use Gemini to determine nutritional requirements.\n\nUse the household information collected during onboarding:\n\n- Household size\n- Age groups\n- Other relevant demographic inputs\n- Dietary preferences where applicable\n\nCalculate the household requirement for relevant nutrients.\n\nKeep the requirement calculation deterministic and transparent.\n\n==================================================\n7. NUTRITION COVERAGE\n==================================================\n\nFor every tracked nutrient calculate:\n\nCoverage % =\nNutrient supplied / Nutrient required \303\227 100\n\nAlso calculate:\n\nNutrition gap =\nNutrient required - Nutrient supplied\n\nDisplay:\n\nRequired\nProduced\nCoverage %\nGap\n\nDo not hide the underlying numbers.\n\n==================================================\n8. NUTRIENTS\n==================================================\n\nUse only nutrients actually supported by the verified nutrition source.\n\nPotential nutrients include:\n\n- Energy\n- Protein\n- Fiber\n- Calcium\n- Iron\n- Vitamin A\n- Vitamin C\n- Other nutrients where reliable data exists\n\nDo not fabricate missing nutrient values.\n\n==================================================\n9. NUTRITION GAP \342\206\222 CROP RECOMMENDATION\n==================================================\n\nIf a household has a significant nutritional gap:\n\nExample:\n\nVitamin C gap\n\nThe system should:\n\n1. Identify crops/foods with verified Vitamin C content.\n2. Identify crops suitable for the farmer\'s location.\n3. Check soil suitability.\n4. Check climate.\n5. Check season.\n6. Check water availability.\n7. Check expected yield.\n8. Consider farm area.\n9. Rank realistic options.\n\nThe recommendation must satisfy BOTH:\n\nNutritional usefulness\nAND\nAgricultural suitability.\n\nDo not recommend a crop solely because it contains a nutrient.\n\n==================================================\n10. NO RANDOM FALLBACK\n==================================================\n\nIf a nutrient value is missing:\n\nDo NOT:\n- generate it\n- estimate it with Gemini\n- use a random value\n- copy another crop\'s value\n\nDisplay:\n\n\"Verified nutrition data unavailable.\"\n\n==================================================\n11. SOURCE TRANSPARENCY\n==================================================\n\nFor every nutrition result show:\n\nSource:\nIFCT 2017 / verified nutrition reference\n\nData type:\nVerified reference data\n\nFor predicted production:\n\nData type:\nML prediction\n\nFor farmer harvest:\n\nData type:\nFarmer recorded actual\n\nClearly distinguish:\n\nVERIFIED\nPREDICTED\nACTUAL\nCALCULATED\n\n==================================================\n12. REFRESH\n==================================================\n\nAdd a Refresh Nutrition button.\n\nWhen clicked:\n\n- Re-fetch current farmer data\n- Recalculate production\n- Recalculate nutrient contribution\n- Recalculate household requirements\n- Recalculate coverage\n- Recalculate gaps\n- Recalculate recommendations\n\nDo NOT call Gemini just to perform arithmetic.\n\n==================================================\n13. DATABASE\n==================================================\n\nStore user-specific nutrition data securely.\n\nA user\'s nutrition information must never be visible to another user.\n\nUse Supabase RLS.\n\nStore appropriate historical snapshots where necessary so that previous farm-progress records remain understandable.\n\n==================================================\n14. IMPORTANT LICENSING\n==================================================\n\nDo not redistribute the complete IFCT database without checking NIN\'s permission requirements.\n\nThe official IFCT 2017 document states that electronic storage/reproduction for creating a product requires prior permission from NIN.\n\nTherefore structure the application so the nutrition source can be replaced or updated if licensing requirements require it.\n\n==================================================\nFINAL REQUIREMENT\n\nBefore implementing, inspect the attached nutrition material and report:\n\n1. Exact food/crop entries available\n2. Exact nutrient columns\n3. Units\n4. Quantity basis\n5. Edible portion information\n6. Which of our crops can be reliably mapped\n7. Which crops cannot be mapped\n8. Any licensing/data-use concerns\n\nDo not fabricate anything."
    items {
      text: "I am attaching the actual datasets/files for my NutriHarvest AI application.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nDo NOT immediately modify the application or train any model.\n\nFirst inspect EVERY attached file and produce a complete data audit. Only after the audit is correct should you implement the data/ML pipeline.\n\nThe core principle of this application is:\n\nREAL FARMER DATA\n+\nREAL VERIFIED DATASETS\n+\nLIVE VERIFIED APIs\n+\nREAL ML MODELS\n+\nTRANSPARENT CALCULATIONS\n\nNEVER:\n- fabricate data\n- generate synthetic training rows\n- use random values\n- create fake model metrics\n- hardcode agricultural values\n- use Gemini to invent agricultural facts\n- silently substitute missing data with fake values\n\n==================================================\nPART 1 \342\200\224 IDENTIFY EVERY ATTACHED FILE\n==================================================\n\nFor every attached file, report:\n\n- Filename\n- File type\n- Number of rows\n- Number of columns\n- Column names\n- Data types\n- Missing values\n- Duplicate records\n- Date/year range\n- Geographic coverage\n- What the dataset appears to represent\n- What feature of NutriHarvest AI it should be used for\n\nCreate a table:\n\n| File | Rows | Columns | Purpose | Recommended Use |\n|------|------|---------|---------|-----------------|\n\nDo NOT assume that a dataset is suitable just because its filename sounds appropriate.\n\nInspect the actual contents.\n\n==================================================\nPART 2 \342\200\224 CROP YIELD DATASET\n==================================================\n\nIdentify the real Agricultural Crop Yield in Indian States dataset.\n\nThis dataset should be used for the REAL XGBoost crop-yield model.\n\nExpected information may include:\n\n- State\n- District\n- Crop Year\n- Season\n- Crop\n- Area\n- Production\n- Rainfall\n- Fertilizer\n- Pesticide\n- Yield\n\nVerify the actual columns in the uploaded file.\n\nTARGET:\n\nYield in kg/hectare.\n\nCRITICAL:\n\nProduction MUST NOT be used as a model feature because:\n\nYield = Production / Area\n\nUsing Production would create target leakage.\n\nRemove/exclude Production from all yield-model feature vectors.\n\nDo NOT delete Production from the original dataset. Keep it available for historical analysis if needed, but never pass it to the yield prediction model.\n\n==================================================\nPART 3 \342\200\224 VERIFY THE YIELD DATASET SIZE\n==================================================\n\nThis is extremely important because the previous implementation incorrectly showed:\n\nTraining = 93 rows\nTest = 52 rows\nR\302\262 = 0.931\n\nDo NOT trust those previous metrics.\n\nUsing the uploaded CSV, calculate the REAL number of rows.\n\nShow:\n\nTotal records:\nTraining:\nValidation:\nFinal Test:\n\nAlso show the number of records for EVERY year.\n\nExpected time range:\n\n1997\342\200\2232020\n\nUse:\n\nTRAINING:\n1997\342\200\2232015\n\nVALIDATION:\n2016\n\nFINAL HELD-OUT TEST:\n2017\342\200\2232020\n\nDo NOT randomly sample the dataset.\n\nDo NOT use only a tiny subset.\n\nDo NOT use .slice() or similar arbitrary row limits.\n\nUse all valid real observations unless there is a documented reason to exclude them.\n\nIf the uploaded dataset contains fewer rows than expected, tell me exactly why.\n\n==================================================\nPART 4 \342\200\224 REAL XGBOOST YIELD MODEL\n==================================================\n\nAfter the dataset audit is complete, train a REAL XGBoost regression model.\n\nThe model must be genuinely trained using the uploaded real dataset.\n\nDO NOT use:\n\n- pseudoRandom()\n- Math.random()\n- synthetic records\n- cropBaseYields\n- hardcoded yield tables\n- mathematical yield generators\n- heuristic prediction functions\n- fake XGBoost metrics\n- manually created training data\n\nThe prediction must come from the actual trained XGBoost model.\n\nEvaluate using:\n\n- R\302\262\n- MAE\n- RMSE\n\nUse the untouched 2017\342\200\2232020 data as the FINAL test set.\n\nDo NOT use the final test set during:\n\n- Training\n- Feature selection\n- Hyperparameter tuning\n- Target encoding\n- Model selection\n\n==================================================\nPART 5 \342\200\224 PREVENT DATA LEAKAGE\n==================================================\n\nAudit every feature for leakage.\n\nFor each feature report:\n\n| Feature | Source | Keep/Remove | Leakage Risk | Reason |\n\nProduction MUST be removed.\n\nAlso check for indirect leakage.\n\nFor example, remove any variable that is mathematically derived from Yield or Production.\n\nTarget encoding must be performed safely:\n\n- Fit only on training data.\n- During cross-validation, fit encoding only on the training fold.\n- Never use validation/test target values when calculating encodings.\n\n==================================================\nPART 6 \342\200\224 PRE-SEASON VS IN-SEASON MODEL\n==================================================\n\nThe application must distinguish between what is known before planting and what becomes known during crop growth.\n\n### PRE-SEASON\n\nOnly use information actually available before planting:\n\n- State\n- District\n- Crop\n- Season\n- Farm area\n- Soil pH\n- Soil nitrogen\n- Historical climate information\n- Planned fertilizer\n- Planned pesticide where appropriate\n\nDO NOT use:\n\n- Future rainfall\n- Actual future fertilizer\n- Actual future pesticide\n- Harvest information\n- Production\n- Actual yield\n\n### IN-SEASON\n\nThe prediction can be updated using information that has actually become available:\n\n- Actual cumulative rainfall\n- Live weather\n- Actual fertilizer applied\n- Actual pesticide applied\n- Irrigation\n- Crop growth stage\n- Farmer observations\n\nClearly label:\n\n\"Pre-season ML Yield Estimate\"\n\nor\n\n\"In-season Updated ML Yield Estimate\"\n\nDo not pretend that future information is already known.\n\n==================================================\nPART 7 \342\200\224 HISTORICAL CLIMATE\n==================================================\n\nIf historical rainfall averages are used:\n\nFor a prediction in year Y, historical statistics must only use information available before year Y.\n\nExample:\n\nA 2017 prediction must NOT use:\n\n2018 rainfall\n2019 rainfall\n2020 rainfall\n\nto calculate its historical rainfall feature.\n\nPrevent this type of temporal leakage.\n\n==================================================\nPART 8 \342\200\224 SOIL DATA\n==================================================\n\nUse the uploaded soil-related data only if it is verified and appropriate.\n\nAlso integrate SoilGrids through its official API where appropriate:\n\nhttps://soilgrids.org/\n\nhttps://rest.isric.org/soilgrids/v2.0/docs\n\nUse SoilGrids only for properties it actually provides, such as:\n\n- pH\n- Total nitrogen\n- Organic carbon\n- Sand\n- Silt\n- Clay\n- etc.\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\nSoilGrids does NOT provide mapped phosphorus and potassium in the standard product.\n\nTherefore:\n\nP + K should ONLY come from:\n\n- Farmer\'s Soil Health Card\n- Laboratory soil test\n- Another verified source\n\nIf unavailable:\n\nLeave P/K missing.\n\nDO NOT invent them.\n\nLabel SoilGrids values:\n\n\"Location-based soil estimate\"\n\nLabel farmer/lab values:\n\n\"Farmer/Soil-Test Provided\"\n\n==================================================\nPART 9 \342\200\224 FARMER SOIL INPUT\n==================================================\n\nThe UI should provide two options.\n\nOPTION A:\n\nFarmer knows their soil-test values.\n\nAllow:\n\n- pH\n- Nitrogen\n- Phosphorus\n- Potassium\n\nOPTION B:\n\nFarmer does not know them.\n\nAllow:\n\n\"I don\'t know my soil values.\"\n\nThen:\n\n- Use SoilGrids for supported properties.\n- Leave unsupported properties unavailable.\n- Do NOT fabricate values.\n\nThe farmer should not be forced to understand technical soil measurements.\n\nAlso add:\n\nState dropdown\n\342\206\222 District dropdown\n\nUse a real verified list of Indian states/districts.\n\n==================================================\nPART 10 \342\200\224 CROP RECOMMENDATION DATASET\n==================================================\n\nIdentify the attached Crop Recommendation Dataset.\n\nUse it ONLY for crop suitability/recommendation.\n\nDo NOT use it as the yield-training dataset.\n\nThe crop recommendation system should consider:\n\n- Soil conditions\n- Climate\n- Rainfall\n- Location\n- Season\n- Water availability\n- Farm size\n- Current crops\n- Household nutrition gaps\n- Crop suitability\n\nThe recommendation should answer:\n\n\"What crops are suitable for THIS farmer?\"\n\nNot simply:\n\n\"What crop is generally popular?\"\n\n==================================================\nPART 11 \342\200\224 NUTRITION DATA\n==================================================\n\nIdentify the attached IFCT 2017 nutrition data/reference.\n\nUse IFCT 2017 as the PRIMARY nutrition composition source.\n\nNutrition must be calculated from what the farmer is actually growing.\n\nThe logic must be:\n\nFarmer\'s crop\n\342\206\223\nExpected or actual yield\n\342\206\223\nUsable/edible quantity where applicable\n\342\206\223\nVerified IFCT nutrient composition\n\342\206\223\nTotal nutrients produced\n\342\206\223\nHousehold nutritional requirement\n\342\206\223\nNutrition coverage %\n\342\206\223\nNutrition gap\n\342\206\223\nPotential crop recommendations\n\nDO NOT generate random nutrition numbers.\n\nFor example, if the farmer produces 500 kg wheat:\n\nThe application must calculate nutrient quantities from the verified nutrient composition of wheat.\n\nIt must NOT simply display a generic percentage.\n\nClearly distinguish:\n\nESTIMATED:\nBased on predicted yield\n\nACTUAL:\nBased on farmer-recorded harvested yield\n\n==================================================\nPART 12 \342\200\224 NUTRITIONAL REQUIREMENTS\n==================================================\n\nDo NOT use Gemini to invent household nutritional requirements.\n\nUse an authoritative nutritional requirement reference.\n\nThe system should calculate requirements based on the household information already collected, such as:\n\n- Household size\n- Age groups\n- Relevant dietary information\n- Other available household parameters\n\nThen compare:\n\nRequired nutrition\nvs\nNutrition produced by farmer\'s crops\n\nShow:\n\n- Required\n- Produced\n- Coverage %\n- Gap\n\nDo not fabricate requirements.\n\n==================================================\nPART 13 \342\200\224 VITAMIN / NUTRIENT GAP CROP RECOMMENDATIONS\n==================================================\n\nIf the household has a nutritional gap:\n\nExample:\n\nVitamin C gap\n\nThe system should:\n\n1. Identify crops containing meaningful Vitamin C from the verified nutrition data.\n2. Check whether those crops are suitable for the farmer\'s location.\n3. Check soil compatibility.\n4. Check climate.\n5. Check water availability.\n6. Check growing season.\n7. Check expected yield.\n8. Recommend only realistic candidates.\n\nDo NOT recommend a crop simply because it contains Vitamin C.\n\n==================================================\nPART 14 \342\200\224 LIVE WEATHER\n==================================================\n\nDo NOT use the uploaded dataset as the source of current weather.\n\nUse Open-Meteo:\n\nhttps://open-meteo.com/\n\nhttps://open-meteo.com/en/docs\n\nUse live/current/forecast information for:\n\n- Temperature\n- Rainfall\n- Humidity\n- Wind\n- ET0\n- Soil moisture where available\n- Forecast\n\nShow:\n\nLast updated:\nTimestamp\n\nSource:\nOpen-Meteo\n\nIf live weather is unavailable:\n\n\"Live weather data currently unavailable.\"\n\nDO NOT replace it with fake weather.\n\n==================================================\nPART 15 \342\200\224 IRRIGATION\n==================================================\n\nBuild irrigation recommendations using actual available information.\n\nPotential inputs:\n\n- Crop\n- Growth stage\n- Farm area\n- Soil characteristics\n- Weather\n- Rainfall\n- Forecast rainfall\n- ET0\n- Soil moisture if available\n- Previous irrigation\n- Water availability\n- Irrigation method\n\nIf soil moisture is unavailable, clearly say that the recommendation is an estimate.\n\nDO NOT pretend that the system knows soil moisture when it does not.\n\n==================================================\nPART 16 \342\200\224 LIVE MANDI PRICES\n==================================================\n\nUse Government of India AGMARKNET/Data.gov.in for current mandi prices:\n\nhttps://www.data.gov.in/catalog/current-daily-price-various-commodities-various-markets-mandi\n\nUse it for:\n\n- Current price\n- Minimum price\n- Maximum price\n- Modal price\n- Market\n- Commodity\n- Date\n\nShow:\n\nPrice\nMandi\nDate\nLast updated\nSource\n\nDo NOT use a historical Kaggle dataset as today\'s price.\n\nHistorical mandi datasets may be used separately for:\n\n- Price trends\n- Historical analysis\n- Forecasting\n\n==================================================\nPART 17 \342\200\224 YIELD TRACKING\n==================================================\n\nThe application must track predicted vs actual yield.\n\nBefore harvest:\n\nPredicted yield\n\nAfter harvest:\n\nFarmer enters actual yield\n\nThen store:\n\n- Crop\n- Farm/plot\n- Predicted yield\n- Actual yield\n- Date\n- Season\n- Relevant inputs\n\nCalculate:\n\nPrediction error =\nActual yield - Predicted yield\n\nDo NOT automatically retrain the production model from one farmer\'s data.\n\nUse farmer history for tracking and future analysis.\n\n==================================================\nPART 18 \342\200\224 JOURNAL AND YIELD HISTORY\n==================================================\n\nMake sure Journal and Yield History support:\n\n- Create\n- View\n- Delete\n\nDelete must actually remove the database record.\n\nDo not merely hide it from the frontend.\n\nUse Supabase RLS so users can only delete their own records.\n\nAfter deletion:\n\n- Re-fetch data\n- Update UI\n- Recalculate charts/statistics\n- Update dependent calculations\n\n==================================================\nPART 19 \342\200\224 REFRESH BUTTONS\n==================================================\n\nAdd working Refresh buttons for dynamically generated/live/calculated data:\n\n- Weather\n- Mandi prices\n- Crop recommendations\n- Nutrition analysis\n- Yield prediction\n- Dashboard\n- Farm progress\n\nRefresh must actually:\n\n- Re-fetch API data\n- Re-fetch database data\n- Recalculate derived data\n- Re-run ML inference where appropriate\n\nDo not simply reload the page.\n\nShow:\n\n\"Refreshing...\"\n\nand then:\n\n\"Updated just now\"\n\nwhere appropriate.\n\n==================================================\nPART 20 \342\200\224 REMOVE DEMO DATA\n==================================================\n\nCompletely remove:\n\n- Load Data\n- Load Demo Data\n- Load Sample Data\n- Generate Sample Data\n- Any fake-data fallback\n\nIf there is no data:\n\n\"No data available yet.\"\n\nIf an API fails:\n\n\"Live data currently unavailable.\"\n\nNEVER substitute fake values.\n\n==================================================\nPART 21 \342\200\224 GEMINI\n==================================================\n\nGemini is NOT an authoritative agricultural data source.\n\nUse Gemini ONLY for:\n\n- Farmer chat\n- Natural-language explanations\n- Explaining recommendations\n- Regional-language responses\n- Summarizing farm progress\n\nGemini must NOT generate:\n\n- Weather\n- Soil values\n- Nutrition values\n- Mandi prices\n- Yield values\n- ML metrics\n- Irrigation calculations\n\nIf Gemini quota is exceeded, core application features must continue working.\n\n==================================================\nPART 22 \342\200\224 DATA PROVENANCE\n==================================================\n\nEvery important value should have a source/type.\n\nUse labels such as:\n\nACTUAL\n\342\206\222 Farmer recorded\n\nLIVE\n\342\206\222 External API\n\nDATASET\n\342\206\222 Verified dataset\n\nPREDICTED\n\342\206\222 ML model\n\nCALCULATED\n\342\206\222 Mathematical calculation\n\nESTIMATED\n\342\206\222 Location/model-derived estimate\n\nNever mix these categories.\n\n==================================================\nPART 23 \342\200\224 FINAL MODEL METRICS\n==================================================\n\nThe previous model displayed:\n\nR\302\262 = 0.931\nTraining = 93 rows\nTest = 52 rows\n\nTreat those metrics as INVALID.\n\nAfter using the complete uploaded dataset, report:\n\nTotal records\nTraining records\nValidation records\nTest records\n\nR\302\262\nMAE\nRMSE\n\nAlso compare:\n\nGlobal mean baseline\nCrop-specific historical mean baseline\n\nAnd report per-crop performance where enough test samples exist.\n\nDO NOT optimize for a high R\302\262.\n\nAn honest R\302\262 of 0.60 is preferable to a fabricated 0.95.\n\n==================================================\nPART 24 \342\200\224 FINAL IMPLEMENTATION ORDER\n==================================================\n\nFollow this order:\n\nSTEP 1:\nAudit every uploaded file.\n\nSTEP 2:\nShow me the dataset audit.\n\nSTEP 3:\nVerify the yield dataset size and year distribution.\n\nSTEP 4:\nVerify there is no target leakage.\n\nSTEP 5:\nBuild the real XGBoost yield pipeline.\n\nSTEP 6:\nEvaluate using temporal validation.\n\nSTEP 7:\nIntegrate soil data.\n\nSTEP 8:\nIntegrate crop recommendation.\n\nSTEP 9:\nIntegrate nutrition calculations.\n\nSTEP 10:\nIntegrate live weather.\n\nSTEP 11:\nIntegrate live mandi prices.\n\nSTEP 12:\nIntegrate irrigation recommendations.\n\nSTEP 13:\nIntegrate actual-yield feedback.\n\nSTEP 14:\nFix Journal/Yield deletion.\n\nSTEP 15:\nAdd Refresh buttons.\n\nSTEP 16:\nRemove demo/sample data.\n\nSTEP 17:\nRun a complete final data-integrity audit.\n\n==================================================\nMOST IMPORTANT RULE\n==================================================\n\nDO NOT MAKE THE APPLICATION LOOK FUNCTIONAL BY GENERATING FAKE DATA.\n\nIf data is unavailable, say:\n\n\"Data unavailable.\"\n\nIf the model cannot make a reliable prediction:\n\n\"Insufficient verified data for a reliable prediction.\"\n\nIf an API fails:\n\n\"Live data currently unavailable.\"\n\nIf a dataset does not contain the required information:\n\n\"Required information is not present in the dataset.\"\n\nNEVER fill the gap with invented values.\n\nThe goal is to build a technically honest SIH prototype using real data, real APIs, real ML, and transparent calculations.\n\nBefore making major changes, inspect the existing application architecture and preserve all working features, authentication, Supabase integration, onboarding, dashboard, and UI design.\n\nSoilGrids does not require an API key.\n\nHowever, the SoilGrids REST API is currently unavailable/paused.\n\nDo NOT fabricate soil values and do NOT make the entire application fail because the REST API is unavailable.\n\nImplement the soil-data layer with a provider abstraction:\n\n1. Try SoilGrids REST API when it becomes available.\n2. If unavailable, use an appropriate official SoilGrids alternative such as WCS/WebDAV.\n3. If no SoilGrids data can be obtained for the farmer\'s location, clearly display:\n   \"Location-based soil data unavailable.\"\n\nNever substitute random or hardcoded soil values.\n\nOnly use SoilGrids properties that are actually available:\n- pH\n- Total Nitrogen\n- Organic Carbon\n- Sand\n- Silt\n- Clay\n- etc.\n\nDo NOT obtain or fabricate Phosphorus or Potassium from SoilGrids.\n\nP and K should only come from:\n- Farmer\'s Soil Health Card\n- Laboratory soil test\n- Another verified source\n\nClearly label SoilGrids values as:\n\"Location-based soil estimate\"\n\nand farmer/lab values as:\n\"Farmer/Soil-Test Provided.\"\nIMPLEMENT THE NUTRITION SYSTEM FOR NUTRIHARVEST AI.\n\nI am attaching the official/reference IFCT 2017 nutrition material.\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\nNutrition must be based on VERIFIED DATA and DETERMINISTIC CALCULATIONS.\n\nGemini must NOT invent nutrition values.\n\nDo NOT use random nutrition values.\n\nDo NOT hardcode arbitrary nutrition numbers.\n\nDo NOT use a generic percentage such as \"this crop provides 70% nutrition\" without calculating it from actual quantities.\n\n==================================================\n1. INSPECT THE ATTACHED NUTRITION DATA\n==================================================\n\nFirst inspect the attached IFCT 2017 material.\n\nIdentify:\n\n- Food/crop names\n- Nutrient fields\n- Units\n- Per-100g/per-unit basis\n- Edible portion information\n- Any preparation/processing distinctions\n- Missing values\n\nCreate a mapping between the farmer\'s crop names and appropriate IFCT food entries.\n\nDo NOT automatically assume that a crop name and an IFCT food entry are equivalent.\n\nIf a mapping is uncertain:\n\nFlag it for review.\n\nIf no appropriate verified entry exists:\n\nDisplay:\n\n\"Nutrition data unavailable for this crop.\"\n\nDo NOT fabricate a value.\n\n==================================================\n2. CROP \342\206\222 FOOD \342\206\222 NUTRIENT PIPELINE\n==================================================\n\nThe calculation must work like:\n\nFarmer crop\n\342\206\223\nCanonical crop/food mapping\n\342\206\223\nVerified IFCT entry\n\342\206\223\nNutrient composition\n\342\206\223\nActual or estimated usable production\n\342\206\223\nTotal nutrient quantity\n\n==================================================\n3. PREDICTED NUTRITION\n==================================================\n\nBefore harvest:\n\nUse:\n\nPredicted yield from the REAL XGBoost model\n\303\227\nFarm area\n=\nEstimated production\n\nThen calculate estimated nutrition using the verified nutrient composition.\n\nClearly label:\n\n\"Estimated Nutrition\"\n\nDo NOT present estimated nutrition as actual nutrition.\n\n==================================================\n4. ACTUAL NUTRITION\n==================================================\n\nAfter harvest:\n\nUse the farmer\'s actual recorded harvest quantity/yield.\n\nCalculate:\n\nActual production\n\342\206\223\nActual nutrition contribution\n\nClearly label:\n\n\"Actual Nutrition\"\n\nActual farmer data must override predicted yield for the actual nutrition calculation.\n\n==================================================\n5. EDIBLE PORTION\n==================================================\n\nDo not blindly multiply harvested agricultural biomass by an IFCT food value.\n\nCheck whether the IFCT entry is based on:\n\n- edible portion\n- raw food\n- cooked food\n- processed food\n- other preparation state\n\nApply the appropriate conversion only when the source provides enough information to justify it.\n\nIf a scientifically valid conversion cannot be established:\n\nDo not invent one.\n\nClearly state what quantity basis is being used.\n\n==================================================\n6. HOUSEHOLD REQUIREMENTS\n==================================================\n\nUse an authoritative NIN/ICMR nutritional requirement reference.\n\nDo NOT use Gemini to determine nutritional requirements.\n\nUse the household information collected during onboarding:\n\n- Household size\n- Age groups\n- Other relevant demographic inputs\n- Dietary preferences where applicable\n\nCalculate the household requirement for relevant nutrients.\n\nKeep the requirement calculation deterministic and transparent.\n\n==================================================\n7. NUTRITION COVERAGE\n==================================================\n\nFor every tracked nutrient calculate:\n\nCoverage % =\nNutrient supplied / Nutrient required \303\227 100\n\nAlso calculate:\n\nNutrition gap =\nNutrient required - Nutrient supplied\n\nDisplay:\n\nRequired\nProduced\nCoverage %\nGap\n\nDo not hide the underlying numbers.\n\n==================================================\n8. NUTRIENTS\n==================================================\n\nUse only nutrients actually supported by the verified nutrition source.\n\nPotential nutrients include:\n\n- Energy\n- Protein\n- Fiber\n- Calcium\n- Iron\n- Vitamin A\n- Vitamin C\n- Other nutrients where reliable data exists\n\nDo not fabricate missing nutrient values.\n\n==================================================\n9. NUTRITION GAP \342\206\222 CROP RECOMMENDATION\n==================================================\n\nIf a household has a significant nutritional gap:\n\nExample:\n\nVitamin C gap\n\nThe system should:\n\n1. Identify crops/foods with verified Vitamin C content.\n2. Identify crops suitable for the farmer\'s location.\n3. Check soil suitability.\n4. Check climate.\n5. Check season.\n6. Check water availability.\n7. Check expected yield.\n8. Consider farm area.\n9. Rank realistic options.\n\nThe recommendation must satisfy BOTH:\n\nNutritional usefulness\nAND\nAgricultural suitability.\n\nDo not recommend a crop solely because it contains a nutrient.\n\n==================================================\n10. NO RANDOM FALLBACK\n==================================================\n\nIf a nutrient value is missing:\n\nDo NOT:\n- generate it\n- estimate it with Gemini\n- use a random value\n- copy another crop\'s value\n\nDisplay:\n\n\"Verified nutrition data unavailable.\"\n\n==================================================\n11. SOURCE TRANSPARENCY\n==================================================\n\nFor every nutrition result show:\n\nSource:\nIFCT 2017 / verified nutrition reference\n\nData type:\nVerified reference data\n\nFor predicted production:\n\nData type:\nML prediction\n\nFor farmer harvest:\n\nData type:\nFarmer recorded actual\n\nClearly distinguish:\n\nVERIFIED\nPREDICTED\nACTUAL\nCALCULATED\n\n==================================================\n12. REFRESH\n==================================================\n\nAdd a Refresh Nutrition button.\n\nWhen clicked:\n\n- Re-fetch current farmer data\n- Recalculate production\n- Recalculate nutrient contribution\n- Recalculate household requirements\n- Recalculate coverage\n- Recalculate gaps\n- Recalculate recommendations\n\nDo NOT call Gemini just to perform arithmetic.\n\n==================================================\n13. DATABASE\n==================================================\n\nStore user-specific nutrition data securely.\n\nA user\'s nutrition information must never be visible to another user.\n\nUse Supabase RLS.\n\nStore appropriate historical snapshots where necessary so that previous farm-progress records remain understandable.\n\n==================================================\n14. IMPORTANT LICENSING\n==================================================\n\nDo not redistribute the complete IFCT database without checking NIN\'s permission requirements.\n\nThe official IFCT 2017 document states that electronic storage/reproduction for creating a product requires prior permission from NIN.\n\nTherefore structure the application so the nutrition source can be replaced or updated if licensing requirements require it.\n\n==================================================\nFINAL REQUIREMENT\n\nBefore implementing, inspect the attached nutrition material and report:\n\n1. Exact food/crop entries available\n2. Exact nutrient columns\n3. Units\n4. Quantity basis\n5. Edible portion information\n6. Which of our crops can be reliably mapped\n7. Which crops cannot be mapped\n8. Any licensing/data-use concerns\n\nDo not fabricate anything."
    }
