  user_input {
    user_response: "Now that I have provided the actual datasets and the data-source/API requirements, proceed with the REAL ML model training and integration for NutriHarvest AI.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nDo not use synthetic data.\nDo not generate training data.\nDo not fabricate metrics.\nDo not use Gemini as the ML model.\nDo not use hardcoded agricultural formulas as a replacement for ML.\n\nI want a complete, reproducible and auditable ML training pipeline.\n\n==================================================\nMODEL 1 \342\200\224 CROP YIELD PREDICTION\n==================================================\n\nTrain a REAL XGBoost regression model using the uploaded real Agricultural Crop Yield dataset.\n\nTARGET:\n\nYield in kg/hectare.\n\nCRITICAL:\n\nProduction MUST NOT be used as an input feature.\n\nProduction may remain in the raw dataset for reference/historical analysis, but it must be completely excluded from XGBoost features.\n\nReason:\n\nYield = Production / Area\n\nUsing Production would cause target leakage.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nDATA SPLIT\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nUse a strict temporal split:\n\nTRAINING:\n1997\342\200\2232015\n\nVALIDATION:\n2016\n\nFINAL HELD-OUT TEST:\n2017\342\200\2232020\n\nDo NOT randomly mix years.\n\nDo NOT use 2017\342\200\2232020 during training or hyperparameter tuning.\n\nThe final test set must remain untouched until the final evaluation.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nDATASET SIZE\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nBefore training, report:\n\nTotal rows:\nTraining rows:\nValidation rows:\nTest rows:\n\nAlso report:\n\nRows per year:\n1997:\n1998:\n...\n2020:\n\nIf the number of training samples is unexpectedly small, STOP and investigate.\n\nDo NOT silently train on a tiny subset.\n\nDo NOT use arbitrary slice(), limit(), sample(), or demo-data restrictions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nFEATURES\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nFirst determine the actual features available in the uploaded dataset.\n\nPotential features include:\n\n- State\n- District\n- Crop\n- Season\n- Area\n- Historical rainfall/climate\n- Fertilizer\n- Pesticide\n- Soil properties where legitimately available\n\nBut DO NOT automatically include a feature just because it exists.\n\nFor every feature determine:\n\n1. Is it available at prediction time?\n2. Is it derived from the target?\n3. Does it contain future information?\n4. Is it appropriate for pre-season or in-season prediction?\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nPRE-SEASON MODEL\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nThe pre-season model must use only information available before planting.\n\nPotential inputs:\n\n- State\n- District\n- Crop\n- Season\n- Farm area\n- Soil pH\n- Soil nitrogen\n- Historical climate statistics\n- Planned fertilizer\n- Planned pesticide if appropriate\n\nIt MUST NOT use:\n\n- Future rainfall\n- Future actual fertilizer\n- Future actual pesticide\n- Harvest data\n- Production\n- Actual yield\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nIN-SEASON MODEL\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nThe in-season prediction can update using information that has actually become available:\n\n- Actual cumulative rainfall\n- Current/live weather\n- Actual fertilizer applied\n- Actual pesticide applied\n- Irrigation\n- Crop growth stage\n- Other verified observations\n\nDo not use future information as if it were already observed.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nTARGET ENCODING\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nIf using target encoding for:\n\n- Crop\n- State\n- District\n\nmake sure:\n\n- It is fitted only on training data.\n- During CV, it is fitted separately within each training fold.\n- Validation/test target values never influence encoding.\n\nNo target leakage.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nMODEL TRAINING\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nTrain a real XGBoost Regressor.\n\nPerform reasonable hyperparameter selection using ONLY the training/validation period.\n\nDo not optimize against the final test set.\n\nSave the trained model artifact and preprocessing/encoding pipeline.\n\nThe application must perform predictions using this actual model artifact.\n\nThere must be NO:\n\n- cropBaseYields\n- pseudoRandom()\n- Math.random()\n- synthetic records\n- hardcoded yield formulas\n- heuristic prediction functions\n- fake model metrics\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nMODEL 1 EVALUATION\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nEvaluate on the untouched 2017\342\200\2232020 test set.\n\nReport:\n\nR\302\262\nMAE\nRMSE\n\nAlso compare against:\n\nBASELINE A:\nGlobal mean yield from training data.\n\nBASELINE B:\nCrop-specific historical mean calculated only from training data.\n\nDisplay:\n\n| Model | R\302\262 | MAE | RMSE |\n|------|----|-----|------|\n| XGBoost | | | |\n| Global Mean | | | |\n| Crop Historical Mean | | | |\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nPER-CROP EVALUATION\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nCalculate test performance for crops with sufficient test observations.\n\nAt minimum include:\n\n- Wheat\n- Rice/Paddy\n- Tomato\n- Potato\n- Mustard\n- Chickpea/Chana\n\nFor each report:\n\nCrop\nTest samples\nR\302\262\nMAE\nRMSE\n\nDo not report misleading R\302\262 for crops with extremely few observations.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nABLATION TEST\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nTo determine whether the model actually learns agricultural/environmental relationships rather than simply recognizing the crop, perform:\n\nMODEL A:\nCrop + State + District\n\nMODEL B:\nCrop + State + District + Area\n\nMODEL C:\nCrop + State + District + Area + legitimate weather/soil/agricultural features\n\nReport:\n\nR\302\262\nMAE\nRMSE\n\nfor each.\n\nThis is important for determining the real value of the additional features.\n\n==================================================\nMODEL 2 \342\200\224 CROP RECOMMENDATION\n==================================================\n\nUse the uploaded crop recommendation dataset ONLY for crop recommendation.\n\nDo NOT use it as yield-training data.\n\nFirst inspect:\n\n- N\n- P\n- K\n- pH\n- Temperature\n- Humidity\n- Rainfall\n- Crop label\n\nTrain an appropriate classification model.\n\nYou may compare:\n\n- XGBoost classifier\n- Random Forest\n- Another appropriate tree-based classifier\n\nSelect the model based on validation performance, not arbitrary preference.\n\nEvaluate using appropriate classification metrics such as:\n\n- Accuracy\n- Macro F1\n- Precision\n- Recall\n- Confusion matrix\n\nDo not fabricate any metrics.\n\n==================================================\nCROP RECOMMENDATION LOGIC\n==================================================\n\nThe ML classifier should NOT be the only factor deciding what the farmer should grow.\n\nThe final recommendation layer should combine:\n\n1. ML crop suitability\n2. Farmer\'s location\n3. Soil information\n4. Climate/weather\n5. Water availability\n6. Season\n7. Farm size\n8. Household nutritional gaps\n9. Expected yield\n10. Market information where appropriate\n\nThe recommendation should therefore answer:\n\n\"What is suitable for this farmer?\"\n\nrather than simply:\n\n\"What crop does the classifier predict?\"\n\n==================================================\nNUTRITION\n==================================================\n\nDo NOT train an ML model for basic nutrition calculations.\n\nUse the verified IFCT/NIN nutrition data.\n\nCalculate:\n\nCrop\n\342\206\222 Estimated/actual production\n\342\206\222 Verified nutrient composition\n\342\206\222 Nutrient produced\n\342\206\222 Household requirement\n\342\206\222 Coverage\n\342\206\222 Gap\n\nDo not use Gemini for nutrition arithmetic.\n\n==================================================\nSOIL\n==================================================\n\nDo not train a fake soil model.\n\nUse:\n\nSoilGrids for supported location-based soil properties.\n\nUse farmer Soil Health Card/lab data for P/K where available.\n\nNever fabricate P/K.\n\n==================================================\nWEATHER\n==================================================\n\nDo not train a fake weather model.\n\nUse Open-Meteo for live/current/forecast weather.\n\nClearly distinguish:\n\nObserved/current weather\nvs\nForecast weather\nvs\nHistorical climate information.\n\n==================================================\nMANDI\n==================================================\n\nDo not train a model for CURRENT mandi price.\n\nUse Government of India / AGMARKNET/Data.gov.in for current prices.\n\nA historical price forecasting model may be implemented separately later if enough validated historical data is available.\n\nDo not display a forecast as the current mandi price.\n\n==================================================\nIRRIGATION\n==================================================\n\nDo not use Gemini to calculate irrigation.\n\nCreate a transparent calculation/rule engine using verified:\n\n- Weather\n- Forecast rainfall\n- ET0\n- Soil moisture where available\n- Soil properties\n- Crop\n- Growth stage\n- Irrigation method\n- Previous irrigation\n- Water availability\n\nClearly identify which values are measured, API-derived, estimated, or farmer-provided.\n\n==================================================\nMODEL VERSIONING\n==================================================\n\nFor every trained ML model save:\n\n- Model name\n- Model type\n- Dataset name\n- Dataset version\n- Training date\n- Training period\n- Validation period\n- Test period\n- Feature list\n- Preprocessing method\n- Hyperparameters\n- Metrics\n- Model artifact/version\n\n==================================================\nAPPLICATION INTEGRATION\n==================================================\n\nConnect the trained models to the farmer workflow.\n\nFarmer enters:\n\nLocation\nCrop\nSeason\nFarm size\nSoil information\nWater availability\nOther required inputs\n\nThen:\n\nCrop recommendation model\n+\nYield model\n+\nVerified soil/weather data\n+\nNutrition engine\n+\nMandi data\n\nproduce the farmer-specific dashboard.\n\n==================================================\nACTUAL YIELD FEEDBACK\n==================================================\n\nWhen the farmer enters actual harvested yield:\n\nStore:\n\n- Predicted yield\n- Actual yield\n- Crop\n- Season\n- Date\n- Farm/plot\n- Relevant model inputs\n\nCalculate:\n\nPrediction error =\nActual yield - Predicted yield\n\nTrack prediction performance over time.\n\nDO NOT automatically retrain the production model from a single farmer record.\n\n==================================================\nFINAL MODEL REPORT\n==================================================\n\nAfter training, give me a complete report containing:\n\n### DATASET\n\nTotal rows:\nTraining:\nValidation:\nTest:\n\n### YIELD MODEL\n\nModel:\nFeatures:\nR\302\262:\nMAE:\nRMSE:\n\n### BASELINES\n\nGlobal Mean:\nR\302\262:\nMAE:\nRMSE:\n\nCrop Mean:\nR\302\262:\nMAE:\nRMSE:\n\n### PER-CROP PERFORMANCE\n\nCrop:\nSamples:\nR\302\262:\nMAE:\nRMSE:\n\n### CROP RECOMMENDATION MODEL\n\nModel:\nTraining samples:\nValidation samples:\nTest samples:\nAccuracy:\nMacro F1:\nPrecision:\nRecall:\n\n### ABLATION\n\nModel A:\nR\302\262:\nMAE:\nRMSE:\n\nModel B:\nR\302\262:\nMAE:\nRMSE:\n\nModel C:\nR\302\262:\nMAE:\nRMSE:\n\n### DATA LEAKAGE AUDIT\n\nConfirm:\n\nProduction excluded: YES/NO\nFuture information excluded: YES/NO\nTarget encoding leakage prevented: YES/NO\nTest set untouched: YES/NO\nSynthetic data used: YES/NO\n\nSynthetic data MUST be NO.\n\n### FINAL VERDICT\n\nGive:\n\nYIELD MODEL:\nREADY / NEEDS CORRECTION\n\nCROP RECOMMENDATION MODEL:\nREADY / NEEDS CORRECTION\n\nDo not hide poor results.\n\nDo not modify metrics to make them look better.\n\nAn honest model with mediocre performance is preferable to an artificially high score.\n\n==================================================\nCRITICAL FINAL RULE\n==================================================\n\nDO NOT just display a model accuracy number.\n\nI need the actual trained model, actual dataset size, actual test methodology, actual metrics, and actual model artifact.\n\nThe previous:\n\nR\302\262 = 0.931\nTraining = 93\nTest = 52\n\nmust NOT be reused.\n\nThat result is invalid until reproduced using the complete uploaded real dataset and the correct temporal split.\n\nProceed with training only after verifying the uploaded datasets."
    items {
      text: "Now that I have provided the actual datasets and the data-source/API requirements, proceed with the REAL ML model training and integration for NutriHarvest AI.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nDo not use synthetic data.\nDo not generate training data.\nDo not fabricate metrics.\nDo not use Gemini as the ML model.\nDo not use hardcoded agricultural formulas as a replacement for ML.\n\nI want a complete, reproducible and auditable ML training pipeline.\n\n==================================================\nMODEL 1 \342\200\224 CROP YIELD PREDICTION\n==================================================\n\nTrain a REAL XGBoost regression model using the uploaded real Agricultural Crop Yield dataset.\n\nTARGET:\n\nYield in kg/hectare.\n\nCRITICAL:\n\nProduction MUST NOT be used as an input feature.\n\nProduction may remain in the raw dataset for reference/historical analysis, but it must be completely excluded from XGBoost features.\n\nReason:\n\nYield = Production / Area\n\nUsing Production would cause target leakage.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nDATA SPLIT\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nUse a strict temporal split:\n\nTRAINING:\n1997\342\200\2232015\n\nVALIDATION:\n2016\n\nFINAL HELD-OUT TEST:\n2017\342\200\2232020\n\nDo NOT randomly mix years.\n\nDo NOT use 2017\342\200\2232020 during training or hyperparameter tuning.\n\nThe final test set must remain untouched until the final evaluation.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nDATASET SIZE\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nBefore training, report:\n\nTotal rows:\nTraining rows:\nValidation rows:\nTest rows:\n\nAlso report:\n\nRows per year:\n1997:\n1998:\n...\n2020:\n\nIf the number of training samples is unexpectedly small, STOP and investigate.\n\nDo NOT silently train on a tiny subset.\n\nDo NOT use arbitrary slice(), limit(), sample(), or demo-data restrictions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nFEATURES\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nFirst determine the actual features available in the uploaded dataset.\n\nPotential features include:\n\n- State\n- District\n- Crop\n- Season\n- Area\n- Historical rainfall/climate\n- Fertilizer\n- Pesticide\n- Soil properties where legitimately available\n\nBut DO NOT automatically include a feature just because it exists.\n\nFor every feature determine:\n\n1. Is it available at prediction time?\n2. Is it derived from the target?\n3. Does it contain future information?\n4. Is it appropriate for pre-season or in-season prediction?\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nPRE-SEASON MODEL\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nThe pre-season model must use only information available before planting.\n\nPotential inputs:\n\n- State\n- District\n- Crop\n- Season\n- Farm area\n- Soil pH\n- Soil nitrogen\n- Historical climate statistics\n- Planned fertilizer\n- Planned pesticide if appropriate\n\nIt MUST NOT use:\n\n- Future rainfall\n- Future actual fertilizer\n- Future actual pesticide\n- Harvest data\n- Production\n- Actual yield\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nIN-SEASON MODEL\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nThe in-season prediction can update using information that has actually become available:\n\n- Actual cumulative rainfall\n- Current/live weather\n- Actual fertilizer applied\n- Actual pesticide applied\n- Irrigation\n- Crop growth stage\n- Other verified observations\n\nDo not use future information as if it were already observed.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nTARGET ENCODING\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nIf using target encoding for:\n\n- Crop\n- State\n- District\n\nmake sure:\n\n- It is fitted only on training data.\n- During CV, it is fitted separately within each training fold.\n- Validation/test target values never influence encoding.\n\nNo target leakage.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nMODEL TRAINING\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nTrain a real XGBoost Regressor.\n\nPerform reasonable hyperparameter selection using ONLY the training/validation period.\n\nDo not optimize against the final test set.\n\nSave the trained model artifact and preprocessing/encoding pipeline.\n\nThe application must perform predictions using this actual model artifact.\n\nThere must be NO:\n\n- cropBaseYields\n- pseudoRandom()\n- Math.random()\n- synthetic records\n- hardcoded yield formulas\n- heuristic prediction functions\n- fake model metrics\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nMODEL 1 EVALUATION\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nEvaluate on the untouched 2017\342\200\2232020 test set.\n\nReport:\n\nR\302\262\nMAE\nRMSE\n\nAlso compare against:\n\nBASELINE A:\nGlobal mean yield from training data.\n\nBASELINE B:\nCrop-specific historical mean calculated only from training data.\n\nDisplay:\n\n| Model | R\302\262 | MAE | RMSE |\n|------|----|-----|------|\n| XGBoost | | | |\n| Global Mean | | | |\n| Crop Historical Mean | | | |\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nPER-CROP EVALUATION\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nCalculate test performance for crops with sufficient test observations.\n\nAt minimum include:\n\n- Wheat\n- Rice/Paddy\n- Tomato\n- Potato\n- Mustard\n- Chickpea/Chana\n\nFor each report:\n\nCrop\nTest samples\nR\302\262\nMAE\nRMSE\n\nDo not report misleading R\302\262 for crops with extremely few observations.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nABLATION TEST\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nTo determine whether the model actually learns agricultural/environmental relationships rather than simply recognizing the crop, perform:\n\nMODEL A:\nCrop + State + District\n\nMODEL B:\nCrop + State + District + Area\n\nMODEL C:\nCrop + State + District + Area + legitimate weather/soil/agricultural features\n\nReport:\n\nR\302\262\nMAE\nRMSE\n\nfor each.\n\nThis is important for determining the real value of the additional features.\n\n==================================================\nMODEL 2 \342\200\224 CROP RECOMMENDATION\n==================================================\n\nUse the uploaded crop recommendation dataset ONLY for crop recommendation.\n\nDo NOT use it as yield-training data.\n\nFirst inspect:\n\n- N\n- P\n- K\n- pH\n- Temperature\n- Humidity\n- Rainfall\n- Crop label\n\nTrain an appropriate classification model.\n\nYou may compare:\n\n- XGBoost classifier\n- Random Forest\n- Another appropriate tree-based classifier\n\nSelect the model based on validation performance, not arbitrary preference.\n\nEvaluate using appropriate classification metrics such as:\n\n- Accuracy\n- Macro F1\n- Precision\n- Recall\n- Confusion matrix\n\nDo not fabricate any metrics.\n\n==================================================\nCROP RECOMMENDATION LOGIC\n==================================================\n\nThe ML classifier should NOT be the only factor deciding what the farmer should grow.\n\nThe final recommendation layer should combine:\n\n1. ML crop suitability\n2. Farmer\'s location\n3. Soil information\n4. Climate/weather\n5. Water availability\n6. Season\n7. Farm size\n8. Household nutritional gaps\n9. Expected yield\n10. Market information where appropriate\n\nThe recommendation should therefore answer:\n\n\"What is suitable for this farmer?\"\n\nrather than simply:\n\n\"What crop does the classifier predict?\"\n\n==================================================\nNUTRITION\n==================================================\n\nDo NOT train an ML model for basic nutrition calculations.\n\nUse the verified IFCT/NIN nutrition data.\n\nCalculate:\n\nCrop\n\342\206\222 Estimated/actual production\n\342\206\222 Verified nutrient composition\n\342\206\222 Nutrient produced\n\342\206\222 Household requirement\n\342\206\222 Coverage\n\342\206\222 Gap\n\nDo not use Gemini for nutrition arithmetic.\n\n==================================================\nSOIL\n==================================================\n\nDo not train a fake soil model.\n\nUse:\n\nSoilGrids for supported location-based soil properties.\n\nUse farmer Soil Health Card/lab data for P/K where available.\n\nNever fabricate P/K.\n\n==================================================\nWEATHER\n==================================================\n\nDo not train a fake weather model.\n\nUse Open-Meteo for live/current/forecast weather.\n\nClearly distinguish:\n\nObserved/current weather\nvs\nForecast weather\nvs\nHistorical climate information.\n\n==================================================\nMANDI\n==================================================\n\nDo not train a model for CURRENT mandi price.\n\nUse Government of India / AGMARKNET/Data.gov.in for current prices.\n\nA historical price forecasting model may be implemented separately later if enough validated historical data is available.\n\nDo not display a forecast as the current mandi price.\n\n==================================================\nIRRIGATION\n==================================================\n\nDo not use Gemini to calculate irrigation.\n\nCreate a transparent calculation/rule engine using verified:\n\n- Weather\n- Forecast rainfall\n- ET0\n- Soil moisture where available\n- Soil properties\n- Crop\n- Growth stage\n- Irrigation method\n- Previous irrigation\n- Water availability\n\nClearly identify which values are measured, API-derived, estimated, or farmer-provided.\n\n==================================================\nMODEL VERSIONING\n==================================================\n\nFor every trained ML model save:\n\n- Model name\n- Model type\n- Dataset name\n- Dataset version\n- Training date\n- Training period\n- Validation period\n- Test period\n- Feature list\n- Preprocessing method\n- Hyperparameters\n- Metrics\n- Model artifact/version\n\n==================================================\nAPPLICATION INTEGRATION\n==================================================\n\nConnect the trained models to the farmer workflow.\n\nFarmer enters:\n\nLocation\nCrop\nSeason\nFarm size\nSoil information\nWater availability\nOther required inputs\n\nThen:\n\nCrop recommendation model\n+\nYield model\n+\nVerified soil/weather data\n+\nNutrition engine\n+\nMandi data\n\nproduce the farmer-specific dashboard.\n\n==================================================\nACTUAL YIELD FEEDBACK\n==================================================\n\nWhen the farmer enters actual harvested yield:\n\nStore:\n\n- Predicted yield\n- Actual yield\n- Crop\n- Season\n- Date\n- Farm/plot\n- Relevant model inputs\n\nCalculate:\n\nPrediction error =\nActual yield - Predicted yield\n\nTrack prediction performance over time.\n\nDO NOT automatically retrain the production model from a single farmer record.\n\n==================================================\nFINAL MODEL REPORT\n==================================================\n\nAfter training, give me a complete report containing:\n\n### DATASET\n\nTotal rows:\nTraining:\nValidation:\nTest:\n\n### YIELD MODEL\n\nModel:\nFeatures:\nR\302\262:\nMAE:\nRMSE:\n\n### BASELINES\n\nGlobal Mean:\nR\302\262:\nMAE:\nRMSE:\n\nCrop Mean:\nR\302\262:\nMAE:\nRMSE:\n\n### PER-CROP PERFORMANCE\n\nCrop:\nSamples:\nR\302\262:\nMAE:\nRMSE:\n\n### CROP RECOMMENDATION MODEL\n\nModel:\nTraining samples:\nValidation samples:\nTest samples:\nAccuracy:\nMacro F1:\nPrecision:\nRecall:\n\n### ABLATION\n\nModel A:\nR\302\262:\nMAE:\nRMSE:\n\nModel B:\nR\302\262:\nMAE:\nRMSE:\n\nModel C:\nR\302\262:\nMAE:\nRMSE:\n\n### DATA LEAKAGE AUDIT\n\nConfirm:\n\nProduction excluded: YES/NO\nFuture information excluded: YES/NO\nTarget encoding leakage prevented: YES/NO\nTest set untouched: YES/NO\nSynthetic data used: YES/NO\n\nSynthetic data MUST be NO.\n\n### FINAL VERDICT\n\nGive:\n\nYIELD MODEL:\nREADY / NEEDS CORRECTION\n\nCROP RECOMMENDATION MODEL:\nREADY / NEEDS CORRECTION\n\nDo not hide poor results.\n\nDo not modify metrics to make them look better.\n\nAn honest model with mediocre performance is preferable to an artificially high score.\n\n==================================================\nCRITICAL FINAL RULE\n==================================================\n\nDO NOT just display a model accuracy number.\n\nI need the actual trained model, actual dataset size, actual test methodology, actual metrics, and actual model artifact.\n\nThe previous:\n\nR\302\262 = 0.931\nTraining = 93\nTest = 52\n\nmust NOT be reused.\n\nThat result is invalid until reproduced using the complete uploaded real dataset and the correct temporal split.\n\nProceed with training only after verifying the uploaded datasets."
    }
