  user_input {
    user_response: "I have independently verified the uploaded crop_yield.csv.\n\nThe file contains:\n\nTotal rows: 19,689\nColumns: 10\nYears: 1997\342\200\2232020\nStates: 30\nCrops: 55\nSeasons: 6\nDuplicate rows: 0\nMissing values: 0\n\nColumns:\n\nCrop\nCrop_Year\nSeason\nState\nArea\nProduction\nAnnual_Rainfall\nFertilizer\nPesticide\nYield\n\nThe verified temporal split is:\n\nTraining (1997\342\200\2232015): 15,404 rows\nValidation (2016): 1,036 rows\nFinal held-out test (2017\342\200\2232020): 3,249 rows\n\nTherefore the previous model result:\n\nR\302\262 = 0.931\nTraining = 93\nTest = 52\n\nis INVALID and must NOT be reused.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nThe uploaded CSV is the actual source of truth.\n\nDo NOT create synthetic data.\nDo NOT generate sample rows.\nDo NOT use random data.\nDo NOT use pseudoRandom().\nDo NOT use hardcoded crop yields.\nDo NOT limit the dataset to a small number of rows.\n\nUse ALL valid records from the uploaded CSV.\n\nOne important observation:\n2020 contains only 37 records in the uploaded CSV.\n\nDo NOT fabricate additional 2020 records.\nDo NOT duplicate 2020 records.\nDo NOT artificially balance the years.\n\nKeep the 2020 data exactly as provided.\n\nNow train the REAL XGBoost yield model.\n\nTARGET:\nYield\n\nEXCLUDE:\nProduction\n\nbecause Production creates target leakage.\n\nUse:\n\n1997\342\200\2232015 \342\206\222 Training\n2016 \342\206\222 Validation\n2017\342\200\2232020 \342\206\222 Final held-out test\n\nBefore training, confirm that the application actually loads all:\n\n19,689 rows\n\nand uses:\n\n15,404 training rows\n1,036 validation rows\n3,249 test rows\n\nIf your application currently loads only 93 training rows and 52 test rows, find and remove the code responsible for that incorrect filtering/limiting.\n\nDo NOT simply change the displayed numbers.\n\nThe actual model training pipeline must use the real rows.\n\nAfter training, calculate on the untouched 3,249-row test set:\n\nR\302\262\nMAE\nRMSE\n\nAlso calculate:\n\n1. Global mean baseline\n2. Crop-specific historical mean baseline\n\nThen provide:\n\nModel R\302\262\nModel MAE\nModel RMSE\n\nBaseline R\302\262\nBaseline MAE\nBaseline RMSE\n\nAlso report per-crop performance where enough test samples exist.\n\nDo not optimize for a high R\302\262.\n\nDo not fabricate or modify the metrics.\n\nFinally, show me:\n\nDataset rows actually loaded:\nTraining rows actually used:\nValidation rows actually used:\nTest rows actually used:\n\nI expect:\n\n19,689\n15,404\n1,036\n3,249\n\nIf the actual numbers differ, STOP and explain why before reporting model metrics."
    items {
      text: "I have independently verified the uploaded crop_yield.csv.\n\nThe file contains:\n\nTotal rows: 19,689\nColumns: 10\nYears: 1997\342\200\2232020\nStates: 30\nCrops: 55\nSeasons: 6\nDuplicate rows: 0\nMissing values: 0\n\nColumns:\n\nCrop\nCrop_Year\nSeason\nState\nArea\nProduction\nAnnual_Rainfall\nFertilizer\nPesticide\nYield\n\nThe verified temporal split is:\n\nTraining (1997\342\200\2232015): 15,404 rows\nValidation (2016): 1,036 rows\nFinal held-out test (2017\342\200\2232020): 3,249 rows\n\nTherefore the previous model result:\n\nR\302\262 = 0.931\nTraining = 93\nTest = 52\n\nis INVALID and must NOT be reused.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nThe uploaded CSV is the actual source of truth.\n\nDo NOT create synthetic data.\nDo NOT generate sample rows.\nDo NOT use random data.\nDo NOT use pseudoRandom().\nDo NOT use hardcoded crop yields.\nDo NOT limit the dataset to a small number of rows.\n\nUse ALL valid records from the uploaded CSV.\n\nOne important observation:\n2020 contains only 37 records in the uploaded CSV.\n\nDo NOT fabricate additional 2020 records.\nDo NOT duplicate 2020 records.\nDo NOT artificially balance the years.\n\nKeep the 2020 data exactly as provided.\n\nNow train the REAL XGBoost yield model.\n\nTARGET:\nYield\n\nEXCLUDE:\nProduction\n\nbecause Production creates target leakage.\n\nUse:\n\n1997\342\200\2232015 \342\206\222 Training\n2016 \342\206\222 Validation\n2017\342\200\2232020 \342\206\222 Final held-out test\n\nBefore training, confirm that the application actually loads all:\n\n19,689 rows\n\nand uses:\n\n15,404 training rows\n1,036 validation rows\n3,249 test rows\n\nIf your application currently loads only 93 training rows and 52 test rows, find and remove the code responsible for that incorrect filtering/limiting.\n\nDo NOT simply change the displayed numbers.\n\nThe actual model training pipeline must use the real rows.\n\nAfter training, calculate on the untouched 3,249-row test set:\n\nR\302\262\nMAE\nRMSE\n\nAlso calculate:\n\n1. Global mean baseline\n2. Crop-specific historical mean baseline\n\nThen provide:\n\nModel R\302\262\nModel MAE\nModel RMSE\n\nBaseline R\302\262\nBaseline MAE\nBaseline RMSE\n\nAlso report per-crop performance where enough test samples exist.\n\nDo not optimize for a high R\302\262.\n\nDo not fabricate or modify the metrics.\n\nFinally, show me:\n\nDataset rows actually loaded:\nTraining rows actually used:\nValidation rows actually used:\nTest rows actually used:\n\nI expect:\n\n19,689\n15,404\n1,036\n3,249\n\nIf the actual numbers differ, STOP and explain why before reporting model metrics."
    }
  }
}
